window.LUMEN_LOAD({"id": "linear-algebra", "lessons": {"vectors": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-start-with-vectors\" data-h=\"3\">Why start with vectors</h3>\n<p>Almost everything in machine learning is stored as a list of numbers. A photo is a list of pixel brightnesses. A song is a list of air-pressure samples. A customer is a list such as age, income and number of purchases. A word inside a language model is a list of a few thousand numbers. Linear algebra is the mathematics of these lists: how to add them, stretch them, compare them and transform them. The basic object, the list itself, is called a <strong>vector</strong>.</p>\n<p>The word may remind you of physics class, where a vector was \"an arrow with a size and a direction\". That picture is still correct and still useful. The trick of linear algebra is that the arrow picture and the list picture are the same thing seen from two sides. When you can switch between them freely, a lot of hard-looking formulas turn into simple pictures, and a lot of pictures turn into easy arithmetic.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you are standing in the middle of a town laid out on a grid. A friend texts you directions to a cafe: \"walk 3 blocks east, then 1 block north\". That instruction is a vector. You can draw it as an arrow from where you stand to the cafe, or you can write it as the pair of numbers <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span>. Both describe the same movement.</p>\n<p>Notice two things. First, the instruction does not care where you start. If you were standing somewhere else, \"3 east, 1 north\" would still be a perfectly good instruction; it would just take you somewhere else. An arrow can slide around the page without changing, as long as its length and direction stay the same. Second, the order of the numbers matters. <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span> means 3 east and 1 north, while <span class=\"m\">(1, 3)</span> means 1 east and 3 north. A vector is an <strong>ordered</strong> list.</p>\n<h3 id=\"three-views-of-one-idea\" data-h=\"3\">Three views of one idea</h3>\n<p>There are three common ways to think about a vector, and you will use all three.</p>\n<p>The <strong>physics view</strong> says a vector is an arrow with a length (also called magnitude) and a direction. Two arrows are equal if they have the same length and point the same way, even if they are drawn in different places.</p>\n<p>The <strong>computer science view</strong> says a vector is an ordered list of numbers, like <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 1)</span> or <span class=\"m\">v = (0.2, -1.7, 4.0)</span>. This is how vectors live inside a computer: as an array.</p>\n<p>The <strong>mathematician's view</strong> says a vector is anything you can add together and multiply by numbers, as long as a few sensible rules hold. Arrows and lists both qualify, and so do some surprising things, such as polynomials and functions. You will meet this view properly in the lesson on vector spaces.</p>\n<p>These views agree because of coordinates. If you put the tail of an arrow at the origin of a coordinate grid, its tip lands at some point. The coordinates of that point are the list of numbers. In linear algebra we usually write a vector as a <strong>column</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">v = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2</span>\nThe symbol <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span> (read \"R two\") means \"all lists of 2 real numbers\", which is the flat plane. Similarly <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^3</span> is ordinary 3-D space, and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> is the set of all lists of <span class=\"m\">n</span> real numbers. You cannot picture <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^{784}</span>, but you can compute in it exactly as you do in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Vector</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An ordered list of <span class=\"m\">n</span> real numbers <span class=\"m\">v = (v_1, v_2, \\dots, v_n)</span>, also pictured as an arrow from the origin to the point with those coordinates. The set of all such lists is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Component</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>One entry <span class=\"m\">v_i</span> of a vector. In <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span> the first component is the horizontal step and the second is the vertical step.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"components-walking-along-the-axes\" data-h=\"3\">Components: walking along the axes</h3>\n<p>The numbers inside a vector are its <strong>components</strong>. For <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 1)</span> the first component is 3 and the second is 1. You can read them as instructions: walk 3 units along the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis, then 1 unit along the <span class=\"m\">y</span>-axis. In 3-D, <span class=\"m\">w = (2, -1, 4)</span> means 2 along <span class=\"m\">x</span>, 1 backwards along <span class=\"m\">y</span>, and 4 up along <span class=\"m\">z</span>. A negative component simply means \"walk the other way\".</p>\n<p>A special vector is the <strong>zero vector</strong> <span class=\"m\">0 = (0, 0, \\dots, 0)</span>. It is the instruction \"don't move\". As an arrow it has no length and no direction. It plays the same role for vectors that the number 0 plays for numbers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"length-pythagoras-in-disguise\" data-h=\"3\">Length: Pythagoras in disguise</h3>\n<p>How long is the arrow <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>? Walk 3 across and 4 up, and you have built a right triangle with legs 3 and 4. The arrow is the hypotenuse, so by Pythagoras its length is <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = \\sqrt{25} = 5</span>. We write the length of <span class=\"m\">v</span> as <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\|</span> and call it the <strong>norm</strong> (or magnitude) of <span class=\"m\">v</span>.</p>\n<p>In 3-D the same idea works twice. For <span class=\"m\">w = (1, 2, 2)</span>, the floor diagonal has length <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{1^2 + 2^2} = \\sqrt5</span>, and going up 2 more gives <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{5 + 2^2} = \\sqrt9 = 3</span>. In general:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|v\\| = \\sqrt{v_1^2 + v_2^2 + \\dots + v_n^2}</span>\nThis is the straight-line, \"as the crow flies\" length. Other ways of measuring size exist, and you'll meet them in the lesson on norms.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Norm (length)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\|v\\| = \\sqrt{v_1^2 + \\dots + v_n^2}</span>, the straight-line length of the arrow <span class=\"m\">v</span>. It is never negative, and it is zero only for the zero vector.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"unit-vectors-pure-direction\" data-h=\"3\">Unit vectors: pure direction</h3>\n<p>Sometimes you only care about direction, not size. A <strong>unit vector</strong> is a vector of length exactly 1. To turn any nonzero vector into a unit vector pointing the same way, divide it by its own length:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat v = \\frac{v}{\\|v\\|}</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 4)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\| = 5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\hat v = (0.6, 0.8)</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{0.36 + 0.64} = 1</span>. This step is called <strong>normalizing</strong>. The hat symbol <span class=\"m\">\\hat{}</span> is the usual way to mark a unit vector.</p>\n<p>Two unit vectors are so common that they get their own names: <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath = (1, 0)</span> points one step along <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath = (0, 1)</span> points one step along <span class=\"m\">y</span>. Every 2-D vector is built from them: <span class=\"m\">(3, 1) = 3\\hat\\imath + 1\\hat\\jmath</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Unit vector</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A vector with length 1. Any nonzero <span class=\"m\">v</span> can be normalized to <span class=\"m\">\\hat v = v / \\|v\\|</span>, which keeps the direction and throws away the size.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>The reason the arrow view and the list view never disagree is that both are describing a displacement, a \"how far in each direction\". The list stores the displacement along each axis separately. The arrow shows the total displacement at once. Pythagoras connects them: the total length comes from the separate pieces. Because the axes are perpendicular, the pieces do not interfere with each other, and this is what makes the formula so simple.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Adding the components to get the length. <span class=\"m\">\\|(3, 4)\\|</span> is <span class=\"m\">5</span>, not <span class=\"m\">7</span>. You square, add, then take the square root.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that squaring kills the sign: <span class=\"m\">\\|(-3, 4)\\| = 5</span> as well.</li>\n<li>Trying to normalize the zero vector. It has length 0, and you can't divide by 0. It has no direction to keep.</li>\n<li>Mixing up a point and a vector. A point is a location; a vector is a movement. In coordinates they look the same, which is convenient but can confuse you when we start moving vectors around.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A vector is an ordered list of numbers that you can also picture as an arrow. Its entries are components, its length is the square root of the sum of squared components, and dividing by the length gives a unit vector that keeps only the direction. In ML, every input, every embedding and every set of model parameters is a vector, so this one idea is the foundation for everything that follows.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"vector\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;comps&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the tip of u. The orange and green segments are its components.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Drag the tip to <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span> and read the length in the panel. It should say 5.\n2. Drag it to <span class=\"m\">(-3, 4)</span> and then <span class=\"m\">(-3, -4)</span>. Watch the angle change but the length stay 5.\n3. Drag it close to the origin, then far away, keeping the same direction. Compare the unit vector shown in the panel each time.\n4. Put the tip exactly on an axis, such as <span class=\"m\">(0, 2)</span>. One component disappears.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the components are the \"shadows\" of the arrow on each axis, exactly the right-triangle legs from the article. The unit vector stays the same when you only change the length, because normalizing throws away size and keeps direction.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">v = (v_1, \\dots, v_n) \\in \\mathbb{R}^n</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|v\\| = \\sqrt{v_1^2 + v_2^2 + \\dots + v_n^2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat v = \\frac{v}{\\|v\\|}\\quad (v \\ne 0)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Length is zero only for the zero vector</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\|v\\| \\ge 0</span> for every <span class=\"m\">v</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\| = 0</span> exactly when <span class=\"m\">v = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\|v\\|^2 = v_1^2 + \\dots + v_n^2</span> is a sum of squares, so it is at least 0. A sum of non-negative numbers is 0 only if every term is 0, so each <span class=\"m\">v_i = 0</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the length and unit vector of <span class=\"m\">v = (2, -1, 2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Square the components: <span class=\"m\">4, 1, 4</span>.</li>\n<li>Add them: <span class=\"m\">4 + 1 + 4 = 9</span>.</li>\n<li>Take the square root: <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\| = 3</span>.</li>\n<li>Divide each component by 3: <span class=\"m\">\\hat v = \\left(\\tfrac23, -\\tfrac13, \\tfrac23\\right)</span>.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac49 + \\tfrac19 + \\tfrac49 = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\|\\hat v\\| = 1</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A drone flies 6 m east, 8 m north and rises 24 m. How far is it from the start?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Write the displacement as a vector: <span class=\"m\">d = (6, 8, 24)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\|d\\|^2 = 36 + 64 + 576 = 676</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\|d\\| = \\sqrt{676} = 26</span> m.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Find all values of <span class=\"m\">k</span> for which the vector <span class=\"m\">(k, 2k, 2)</span> has length 3.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">\\|(k, 2k, 2)\\|^2 = k^2 + 4k^2 + 4 = 5k^2 + 4</span>. Setting this equal to <span class=\"m\">3^2 = 9</span> gives <span class=\"m\">5k^2 = 5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">k^2 = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">k = 1</span> or <span class=\"m\">k = -1</span>. Check <span class=\"m\">k = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{1 + 4 + 4} = 3</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Everything is a vector</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>A 28×28 grayscale image is a vector in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^{784}</span>, one component per pixel.</li>\n<li>A word embedding is a vector in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^{300}</span> (or several thousand dimensions in a large language model).</li>\n<li>A row of a spreadsheet (age, income, clicks) is a feature vector.</li>\n<li>All the weights of a neural network form one huge vector <span class=\"m\">\\theta \\in \\mathbb{R}^{p}</span>, and training moves this vector around.</li>\n<li>Normalizing embeddings to unit length is standard before comparing them, so that only direction (meaning) matters, not size.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A GPS fix, a wind reading (\"15 km/h from the north-west\") and the colour of a pixel <span class=\"m\">(R, G, B) = (255, 128, 0)</span> are all vectors. A pilot adds the plane's velocity vector and the wind vector to get the actual ground velocity.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In code a vector is just an array: <code>v = [3, 1]</code>. Its length is <code>math.sqrt(sum(x*x for x in v))</code>, or <code>np.linalg.norm(v)</code> in NumPy. Graphics engines store positions, velocities and surface normals as 3-D vectors and normalize the normals constantly for lighting calculations.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">\\left\\| (3, 4) \\right\\|</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the unit vector in the direction of <span class=\"m\">(0, -2)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(0, 2)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">(0, -1)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(0, -\\tfrac12)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Divide by the length 2: <span class=\"m\">(0, -2)/2 = (0, -1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the length of <span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 2)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{1 + 4 + 4} = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which statement is true about <span class=\"m\">(2, 5)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(5, 2)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">They are the same vector because they have the same numbers.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">They are different vectors with the same length.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">They have different lengths.</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Order matters, so they are different vectors, but both have length <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{29}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A vector in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^{784}</span> has how many components?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"784\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> is the set of lists of <span class=\"m\">n</span> numbers.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The first component of the unit vector in the direction of <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.6|3/5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\hat v = (3, 4)/5 = (0.6, 0.8)</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "vector-ops": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-need-operations-on-vectors\" data-h=\"3\">Why we need operations on vectors</h3>\n<p>A list of numbers on its own is just storage. Vectors become powerful when we can combine them. Two operations do almost all the work in linear algebra: <strong>adding</strong> two vectors and <strong>scaling</strong> a vector by a number. Every other idea in this course, from spans to matrices to neural network layers, is built out of these two moves. They are simple, but it is worth understanding them in both the list view and the arrow view, because the arrow view is where the intuition comes from.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you're rowing a boat across a river. You row straight across at 3 km/h, so your rowing velocity is <span class=\"m\">(3, 0)</span>. Meanwhile the river current pushes you downstream at 1 km/h, which is <span class=\"m\">(0, -1)</span>. Where do you actually go? Each hour you move 3 across <em>and</em> 1 downstream, so your real velocity is <span class=\"m\">(3, 0) + (0, -1) = (3, -1)</span>. That is vector addition: two effects acting together combine into one.</p>\n<p>Now suppose you row twice as hard. Your rowing velocity becomes <span class=\"m\">2 \\cdot (3, 0) = (6, 0)</span>. Same direction, double the length. That is scaling.</p>\n<h3 id=\"adding-vectors\" data-h=\"3\">Adding vectors</h3>\n<p>To add two vectors, add matching components:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3\\\\2 + (-1)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nBoth vectors must have the same number of components. Adding a 2-D vector to a 3-D vector makes no sense.</p>\n<p>In the arrow view, addition is <strong>tip-to-tail</strong>. Draw the first arrow. Then slide the second arrow so its tail sits at the tip of the first. The sum is the arrow from the very start to the very end. This is exactly \"walk <span class=\"m\">u</span>, then walk <span class=\"m\">v</span>\". Since walking <span class=\"m\">v</span> first and then <span class=\"m\">u</span> takes you to the same place, <span class=\"m\">u + v = v + u</span>. The two possible paths form the sides of a parallelogram, and the sum is its diagonal. This is why vector addition is sometimes called the <strong>parallelogram rule</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"scaling-vectors\" data-h=\"3\">Scaling vectors</h3>\n<p>A plain number is called a <strong>scalar</strong> in linear algebra, because it scales things. To multiply a vector by a scalar <span class=\"m\">c</span>, multiply every component by <span class=\"m\">c</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">3\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3\\\\6\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nIn the arrow view, <span class=\"m\">cv</span> points along the same line as <span class=\"m\">v</span> and is <span class=\"m\">|c|</span> times as long. If <span class=\"m\">c</span> is between 0 and 1 it shrinks the arrow, if <span class=\"m\">c &gt; 1</span> it stretches it, if <span class=\"m\">c = 0</span> it collapses to the zero vector, and if <span class=\"m\">c</span> is negative it flips the arrow to point the opposite way. In particular <span class=\"m\">-v = (-1)v</span> has the same length as <span class=\"m\">v</span> but the opposite direction.</p>\n<p>The length scales in a clean way: <span class=\"m\">\\|cv\\| = |c|\\,\\|v\\|</span>. For example <span class=\"m\">\\|(3, 6)\\| = 3\\|(1, 2)\\| = 3\\sqrt5</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Scalar</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An ordinary real number, used to multiply (scale) a vector. Multiplying <span class=\"m\">v</span> by <span class=\"m\">c</span> multiplies each component by <span class=\"m\">c</span> and its length by <span class=\"m\">|c|</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"subtracting-vectors-and-measuring-distance\" data-h=\"3\">Subtracting vectors and measuring distance</h3>\n<p>Subtraction is adding the negative: <span class=\"m\">u - v = u + (-v)</span>, done component by component. For <span class=\"m\">u = (4, 5)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (1, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">u - v = (3, 4)</span>.</p>\n<p>The arrow picture is the useful part. <span class=\"m\">u - v</span> is the arrow that goes <strong>from the tip of <span class=\"m\">v</span> to the tip of <span class=\"m\">u</span></strong>. Check it: start at <span class=\"m\">v</span>, add <span class=\"m\">u - v</span>, and you land on <span class=\"m\">v + (u - v) = u</span>. So if <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> are two points, <span class=\"m\">u - v</span> is the step you need to get from <span class=\"m\">v</span> to <span class=\"m\">u</span>, and the <strong>distance</strong> between them is the length of that step:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{dist}(u, v) = \\|u - v\\|</span>\nFor the points <span class=\"m\">(4, 5)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>, the distance is <span class=\"m\">\\|(3, 4)\\| = 5</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"combining-both-moves\" data-h=\"3\">Combining both moves</h3>\n<p>You can scale and add in one expression. For <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (3, -1)</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">2u - v = (2, 4) - (3, -1) = (-1, 5)</span>\nThe order of operations is the same as with numbers: do scaling before adding unless brackets say otherwise. Expressions like <span class=\"m\">au + bv</span> are so important that they get a name, <strong>linear combinations</strong>, and they are the subject of the next lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-rules-these-operations-follow\" data-h=\"3\">The rules these operations follow</h3>\n<p>Vector addition and scaling obey the same algebra rules you already know from numbers. Addition is <strong>commutative</strong> (<span class=\"m\">u + v = v + u</span>) and <strong>associative</strong> (<span class=\"m\">(u + v) + w = u + (v + w)</span>). Adding the zero vector changes nothing. Every vector has a negative that cancels it. Scaling <strong>distributes</strong>: <span class=\"m\">c(u + v) = cu + cv</span> and <span class=\"m\">(a + b)v = av + bv</span>. And <span class=\"m\">1v = v</span>. Each rule is true because it is true for every component separately, and components are just numbers. These eight rules are exactly what mathematicians use to define a \"vector space\".</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key idea</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Addition and scaling are done one component at a time. Geometrically, addition is walking one arrow after another, and scaling stretches, shrinks or flips an arrow along its own line.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>Each component tracks movement along one axis, and movements along different axes don't interfere. If you walk 1 east then 3 east, you walked 4 east, no matter how much you walked north along the way. That is why we can simply add the east parts and the north parts separately. Scaling works the same way: doing the same walk three times triples each part.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Thinking <span class=\"m\">\\|u + v\\| = \\|u\\| + \\|v\\|</span>. Lengths only add when the vectors point the same way. For <span class=\"m\">(3, 0) + (0, 4) = (3, 4)</span> the lengths are <span class=\"m\">3 + 4 = 7</span> but the sum has length 5.</li>\n<li>Getting subtraction backwards. <span class=\"m\">u - v</span> points <strong>to</strong> <span class=\"m\">u</span>, <strong>from</strong> <span class=\"m\">v</span>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that a negative scalar reverses direction; <span class=\"m\">-2v</span> is not \"a shorter <span class=\"m\">v</span>\", it's twice as long and backwards.</li>\n<li>Adding vectors of different sizes, like <span class=\"m\">(1, 2) + (1, 2, 3)</span>. This is undefined.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Add vectors by adding components; picture it as tip-to-tail walking. Scale a vector by multiplying every component; picture it as stretching along its own line, flipping if the scalar is negative. Subtraction <span class=\"m\">u - v</span> is the arrow from <span class=\"m\">v</span> to <span class=\"m\">u</span>, and its length is the distance between them. These two operations, and the familiar rules they obey, are the entire toolkit linear algebra is built from.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"vector\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;add&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Tip-to-tail: the dashed copies show walking u then v, or v then u. Both reach u + v.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"vector\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;add&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Switch between u + v, u − v and c·u using the buttons. Drag the arrow tips.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. In <strong>u + v</strong> mode, drag <span class=\"m\">v</span> around and watch the dashed parallelogram. The purple sum is always its diagonal.\n2. Make <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> point the same way. The length of the sum equals the sum of the lengths. Now make them perpendicular and compare.\n3. Switch to <strong>u − v</strong>. Put <span class=\"m\">u = (4, 2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (1, -2)</span> and read off the distance between the tips. It should be 5.\n4. Switch to <strong>c · u</strong> and drag the slider from 3 down to −3, stopping at 0.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the sum walks one arrow after the other, the difference connects the two tips, and scaling never leaves the dashed line through <span class=\"m\">u</span> (its span). These are the three pictures from the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">u + v = (u_1 + v_1, \\dots, u_n + v_n)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">cv = (cv_1, \\dots, cv_n), \\qquad \\|cv\\| = |c|\\,\\|v\\|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{dist}(u, v) = \\|u - v\\|</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Triangle inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any vectors <span class=\"m\">u, v</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\|u + v\\| \\le \\|u\\| + \\|v\\|</span>, with equality exactly when one is a non-negative multiple of the other.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Sketch</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Tip-to-tail, <span class=\"m\">u</span>, <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">u + v</span> form a triangle. One side of a triangle can't be longer than the other two put together, because the straight path is the shortest. The two sides line up into one straight path only when <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> point the same way. (An algebraic proof uses the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality from the dot product lesson.)</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute <span class=\"m\">3(1, -2) - 2(2, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Scale first: <span class=\"m\">3(1, -2) = (3, -6)</span> and <span class=\"m\">2(2, 1) = (4, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Subtract component by component: <span class=\"m\">(3 - 4, -6 - 2) = (-1, -8)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the distance between <span class=\"m\">(1, 1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(3, -1, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Subtract: <span class=\"m\">(3, -1, 1) - (1, 1, 0) = (2, -2, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Length: <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{4 + 4 + 1} = \\sqrt9 = 3</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the midpoint of <span class=\"m\">p = (2, 6)</span> and <span class=\"m\">q = (8, 2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>The step from <span class=\"m\">p</span> to <span class=\"m\">q</span> is <span class=\"m\">q - p = (6, -4)</span>.</li>\n<li>Go half of it: <span class=\"m\">p + \\tfrac12(q - p) = (2, 6) + (3, -2) = (5, 4)</span>.</li>\n<li>Equivalently, <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac12(p + q) = \\tfrac12(10, 8) = (5, 4)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Find scalars <span class=\"m\">a</span> and <span class=\"m\">b</span> such that <span class=\"m\">a(1, 2) + b(3, 1) = (7, 9)</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Componentwise: <span class=\"m\">a + 3b = 7</span> and <span class=\"m\">2a + b = 9</span>. From the first, <span class=\"m\">a = 7 - 3b</span>. Substitute: <span class=\"m\">2(7 - 3b) + b = 9</span>, so <span class=\"m\">14 - 5b = 9</span> and <span class=\"m\">b = 1</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">a = 4</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">4(1, 2) + 1(3, 1) = (4 + 3, 8 + 1) = (7, 9)</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Word embeddings support arithmetic: <span class=\"m\">\\text{king} - \\text{man} + \\text{woman}</span> lands close to <span class=\"m\">\\text{queen}</span>, because directions in embedding space carry meaning. Every gradient descent step is a scaled vector addition, <span class=\"m\">\\theta_{\\text{new}} = \\theta - \\eta\\,\\nabla L</span>: the learning rate <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> scales the gradient, and we subtract it from the parameters. Averaging vectors (adding and scaling by <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac1n</span>) gives the mean of a dataset or the centroid in k-means.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Forces add as vectors: if two people pull a box with forces <span class=\"m\">(30, 40)</span> N and <span class=\"m\">(50, -40)</span> N, the box feels <span class=\"m\">(80, 0)</span> N, straight ahead. Pilots and sailors add their own velocity to the wind or current velocity to find where they really go.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Game engines update positions every frame with <code>position = position + velocity * dt</code>, which is scaling and addition. In NumPy <code>u + v</code> and <code>3 * v</code> work component by component on whole arrays at once.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Compute <span class=\"m\">3(1, -2) - (2, 2)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">(1, -8)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(1, -4)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(5, -4)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(3, -6) - (2, 2) = (1, -8)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the distance between <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(4, 5)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The difference is <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>, whose length is 5.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\| = 4</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">\\|-3v\\|</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"12\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\|cv\\| = |c|\\,\\|v\\| = 3 \\cdot 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The vector <span class=\"m\">u - v</span> points...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">from the tip of <span class=\"m\">u</span> to the tip of <span class=\"m\">v</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">from the tip of <span class=\"m\">v</span> to the tip of <span class=\"m\">u</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">from the origin to the midpoint</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">v + (u - v) = u</span>, so it starts at <span class=\"m\">v</span> and ends at <span class=\"m\">u</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\|(3, 0) + (0, 4)\\|</span> equals...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The sum is <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>. Lengths don't simply add unless the vectors point the same way.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the first component of the midpoint of <span class=\"m\">(2, 6)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(8, 2)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\tfrac12\\big((2, 6) + (8, 2)\\big) = (5, 4)</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "span": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-question-this-lesson-answers\" data-h=\"3\">The question this lesson answers</h3>\n<p>Suppose someone hands you a few vectors and lets you scale them and add them together as much as you like. Which points can you reach? Can you reach everywhere, or only some places? And are all the vectors you were given actually needed, or are some of them just repeating what the others already do? These three questions lead to three of the most important words in linear algebra: <strong>span</strong>, <strong>linear independence</strong> and <strong>basis</strong>. They come up every time we ask whether a system of equations has a solution, whether a dataset has redundant features, or how many numbers we really need to describe something.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a paint shop that only stocks two base paints, red and blue, and you can mix any amount of each. Every colour you can make is \"some amount of red plus some amount of blue\". You can make purples of every shade, but no amount of mixing will ever give you yellow. The set of all colours you can mix is the span of red and blue. Now suppose the shop also stocks purple. That doesn't let you make anything new, because purple was already a mix of red and blue. Purple is <strong>redundant</strong>: the three paints are not independent. To reach yellow you'd need a genuinely new direction, a paint that is not a mix of the ones you have.</p>\n<h3 id=\"linear-combinations\" data-h=\"3\">Linear combinations</h3>\n<p>A <strong>linear combination</strong> of vectors <span class=\"m\">v_1, \\dots, v_k</span> is any expression\n<span class=\"mb\">c_1 v_1 + c_2 v_2 + \\dots + c_k v_k</span>\nwhere the <span class=\"m\">c_i</span> are scalars, called the <strong>weights</strong> or coefficients. You scale each vector by its weight and add the results. For example with <span class=\"m\">v = (2, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">w = (-1, 1)</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">1v + 1w = (1, 2), \\qquad 2v - w = (5, 1), \\qquad 0v + 0w = (0, 0)</span>\nNotice that the zero vector is always a linear combination: just pick every weight to be 0.</p>\n<h3 id=\"span\" data-h=\"3\">Span</h3>\n<p>The <strong>span</strong> of a set of vectors is the set of every linear combination of them, everything you can reach. Let's build intuition with small cases in the plane.</p>\n<p>The span of a single nonzero vector <span class=\"m\">v</span> is all the multiples <span class=\"m\">cv</span>. Those all lie on one line through the origin, the line through <span class=\"m\">v</span>. So the span of one vector is a line.</p>\n<p>The span of two vectors <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">w</span> that point in different directions is the whole plane. Here is why. You can slide along the <span class=\"m\">v</span>-line any distance and then along the <span class=\"m\">w</span>-direction any distance. With two different directions available you can reach any point, the same way \"east-west\" and \"north-south\" streets reach every corner of a town, even if the streets are slanted.</p>\n<p>But if <span class=\"m\">w</span> is a multiple of <span class=\"m\">v</span>, for example <span class=\"m\">v = (1, 2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">w = (-2, -4) = -2v</span>, then any combination <span class=\"m\">av + bw = (a - 2b)v</span> is still just a multiple of <span class=\"m\">v</span>. The span collapses to a line. The second vector added nothing new.</p>\n<p>In 3-D the pattern continues: one nonzero vector spans a line, two vectors in different directions span a plane through the origin, and three vectors that don't all lie in a common plane span all of space.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Linear combination</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A sum of scalar multiples <span class=\"m\">c_1v_1 + \\dots + c_kv_k</span> of the vectors <span class=\"m\">v_1, \\dots, v_k</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Span</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The set of all linear combinations of a collection of vectors. It is always a line, plane or higher-dimensional flat space passing through the origin.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"linear-independence-is-anyone-redundant\" data-h=\"3\">Linear independence: is anyone redundant?</h3>\n<p>A set of vectors is <strong>linearly dependent</strong> if at least one of them is a linear combination of the others, so it adds nothing to the span. If none of them is redundant, the set is <strong>linearly independent</strong>.</p>\n<p>The official test is neat. The vectors <span class=\"m\">v_1, \\dots, v_k</span> are independent exactly when the <strong>only</strong> way to make zero,\n<span class=\"mb\">c_1 v_1 + \\dots + c_k v_k = 0,</span>\nis the boring way with every <span class=\"m\">c_i = 0</span>. Why does this capture redundancy? If some combination with a nonzero weight gives zero, say <span class=\"m\">2v_1 + v_2 - v_3 = 0</span>, then you can solve for the vector with nonzero weight: <span class=\"m\">v_3 = 2v_1 + v_2</span>. That vector was redundant.</p>\n<p>Some quick checks: any set containing the zero vector is dependent (use weight 1 on the zero vector). Two vectors are dependent exactly when one is a multiple of the other. And in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span>, any three vectors are dependent, because two independent ones already span the plane, so the third must be a combination of them.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Linear independence</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Vectors <span class=\"m\">v_1, \\dots, v_k</span> are linearly independent if <span class=\"m\">c_1v_1 + \\dots + c_kv_k = 0</span> forces <span class=\"m\">c_1 = \\dots = c_k = 0</span>. Otherwise at least one of them is a combination of the others.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"basis-and-dimension\" data-h=\"3\">Basis and dimension</h3>\n<p>A <strong>basis</strong> of a space is a set of vectors that is both independent and spans the space. It is a \"just right\" set: enough vectors to reach everything, and no extras. The most familiar basis of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span> is the <strong>standard basis</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath = (1, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath = (0, 1)</span>, and <span class=\"m\">(3, 1) = 3\\hat\\imath + 1\\hat\\jmath</span>. But <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(-1, 1)</span> are also a basis of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span>. There are infinitely many bases.</p>\n<p>A basis has a wonderful property: every vector in the space can be written as a combination of basis vectors in <strong>exactly one</strong> way. The weights are then called the <strong>coordinates</strong> of the vector in that basis. And although there are many bases, they all have the same number of vectors. That number is the <strong>dimension</strong> of the space: 2 for the plane, 3 for ordinary space, <span class=\"m\">n</span> for <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Basis</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A linearly independent set of vectors that spans the space. Every vector in the space is a combination of basis vectors in exactly one way.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Dimension</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The number of vectors in any basis of a space. <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> has dimension <span class=\"m\">n</span>; a plane through the origin has dimension 2.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>Uniqueness of coordinates comes straight from independence. Suppose a vector had two different recipes, <span class=\"m\">x = a_1b_1 + a_2b_2 = c_1b_1 + c_2b_2</span>. Subtracting gives <span class=\"m\">(a_1 - c_1)b_1 + (a_2 - c_2)b_2 = 0</span>. Independence says both weights must be zero, so <span class=\"m\">a_1 = c_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">a_2 = c_2</span>. The two recipes were the same after all.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Thinking \"more vectors means bigger span\". Adding a redundant vector doesn't change the span at all.</li>\n<li>Thinking two vectors are independent just because they look different. <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(-3, -6)</span> look different but are parallel.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that a span always contains the origin. A line that doesn't pass through 0 is not the span of anything.</li>\n<li>Checking pairs only. Three vectors in 3-D can be pairwise non-parallel yet still lie in one plane, like <span class=\"m\">(1, 0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0, 1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, 1, 0)</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A linear combination scales vectors and adds them. The span is everything you can reach that way. Vectors are independent when none is a combination of the others, which is the same as saying the only combination giving zero uses all-zero weights. A basis is an independent spanning set, it gives every vector unique coordinates, and its size is the dimension of the space.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"span\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Move the sliders a and b to reach different points. Then drag w onto the line through v.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">a = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = -1</span> and read the combination. Now try to reach the point <span class=\"m\">(0, 3)</span> by moving only the sliders.\n2. Drag <span class=\"m\">w</span> around and notice the slanted grid: every grid crossing is a combination with whole-number weights.\n3. Drag <span class=\"m\">w</span> until it lies along the line through <span class=\"m\">v</span> (for example <span class=\"m\">w = (-2, -1)</span>). The panel says \"dependent\" and the grid collapses to a single orange line.\n4. With <span class=\"m\">w</span> parallel to <span class=\"m\">v</span>, try to reach a point off the line using the sliders. You can't.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> with two independent vectors the slanted grid covers the whole plane, so the span is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">(v, w)</span> is a basis. When <span class=\"m\">w</span> becomes a multiple of <span class=\"m\">v</span> the span drops to a line, exactly the redundancy described in the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{span}(v_1, \\dots, v_k) = \\{c_1v_1 + \\dots + c_kv_k : c_i \\in \\mathbb{R}\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{independent} \\iff \\left(c_1v_1 + \\dots + c_kv_k = 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{all } c_i = 0\\right)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\dim \\mathbb{R}^n = n</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Too many vectors are always dependent</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Any <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> (or more) vectors in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> are linearly dependent. Any <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent vectors in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> form a basis.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Idea</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Solving <span class=\"m\">c_1v_1 + \\dots + c_{n+1}v_{n+1} = 0</span> is a system of <span class=\"m\">n</span> equations in <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> unknowns with zero on the right. Elimination leaves at least one free unknown, so a nonzero solution exists. For the second claim: if <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent vectors did not span <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span>, we could add a vector outside their span and get <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> independent vectors, contradicting the first claim.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Are <span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 3)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0, 1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(2, 5, 7)</span> independent?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Look for <span class=\"m\">a, b, c</span> with <span class=\"m\">a(1,2,3) + b(0,1,1) + c(2,5,7) = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>First component: <span class=\"m\">a + 2c = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">a = -2c</span>.</li>\n<li>Second: <span class=\"m\">2a + b + 5c = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">-4c + b + 5c = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">b = -c</span>.</li>\n<li>Third: <span class=\"m\">3a + b + 7c = -6c - c + 7c = 0</span>. Always true, so <span class=\"m\">c</span> is free. Take <span class=\"m\">c = -1</span>: <span class=\"m\">a = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 1</span>.</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">2v_1 + v_2 - v_3 = 0</span>. They are <strong>dependent</strong>: <span class=\"m\">(2, 5, 7) = 2(1, 2, 3) + (0, 1, 1)</span>, and they span only a plane.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Write <span class=\"m\">(7, 4)</span> in the basis <span class=\"m\">v = (2, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">w = (-1, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Solve <span class=\"m\">a(2, 1) + b(-1, 1) = (7, 4)</span>: <span class=\"m\">2a - b = 7</span> and <span class=\"m\">a + b = 4</span>.</li>\n<li>Add the equations: <span class=\"m\">3a = 11</span>, so <span class=\"m\">a = \\tfrac{11}{3}</span>.</li>\n<li>Then <span class=\"m\">b = 4 - \\tfrac{11}{3} = \\tfrac13</span>.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac{11}{3}(2, 1) + \\tfrac13(-1, 1) = (\\tfrac{22 - 1}{3}, \\tfrac{11 + 1}{3}) = (7, 4)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For which value of <span class=\"m\">k</span> are <span class=\"m\">(1, 3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(2, k)</span> linearly dependent? What is their span then?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Two vectors are dependent exactly when one is a multiple of the other. <span class=\"m\">(2, k) = 2(1, 3)</span> requires <span class=\"m\">k = 6</span>. (Equivalently, <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot k - 3\\cdot 2 = 0</span>.) Then the span is the line through the origin in the direction <span class=\"m\">(1, 3)</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">y = 3x</span>. For every other <span class=\"m\">k</span> the span is all of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Redundant features</strong>: if one column of your data is a combination of others (say \"total price\" = \"price\" + \"tax\"), the features are dependent. Linear regression can't separate their effects and <span class=\"m\">X^TX</span> becomes non-invertible.</li>\n<li><strong>Expressive power</strong>: a linear layer's outputs always lie in the span of its weight columns. That's one reason we add nonlinearities between layers.</li>\n<li><strong>Embeddings</strong>: the dimension of an embedding space is the number of independent directions available to encode meaning.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Colour screens mix red, green and blue light. Every displayable colour is a combination of those three independent \"basis colours\", which is why colour is often described as 3-dimensional. A fourth primary would be redundant for most colours.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the dimension of the span of <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(-2, -2)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(-2, -2) = -2(1, 1)</span>, so both lie on one line.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many vectors are in any basis of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^5</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The dimension of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> is <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Any set of vectors containing the zero vector is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">linearly dependent</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">linearly independent</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a basis</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1\\cdot 0 = 0</span> is a combination with a nonzero weight that gives zero.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Three vectors in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span> are always...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">independent</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">dependent</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a basis</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>At most 2 vectors in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span> can be independent.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For which <span class=\"m\">k</span> are <span class=\"m\">(2, 4)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(3, k)</span> dependent?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(3, k) = 1.5(2, 4)</span> needs <span class=\"m\">k = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">(1, 0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0, 1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, 1, 0)</span> span...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">all of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^3</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a plane</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a line</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The third is the sum of the first two, so only two independent directions remain.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "vector-spaces": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-define-a-vector-space-at-all\" data-h=\"3\">Why define a \"vector space\" at all</h3>\n<p>So far a vector has been a list of numbers. But many other objects behave exactly like lists: you can add them and scale them, and the same rules hold. Polynomials, sound signals, images and even functions can be added and scaled. Mathematicians noticed that every theorem in linear algebra only ever uses those two operations and a handful of rules. So instead of proving everything separately for lists, for polynomials and for functions, they defined a <strong>vector space</strong> as \"any collection where addition and scaling work properly\". Prove a theorem once for vector spaces, and it holds for all of them. This lesson also introduces <strong>subspaces</strong>, the flat pieces of space through the origin that will show up again as column spaces, null spaces and the spaces PCA projects onto.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a music app that stores every song clip as a list of 44,100 sound samples per second. If you play two clips at the same time, the speaker adds the samples together. If you turn the volume up by 50%, every sample is multiplied by 1.5. Audio clips therefore add and scale exactly like vectors. Audio engineers mixing tracks are, without saying it, doing linear algebra in a vector space with tens of thousands of dimensions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-rules\" data-h=\"3\">The rules</h3>\n<p>A <strong>vector space</strong> is a set <span class=\"m\">V</span> of objects (called vectors) together with an addition and a scalar multiplication, such that the results stay in <span class=\"m\">V</span> and these rules hold for all <span class=\"m\">u, v, w</span> in <span class=\"m\">V</span> and all scalars <span class=\"m\">a, b</span>:</p>\n<p>The addition rules say <span class=\"m\">u + v = v + u</span>, <span class=\"m\">(u + v) + w = u + (v + w)</span>, there is a zero vector with <span class=\"m\">v + 0 = v</span>, and every <span class=\"m\">v</span> has a negative <span class=\"m\">-v</span> with <span class=\"m\">v + (-v) = 0</span>. The scaling rules say <span class=\"m\">a(u + v) = au + av</span>, <span class=\"m\">(a + b)v = av + bv</span>, <span class=\"m\">a(bv) = (ab)v</span>, and <span class=\"m\">1v = v</span>.</p>\n<p>You don't need to memorize these eight rules. They are exactly the familiar rules of arithmetic, and they hold for lists because they hold for each component. The important part is the word \"stay\": adding two vectors, or scaling one, must never take you outside the set. This is called being <strong>closed</strong> under addition and scaling.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Vector space</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A set with an addition and a scalar multiplication that stay inside the set (closure) and obey the usual rules of arithmetic: commutativity, associativity, a zero vector, negatives and distributivity.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"examples-beyond-arrows\" data-h=\"3\">Examples beyond arrows</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> is the main example. Here are others.</p>\n<p><strong>Polynomials</strong> of degree at most 2, like <span class=\"m\">p(x) = 3 + 2x - x^2</span>. Add two of them and you get another polynomial of degree at most 2; scale one and the same is true. A natural basis is <span class=\"m\">1, x, x^2</span>, so this space has dimension 3, and the polynomial <span class=\"m\">3 + 2x - x^2</span> has coordinates <span class=\"m\">(3, 2, -1)</span>. Polynomials are secretly vectors of coefficients.</p>\n<p><strong>Matrices</strong> of a fixed shape, like all <span class=\"m\">2 \\times 2</span> matrices. You add them entry by entry and scale every entry. This space has dimension 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions</strong> from <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}</span>. The sum <span class=\"m\">(f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x)</span> and the scaling <span class=\"m\">(cf)(x) = c\\,f(x)</span> are again functions. This space is infinite-dimensional, which is the setting for Fourier analysis and kernel methods.</p>\n<h3 id=\"subspaces\" data-h=\"3\">Subspaces</h3>\n<p>A <strong>subspace</strong> is a subset of a vector space that is itself a vector space using the same operations. Because the rules are inherited automatically, you only need to check three things. A subset <span class=\"m\">S</span> is a subspace if:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It contains the zero vector.</li>\n<li>It is closed under addition: if <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> are in <span class=\"m\">S</span>, so is <span class=\"m\">u + v</span>.</li>\n<li>It is closed under scaling: if <span class=\"m\">v</span> is in <span class=\"m\">S</span>, so is <span class=\"m\">cv</span> for every scalar <span class=\"m\">c</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Let's test a few sets in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span>. The line <span class=\"m\">y = 2x</span> contains <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>. If <span class=\"m\">(a, 2a)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(b, 2b)</span> are on it, their sum <span class=\"m\">(a + b, 2(a + b))</span> is too, and <span class=\"m\">c(a, 2a) = (ca, 2ca)</span> is too. So it is a subspace.</p>\n<p>The line <span class=\"m\">y = 2x + 1</span> does not contain <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>, because <span class=\"m\">0 \\ne 1</span>. It fails rule 1 immediately, so it is not a subspace, even though it is a perfectly straight line.</p>\n<p>The first quadrant, all <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> with <span class=\"m\">x \\ge 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">y \\ge 0</span>, contains zero and is closed under addition. But <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> is in it and <span class=\"m\">-1\\cdot(1, 1) = (-1, -1)</span> isn't. It fails rule 3.</p>\n<p>In <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^3</span> the subspaces are exactly: the origin alone, lines through the origin, planes through the origin, and all of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^3</span>. In general, every span is a subspace, and every subspace of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> is the span of some basis.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Subspace</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A subset of a vector space that contains the zero vector and is closed under addition and scalar multiplication. In <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> these are the flat spaces (lines, planes, ...) through the origin.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"two-subspaces-you-will-meet-constantly\" data-h=\"3\">Two subspaces you will meet constantly</h3>\n<p>The set of solutions of a <strong>homogeneous</strong> system <span class=\"m\">Ax = 0</span> is always a subspace, called the null space. If <span class=\"m\">Ax = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">Ay = 0</span>, then <span class=\"m\">A(x + y) = 0 + 0 = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">A(cx) = c\\cdot 0 = 0</span>. Similarly, the set of all outputs <span class=\"m\">Ax</span>, the column space, is a subspace because it is the span of the columns of <span class=\"m\">A</span>. Both are central in the lesson on rank.</p>\n<p>By contrast, the solutions of <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> with <span class=\"m\">b \\ne 0</span> are <strong>not</strong> a subspace, because <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">A0 = 0 \\ne b</span>. They form a shifted copy of the null space.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>The three-rule test is enough because all the arithmetic rules (commutativity, distributivity and so on) are already true in the bigger space, so they remain true for any subset. The only things that can go wrong for a subset are that the zero vector or a negative might be missing, or that an operation could carry you out of the subset. Closure under scaling with <span class=\"m\">c = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">c = -1</span> supplies zero and negatives, so really closure is the heart of the matter.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Calling any straight line a subspace. It must pass through the origin.</li>\n<li>Checking closure with one example. You must show it for <strong>every</strong> pair of vectors and every scalar, usually with letters, not numbers.</li>\n<li>Forgetting negative scalars. Sets like \"vectors with non-negative entries\" fail only because of negative scaling.</li>\n<li>Assuming the union of two subspaces is a subspace. The <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis together with the <span class=\"m\">y</span>-axis is not: <span class=\"m\">(1, 0) + (0, 1) = (1, 1)</span> is on neither.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A vector space is any set where addition and scaling stay inside the set and follow ordinary arithmetic rules. Lists, polynomials, matrices, signals and functions all qualify. A subspace is a subset that contains zero and is closed under addition and scaling; in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> these are the lines, planes and higher flat pieces through the origin. Null spaces and column spaces are the subspaces linear algebra cares about most.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"span\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Every point reachable with the sliders is in the span. A span always passes through the origin and is closed under adding and scaling.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set both sliders to 0. The combination is the zero vector, so the origin is always in the span.\n2. Make <span class=\"m\">w</span> parallel to <span class=\"m\">v</span> so the span is a single line. Pick two points on the line (two slider settings) and add them in your head; the sum is still on the line.\n3. Now set <span class=\"m\">a = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 0</span>: scaling by a negative number keeps you on the line too.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the span of any set is a subspace. It always contains zero and never lets addition or scaling escape. A line that missed the origin could never appear as a span here, which is exactly why <span class=\"m\">y = 2x + 1</span> is not a subspace.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">S \\text{ is a subspace} \\iff 0 \\in S,\\ u + v \\in S,\\ cv \\in S \\ \\ (\\forall u, v \\in S,\\ c \\in \\mathbb{R})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">N(A) = \\{x : Ax = 0\\} \\text{ is always a subspace}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Every span is a subspace</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any vectors <span class=\"m\">v_1, \\dots, v_k</span> in a vector space, <span class=\"m\">\\text{span}(v_1, \\dots, v_k)</span> is a subspace, and it is the smallest subspace containing them.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">0 = 0v_1 + \\dots + 0v_k</span> is in the span. Adding <span class=\"m\">\\sum a_iv_i</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sum b_iv_i</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\sum (a_i + b_i)v_i</span>, again a combination. Scaling gives <span class=\"m\">\\sum (ca_i)v_i</span>. So all three rules hold. Any subspace containing the <span class=\"m\">v_i</span> must, by closure, contain all their combinations, so it contains the span.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Is <span class=\"m\">S = \\{(x, y, z) : x + y + z = 0\\}</span> a subspace of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^3</span>?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Zero: <span class=\"m\">0 + 0 + 0 = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">(0, 0, 0) \\in S</span>.</li>\n<li>Addition: if <span class=\"m\">x_1 + y_1 + z_1 = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_2 + y_2 + z_2 = 0</span>, the sum has <span class=\"m\">(x_1 + x_2) + (y_1 + y_2) + (z_1 + z_2) = 0 + 0 = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>Scaling: <span class=\"m\">cx + cy + cz = c(x + y + z) = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>Yes. It is a plane through the origin, with basis <span class=\"m\">(1, -1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, 0, -1)</span>, so its dimension is 2.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Is <span class=\"m\">T = \\{(x, y) : xy = 0\\}</span> a subspace of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span>?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>It contains <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> and is closed under scaling: <span class=\"m\">(cx)(cy) = c^2xy = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>But <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span> are in <span class=\"m\">T</span> while their sum <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> has <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot1 = 1 \\ne 0</span>.</li>\n<li>Not a subspace: it is the union of the two axes, which fails closure under addition.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">P_2</span> be the polynomials of degree at most 2. Show that <span class=\"m\">W = \\{p \\in P_2 : p(1) = 0\\}</span> is a subspace, and find its dimension.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Zero polynomial: <span class=\"m\">0(1) = 0</span>. If <span class=\"m\">p(1) = q(1) = 0</span> then <span class=\"m\">(p + q)(1) = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">(cp)(1) = c\\cdot0 = 0</span>. So <span class=\"m\">W</span> is a subspace. Writing <span class=\"m\">p = a + bx + cx^2</span>, the condition is <span class=\"m\">a + b + c = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">a = -b - c</span> and <span class=\"m\">p = b(x - 1) + c(x^2 - 1)</span>. The polynomials <span class=\"m\">x - 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">x^2 - 1</span> are independent and span <span class=\"m\">W</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\dim W = 2</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>PCA, autoencoders and LoRA all assume data or updates live near a <strong>low-dimensional subspace</strong> of a huge space.</li>\n<li>Kernel methods treat functions as vectors in an infinite-dimensional space and still use dot products and projections.</li>\n<li>The null space of a weight matrix is the set of input directions a linear layer completely ignores.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Mixing audio, blending photographs (a 30% / 70% cross-fade is <span class=\"m\">0.3a + 0.7b</span>) and combining recipes in fixed proportions all treat signals as vectors in a vector space.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is the line <span class=\"m\">y = 3x + 2</span> a subspace of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It doesn't pass through the origin.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the dimension of the space of polynomials of degree at most 3?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A basis is <span class=\"m\">1, x, x^2, x^3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the dimension of the plane <span class=\"m\">x + y + z = 0</span> in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^3</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>One linear condition removes one dimension from 3.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which set is a subspace of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">all <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> with <span class=\"m\">x \\ge 0</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">all <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> with <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">all <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> with <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The <span class=\"m\">y</span>-axis contains 0 and is closed under addition and scaling.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The solution set of <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> with <span class=\"m\">b \\ne 0</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">always a subspace</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">never a subspace</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a subspace only if <span class=\"m\">A</span> is square</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It doesn't contain <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span>, since <span class=\"m\">A0 = 0 \\ne b</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the dimension of the space of all <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> matrices?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>There are 6 independent entries.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "dot-product": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-need-a-way-to-compare-vectors\" data-h=\"3\">Why we need a way to compare vectors</h3>\n<p>Adding and scaling tell us how to combine vectors, but not how to compare them. Are two vectors pointing in similar directions, opposite directions, or at right angles? How much of one vector lies along another? The <strong>dot product</strong> answers these questions with a single number. It is probably the most used operation in all of machine learning: every neuron, every linear model, every search engine similarity score and every attention head in a transformer is a dot product at heart.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you're pulling a sled with a rope. If you pull straight forward, all your effort moves the sled. If the rope is tilted up at an angle, part of your pull is wasted lifting instead of moving forward. If you pull straight up, none of the pull moves the sled forward at all. The useful amount is your pulling force \"measured along\" the direction of travel. That measurement is exactly what the dot product computes. Physicists call the result <strong>work</strong>: work equals force dot displacement.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition-multiply-and-add\" data-h=\"3\">The definition: multiply and add</h3>\n<p>The dot product of two vectors of the same length is found by multiplying matching components and adding:\n<span class=\"mb\">u \\cdot v = u_1v_1 + u_2v_2 + \\dots + u_nv_n</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 2, 3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (4, -5, 6)</span>: <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = 4 - 10 + 18 = 12</span>. The answer is a plain number (a scalar), not a vector. That's why it's also called the <strong>scalar product</strong> or <strong>inner product</strong>.</p>\n<p>A useful special case: a vector dotted with itself gives its squared length, <span class=\"m\">v \\cdot v = v_1^2 + \\dots + v_n^2 = \\|v\\|^2</span>. For <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 4)</span>, <span class=\"m\">v \\cdot v = 25 = 5^2</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-meaning-lengths-and-the-angle\" data-h=\"3\">The meaning: lengths and the angle</h3>\n<p>The same number has a geometric meaning. If <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> is the angle between <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span>, then\n<span class=\"mb\">u \\cdot v = \\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span>\nSo the dot product is \"length times length times how aligned they are\". The cosine does the comparing. When the vectors point the same way, <span class=\"m\">\\theta = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta = 1</span>, so the dot product is as large as possible. At right angles, <span class=\"m\">\\cos 90° = 0</span>, so the dot product is 0. Pointing opposite ways, <span class=\"m\">\\cos 180° = -1</span>, and the dot product is as negative as possible.</p>\n<p>This gives a quick sign test. If <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v &gt; 0</span> the angle is less than <span class=\"m\">90°</span>; if <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = 0</span> the vectors are <strong>orthogonal</strong> (perpendicular); if <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v &lt; 0</span> the angle is more than <span class=\"m\">90°</span>. For example <span class=\"m\">(1, 2) \\cdot (2, -1) = 2 - 2 = 0</span>, so those two vectors are perpendicular, which you can confirm by sketching them.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Dot product</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = u_1v_1 + \\dots + u_nv_n = \\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span>. A single number measuring how much <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> point in the same direction.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Orthogonal vectors</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Vectors whose dot product is 0. Nonzero orthogonal vectors are at right angles to each other.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"finding-angles\" data-h=\"3\">Finding angles</h3>\n<p>Rearranging the geometric formula lets you compute the angle between any two vectors, even in 100 dimensions where you can't draw them:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\cos\\theta = \\frac{u \\cdot v}{\\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|}</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (1, 1)</span>: <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\| = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\| = \\sqrt2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta = 1/\\sqrt2</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\theta = 45°</span>, as the picture says. The quantity <span class=\"m\">\\frac{u \\cdot v}{\\|u\\|\\|v\\|}</span> is called the <strong>cosine similarity</strong>. It ignores lengths and only measures direction, ranging from <span class=\"m\">-1</span> to <span class=\"m\">1</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"projection-the-shadow-of-one-vector-on-another\" data-h=\"3\">Projection: the shadow of one vector on another</h3>\n<p>Back to the sled. The part of <span class=\"m\">v</span> that lies along the direction of <span class=\"m\">u</span> is called the projection of <span class=\"m\">v</span> onto <span class=\"m\">u</span>. Its signed length is <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span>, which by the formula above equals <span class=\"m\">\\frac{u\\cdot v}{\\|u\\|}</span>. As a vector it points along <span class=\"m\">u</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{proj}_u v = \\frac{u \\cdot v}{u \\cdot u}\\,u</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">u = (2, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 4)</span>: <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = 6</span> and <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot u = 4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\text{proj}_u v = 1.5(2, 0) = (3, 0)</span>. That is the shadow of <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span> on the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis, just as expected. If <span class=\"m\">u</span> is a unit vector the formula simplifies to <span class=\"m\">(u \\cdot v)\\,u</span>, which is why unit vectors are so convenient.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-two-formulas-agree\" data-h=\"3\">Why the two formulas agree</h3>\n<p>Here is the argument using the law of cosines. Draw <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> from the same point; the third side of the triangle is <span class=\"m\">u - v</span>. The law of cosines says <span class=\"m\">\\|u - v\\|^2 = \\|u\\|^2 + \\|v\\|^2 - 2\\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span>. Now expand the left side with components: <span class=\"m\">\\|u - v\\|^2 = (u - v)\\cdot(u - v) = u\\cdot u - 2u\\cdot v + v\\cdot v</span>. Comparing the two expressions, the <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\|^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\|^2</span> terms cancel and we're left with <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = \\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-of-the-dot-product\" data-h=\"3\">Rules of the dot product</h3>\n<p>The dot product is <strong>symmetric</strong>, <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = v \\cdot u</span>, and it <strong>distributes</strong>, <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot (v + w) = u\\cdot v + u \\cdot w</span>. Scalars pull out: <span class=\"m\">(cu)\\cdot v = c(u \\cdot v)</span>. These make it easy to expand expressions like <span class=\"m\">(u + v)\\cdot(u + v) = \\|u\\|^2 + 2u\\cdot v + \\|v\\|^2</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Thinking the dot product gives a vector. It gives a single number.</li>\n<li>Multiplying components without adding them up. <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)\\cdot(3, 4)</span> is <span class=\"m\">3 + 8 = 11</span>, not <span class=\"m\">(3, 8)</span>.</li>\n<li>Dotting vectors of different lengths, which is undefined.</li>\n<li>Concluding that a large dot product means \"very similar\". It can be large just because the vectors are long. Use cosine similarity to compare directions only.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The dot product multiplies matching components and adds them. Geometrically it equals the product of the lengths times the cosine of the angle, so its sign tells you whether vectors point together, apart, or at right angles, and zero means orthogonal. Dividing by the lengths gives the cosine similarity, and the dot product with a direction gives a projection. This one operation is the workhorse of machine learning.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"dot\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag u and v. The green bar is the projection of v onto u; it turns red when the dot product is negative.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Make <span class=\"m\">u = (3, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (2, 2)</span>. Read the dot product (6) and the angle (<span class=\"m\">45°</span>).\n2. Rotate <span class=\"m\">v</span> until the dot product reads 0. Check the angle: <span class=\"m\">90°</span>. The green bar disappears.\n3. Keep rotating past <span class=\"m\">90°</span>. The dot product goes negative and the projection bar turns red and points backwards.\n4. Double the length of <span class=\"m\">u</span> while keeping its direction. The dot product doubles, but the angle and the projection length stay the same.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the dot product's sign follows <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta</span>, and the green bar is <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span>, the \"shadow\" from the article. Step 4 shows why cosine similarity divides by lengths: the raw dot product mixes up size and direction.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">u \\cdot v = \\sum_i u_iv_i = \\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">v \\cdot v = \\|v\\|^2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\cos\\theta = \\frac{u \\cdot v}{\\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{proj}_u v = \\frac{u\\cdot v}{u\\cdot u}\\,u</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cauchy–Schwarz inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any vectors <span class=\"m\">u, v</span>: <span class=\"m\">|u\\cdot v| \\le \\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|</span>, with equality exactly when one is a multiple of the other.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">v = 0</span> both sides are 0. Otherwise, for every real <span class=\"m\">t</span>, <span class=\"m\">0 \\le \\|u - tv\\|^2 = \\|u\\|^2 - 2t(u\\cdot v) + t^2\\|v\\|^2</span>. Choose <span class=\"m\">t = \\frac{u\\cdot v}{\\|v\\|^2}</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">0 \\le \\|u\\|^2 - \\frac{(u\\cdot v)^2}{\\|v\\|^2}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">(u\\cdot v)^2 \\le \\|u\\|^2\\|v\\|^2</span>. Equality needs <span class=\"m\">\\|u - tv\\| = 0</span>, that is, <span class=\"m\">u = tv</span>. This is why <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta</span> always lies between <span class=\"m\">-1</span> and <span class=\"m\">1</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Angle between <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 2, 2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (2, 0, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = 2 + 0 + 2 = 4</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\|u\\| = \\sqrt{1 + 4 + 4} = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\| = \\sqrt{4 + 0 + 1} = \\sqrt5</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta = \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt5} \\approx 0.596</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\theta \\approx 53.4°</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find <span class=\"m\">k</span> so that <span class=\"m\">(2, k, 1)</span> is orthogonal to <span class=\"m\">(3, -1, 4)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Set the dot product to zero: <span class=\"m\">6 - k + 4 = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">k = 10</span>.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"m\">(2, 10, 1)\\cdot(3, -1, 4) = 6 - 10 + 4 = 0</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Work done by a force <span class=\"m\">F = (10, 5)</span> N moving a box by <span class=\"m\">d = (4, 0)</span> m</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Work is <span class=\"m\">F \\cdot d = 10\\cdot4 + 5\\cdot0 = 40</span> joules.</li>\n<li>Only the horizontal part of the force (10 N) did work, because the motion was horizontal.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Vectors <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> satisfy <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\| = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\| = 4</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|u + v\\| = 5</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v</span> and the angle between them.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">\\|u + v\\|^2 = \\|u\\|^2 + 2u\\cdot v + \\|v\\|^2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">25 = 9 + 2u\\cdot v + 16</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = 0</span>. The vectors are orthogonal: <span class=\"m\">\\theta = 90°</span>. (This is Pythagoras, <span class=\"m\">3^2 + 4^2 = 5^2</span>.)</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">The dot product is everywhere</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>A <strong>linear model</strong> predicts <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = w\\cdot x + b</span>: a weighted sum of features.</li>\n<li>A <strong>neuron</strong> computes <span class=\"m\">\\sigma(w \\cdot x + b)</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Attention</strong> in transformers scores how well a query matches a key with <span class=\"m\">q\\cdot k / \\sqrt{d}</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cosine similarity</strong> between embeddings powers semantic search, recommendation and retrieval for chatbots.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A shopping total is a dot product: quantities <span class=\"m\">(2, 1, 3)</span> of items priced 4, 10 and 1 dollars cost <span class=\"m\">(2, 1, 3)\\cdot(4, 10, 1) = 8 + 10 + 3 = 21</span> dollars. Solar panels produce power in proportion to the dot product of the sunlight direction and the panel's facing direction, which is why panels are tilted toward the sun.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><code>np.dot(u, v)</code> or <code>u @ v</code> computes a dot product. Graphics shaders compute <code>dot(normal, lightDir)</code> for every pixel to decide how brightly a surface is lit.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Compute <span class=\"m\">(2, -1, 3)\\cdot(1, 4, 2)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2 - 4 + 6 = 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v = 0</span> and both are nonzero, the angle between them is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">0°</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">90°</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">180°</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta = 0</span> means a right angle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">v \\cdot v</span> for <span class=\"m\">v = (1, 2, 2)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">v\\cdot v = \\|v\\|^2 = 9</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For which <span class=\"m\">k</span> is <span class=\"m\">(1, k)</span> orthogonal to <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">4 + 2k = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The dot product of two vectors is negative. The angle between them is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">less than <span class=\"m\">90°</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">exactly <span class=\"m\">90°</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">more than <span class=\"m\">90°</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The sign of the dot product is the sign of <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the first component of <span class=\"m\">\\text{proj}_u v</span> for <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 7)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{u\\cdot v}{u\\cdot u}u = 3(1, 0) = (3, 0)</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "cross-product": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-another-product\" data-h=\"3\">Why another product?</h3>\n<p>The dot product takes two vectors and returns a number that measures alignment. In three dimensions there is a second, very different product: the <strong>cross product</strong> <span class=\"m\">u \\times v</span>. It takes two 3-D vectors and returns a <strong>vector</strong> that sticks straight out, perpendicular to both of them. Its length tells you the area of the parallelogram the two vectors make. Physics, robotics and 3-D graphics use it constantly: to find the direction a surface faces, to compute torque, and to tell whether a turn is clockwise or counterclockwise. In ML it appears less often, but it's essential for anything involving 3-D geometry, such as point clouds, pose estimation and rendering, and the related idea of \"signed area\" leads directly to determinants.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine tightening a bolt with a wrench. You push on the handle sideways, but the bolt moves along its own axis, in a direction perpendicular to both the handle and your push. Push harder, or use a longer wrench, and the turning effect grows. Push along the handle instead of across it, and nothing turns at all. That turning effect is called <strong>torque</strong>, and it is the cross product of the handle vector and the force vector. The direction of the answer is perpendicular to both inputs, and the size depends on how \"across\" the push is.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-formula\" data-h=\"3\">The formula</h3>\n<p>For <span class=\"m\">u = (u_1, u_2, u_3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (v_1, v_2, v_3)</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">u \\times v = \\begin{bmatrix} u_2v_3 - u_3v_2 \\\\ u_3v_1 - u_1v_3 \\\\ u_1v_2 - u_2v_1 \\end{bmatrix}</span>\nIt looks arbitrary, but there's a pattern. Each component skips its own index and uses the other two in a cyclic order. The first component uses indices 2 and 3, the second uses 3 and 1, and the third uses 1 and 2. In each, it's \"this times that, minus that times this\".</p>\n<p>Let's compute <span class=\"m\">(1, 0, 0) \\times (0, 1, 0)</span>, the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis crossed with the <span class=\"m\">y</span>-axis. First component: <span class=\"m\">0\\cdot0 - 0\\cdot1 = 0</span>. Second: <span class=\"m\">0\\cdot0 - 1\\cdot0 = 0</span>. Third: <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot1 - 0\\cdot0 = 1</span>. The answer is <span class=\"m\">(0, 0, 1)</span>, the <span class=\"m\">z</span>-axis. So <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath \\times \\hat\\jmath = \\hat k</span>, perpendicular to both, as promised.</p>\n<p>A messier example: <span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 3) \\times (4, 5, 6)</span>. First: <span class=\"m\">2\\cdot6 - 3\\cdot5 = 12 - 15 = -3</span>. Second: <span class=\"m\">3\\cdot4 - 1\\cdot6 = 12 - 6 = 6</span>. Third: <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot5 - 2\\cdot4 = 5 - 8 = -3</span>. So the cross product is <span class=\"m\">(-3, 6, -3)</span>. Check perpendicularity with dot products: <span class=\"m\">(-3, 6, -3)\\cdot(1, 2, 3) = -3 + 12 - 9 = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">(-3, 6, -3)\\cdot(4, 5, 6) = -12 + 30 - 18 = 0</span>. Both zero.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cross product</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For 3-D vectors, <span class=\"m\">u \\times v = (u_2v_3 - u_3v_2,\\ u_3v_1 - u_1v_3,\\ u_1v_2 - u_2v_1)</span>. It is perpendicular to both <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span>, and its length is the area of the parallelogram they span.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"which-way-does-it-point-the-right-hand-rule\" data-h=\"3\">Which way does it point? The right-hand rule</h3>\n<p>There are two directions perpendicular to a plane: \"up\" out of it and \"down\" into it. The cross product picks one using the <strong>right-hand rule</strong>. Point the fingers of your right hand along <span class=\"m\">u</span>, curl them toward <span class=\"m\">v</span>, and your thumb points along <span class=\"m\">u \\times v</span>. With <span class=\"m\">u</span> along <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> along <span class=\"m\">y</span>, your thumb points up the <span class=\"m\">z</span>-axis, matching the calculation above.</p>\n<p>This means order matters. If you swap the inputs, your fingers curl the other way and the thumb flips: <span class=\"m\">v \\times u = -(u \\times v)</span>. The cross product is <strong>anti-commutative</strong>. For instance <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath \\times \\hat\\imath = -\\hat k</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"how-long-is-it-area\" data-h=\"3\">How long is it? Area</h3>\n<p>The length of the cross product is\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|u \\times v\\| = \\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|\\sin\\theta</span>\nwhere <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> is the angle between the vectors. Compare this with the dot product, which uses <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta</span>. Geometrically, <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|\\sin\\theta</span> is the area of the parallelogram with sides <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span>: the base is <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\|</span> and the height is <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\|\\sin\\theta</span>. Half of it is the area of the triangle with those two sides.</p>\n<p>Two consequences follow. If <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> are parallel, <span class=\"m\">\\sin\\theta = 0</span>, so the cross product is the zero vector: no parallelogram, no area, no well-defined perpendicular direction. And if <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> are perpendicular unit vectors, the cross product is another unit vector, which is how 3-D software builds a third axis from two.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-of-the-cross-product\" data-h=\"3\">Rules of the cross product</h3>\n<p>It distributes over addition: <span class=\"m\">u \\times (v + w) = u \\times v + u \\times w</span>. Scalars pull out: <span class=\"m\">(cu)\\times v = c(u \\times v)</span>. Any vector crossed with itself gives zero. But it is <strong>not</strong> associative: <span class=\"m\">(u \\times v)\\times w</span> is usually different from <span class=\"m\">u \\times (v \\times w)</span>. For example <span class=\"m\">(\\hat\\imath \\times \\hat\\imath)\\times \\hat\\jmath = 0</span>, while <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath \\times (\\hat\\imath \\times \\hat\\jmath) = \\hat\\imath \\times \\hat k = -\\hat\\jmath</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"volume-the-scalar-triple-product\" data-h=\"3\">Volume: the scalar triple product</h3>\n<p>Combine both products: <span class=\"m\">u \\cdot (v \\times w)</span> is a single number whose absolute value is the volume of the slanted box (parallelepiped) with edges <span class=\"m\">u</span>, <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">w</span>. The base area is <span class=\"m\">\\|v \\times w\\|</span>, and dotting with <span class=\"m\">u</span> picks up the height. If the three vectors lie in one plane, the volume is 0, so the triple product is a quick test for whether three 3-D vectors are dependent. This number is exactly the determinant of the <span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span> matrix with rows <span class=\"m\">u</span>, <span class=\"m\">v</span>, <span class=\"m\">w</span>, which you will meet later.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>Why is <span class=\"m\">u \\times v</span> perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">u</span>? Expand the dot product: <span class=\"m\">u_1(u_2v_3 - u_3v_2) + u_2(u_3v_1 - u_1v_3) + u_3(u_1v_2 - u_2v_1)</span>. Multiply out and every term appears twice with opposite signs, for example <span class=\"m\">u_1u_2v_3</span> and <span class=\"m\">-u_2u_1v_3</span>, so everything cancels to 0. The same happens for <span class=\"m\">v</span>. The length formula comes from the identity <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\times v\\|^2 = \\|u\\|^2\\|v\\|^2 - (u\\cdot v)^2</span>, and since <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v = \\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span>, this equals <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\|^2\\|v\\|^2(1 - \\cos^2\\theta) = \\|u\\|^2\\|v\\|^2\\sin^2\\theta</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Using the cross product in 2-D or 5-D. The vector version only exists in 3-D. (In 2-D people often use the single number <span class=\"m\">u_1v_2 - u_2v_1</span>, the \"2-D cross product\", which is the signed area.)</li>\n<li>Getting the middle component's sign wrong. It's <span class=\"m\">u_3v_1 - u_1v_3</span>, not <span class=\"m\">u_1v_3 - u_3v_1</span>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that order matters: <span class=\"m\">u \\times v = -(v \\times u)</span>.</li>\n<li>Expecting a number. The cross product is a vector; the dot product is the one that returns a number.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The cross product of two 3-D vectors is a vector perpendicular to both, pointing by the right-hand rule, with length <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\sin\\theta</span>, the area of their parallelogram. It is zero for parallel vectors and flips sign when the order is swapped. Dotting it with a third vector gives the volume of a box, which is the determinant. Always check your answer by dotting it with both inputs: you should get zero twice.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g03-cross\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Change u and v with the sliders and turn the camera. The purple arrow is u × v; the yellow parallelogram's area is its length.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start with <span class=\"m\">u = (2, 0, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (1, 2, 0)</span>. Both lie flat on the floor, so <span class=\"m\">u \\times v</span> points straight up. Read its length: 4, the area of the parallelogram (base 2, height 2).\n2. Swap roles by making <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 2, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (2, 0, 0)</span>. The purple arrow flips downwards: anti-commutativity.\n3. Make <span class=\"m\">v</span> parallel to <span class=\"m\">u</span>, for example <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 0, 0)</span>. The parallelogram flattens and <span class=\"m\">u \\times v</span> becomes zero.\n4. Give both vectors a <span class=\"m\">z</span>-component and turn the camera. The purple arrow stays at right angles to the yellow parallelogram, and the panel's two dot-product checks stay at 0.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the direction of <span class=\"m\">u \\times v</span> is perpendicular to the plane of <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span>, and its length is the area, exactly the two facts in the article. Area depends on how \"across\" the vectors are, the <span class=\"m\">\\sin\\theta</span> factor.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">u \\times v = (u_2v_3 - u_3v_2,\\ u_3v_1 - u_1v_3,\\ u_1v_2 - u_2v_1)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|u \\times v\\| = \\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|\\sin\\theta = \\text{area of parallelogram}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">v \\times u = -(u \\times v), \\qquad u \\times u = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{volume} = |u \\cdot (v \\times w)|</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Perpendicularity and Lagrange's identity</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">(u \\times v)\\cdot u = (u\\times v)\\cdot v = 0</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\|u \\times v\\|^2 = \\|u\\|^2\\|v\\|^2 - (u\\cdot v)^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Perpendicularity: expanding <span class=\"m\">(u\\times v)\\cdot u</span> gives six terms that cancel in pairs, as shown in the article; similarly for <span class=\"m\">v</span>. For the identity, expand both sides in components. The left side is <span class=\"m\">(u_2v_3 - u_3v_2)^2 + (u_3v_1 - u_1v_3)^2 + (u_1v_2 - u_2v_1)^2</span> and the right side is <span class=\"m\">(u_1^2 + u_2^2 + u_3^2)(v_1^2 + v_2^2 + v_3^2) - (u_1v_1 + u_2v_2 + u_3v_3)^2</span>. Both simplify to <span class=\"m\">\\sum_{i&lt;j}(u_iv_j - u_jv_i)^2</span>. Substituting <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v = \\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span> then gives <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\times v\\| = \\|u\\|\\|v\\|\\sin\\theta</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute <span class=\"m\">(2, 1, -1) \\times (1, 3, 2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>First: <span class=\"m\">u_2v_3 - u_3v_2 = 1\\cdot2 - (-1)\\cdot3 = 2 + 3 = 5</span>.</li>\n<li>Second: <span class=\"m\">u_3v_1 - u_1v_3 = (-1)\\cdot1 - 2\\cdot2 = -1 - 4 = -5</span>.</li>\n<li>Third: <span class=\"m\">u_1v_2 - u_2v_1 = 2\\cdot3 - 1\\cdot1 = 5</span>.</li>\n<li>Result <span class=\"m\">(5, -5, 5)</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">(5, -5, 5)\\cdot(2, 1, -1) = 10 - 5 - 5 = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">(5, -5, 5)\\cdot(1, 3, 2) = 5 - 15 + 10 = 0</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Area of the triangle with corners <span class=\"m\">A = (0, 0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">B = (3, 0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">C = (0, 4, 0)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Edge vectors: <span class=\"m\">B - A = (3, 0, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">C - A = (0, 4, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li>Cross product: <span class=\"m\">(0\\cdot0 - 0\\cdot4,\\ 0\\cdot0 - 3\\cdot0,\\ 3\\cdot4 - 0\\cdot0) = (0, 0, 12)</span>.</li>\n<li>Parallelogram area 12, so the triangle's area is <span class=\"m\">6</span>. (Check: a right triangle with legs 3 and 4 has area <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac12\\cdot3\\cdot4 = 6</span>.)</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Find a unit vector perpendicular to the plane containing <span class=\"m\">P = (1, 0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">Q = (0, 1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">R = (0, 0, 1)</span>, and the area of triangle <span class=\"m\">PQR</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Edges: <span class=\"m\">Q - P = (-1, 1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">R - P = (-1, 0, 1)</span>. Cross product: <span class=\"m\">(1\\cdot1 - 0\\cdot0,\\ 0\\cdot(-1) - (-1)\\cdot1,\\ (-1)\\cdot0 - 1\\cdot(-1)) = (1, 1, 1)</span>. Its length is <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt3</span>, so a unit normal is <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}(1, 1, 1)</span>. The triangle's area is half the parallelogram's: <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2} \\approx 0.866</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>3-D graphics compute a <strong>surface normal</strong> for every triangle in a mesh as <span class=\"m\">(B - A)\\times(C - A)</span>, normalized. The normal decides lighting and whether a face points toward the camera (back-face culling). Computational geometry uses the 2-D cross product <span class=\"m\">u_1v_2 - u_2v_1</span> to test whether three points make a left turn or a right turn, the key step in convex-hull algorithms.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>3-D vision models (point-cloud networks, pose estimation, neural rendering) compute normals and rotations with cross products. Rotation matrices can be built from an axis and a cross-product matrix (Rodrigues' formula).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Torque is <span class=\"m\">\\tau = r \\times F</span>: a 0.3 m wrench with a 50 N push at right angles gives <span class=\"m\">0.3\\cdot50 = 15</span> N·m of torque along the bolt. The magnetic force on a moving charge, <span class=\"m\">F = q\\,v \\times B</span>, is why electric motors spin.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath \\times \\hat\\imath</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\hat k</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">-\\hat k</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">0</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Anti-commutativity: <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath\\times\\hat\\imath = -(\\hat\\imath\\times\\hat\\jmath) = -\\hat k</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the third component of <span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 3)\\times(4, 5, 6)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">u_1v_2 - u_2v_1 = 5 - 8 = -3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> are parallel, <span class=\"m\">u \\times v</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the zero vector</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a unit vector</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">parallel to <span class=\"m\">u</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sin\\theta = 0</span>, so there is no area.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the area of the parallelogram spanned by <span class=\"m\">(2, 0, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(0, 3, 0)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The cross product is <span class=\"m\">(0, 0, 6)</span>, of length 6.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">(u \\times v)\\cdot u</span> equals...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">0</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\|u\\|^2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">u \\cdot v</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The cross product is perpendicular to both inputs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the volume of the box with edges <span class=\"m\">(1, 0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0, 2, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0, 0, 3)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(0, 2, 0)\\times(0, 0, 3) = (6, 0, 0)</span>, and dotting with <span class=\"m\">(1, 0, 0)</span> gives 6.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "norms": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-more-than-one-way-to-measure-length\" data-h=\"3\">Why more than one way to measure length?</h3>\n<p>In the first lesson the length of a vector came from Pythagoras: square, add, square-root. That's the right answer if you can fly in a straight line. But it isn't the only reasonable answer. A taxi in a city has to drive along streets. A chess king moves one square in any direction per turn. A warehouse robot might be limited by its slowest motor. Each situation measures \"how far\" differently. Mathematicians capture all of these with the idea of a <strong>norm</strong>, a function that measures the size of a vector and obeys a few common-sense rules. In machine learning, the choice of norm changes the behaviour of models: it decides whether a regularizer makes weights small or makes them exactly zero, and how robust a distance-based method is to outliers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you're in Manhattan at the corner of 1st Avenue and 1st Street, and you want to get to 4th Avenue and 5th Street. The blocks form a grid. A bird flies straight there: 3 blocks across and 4 blocks up gives a straight-line distance of <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5</span> blocks. A taxi can't cut through buildings. It must drive 3 blocks across and 4 blocks up, <span class=\"m\">3 + 4 = 7</span> blocks, whatever route it takes along the grid. And if you only cared about the longest single leg of the trip, say because you're timing how long you wait at one long stretch, it would be <span class=\"m\">\\max(3, 4) = 4</span>. Three different, sensible notions of distance for the same trip. These are the <span class=\"m\">L_2</span>, <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> norms of the vector <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-main-norms\" data-h=\"3\">The three main norms</h3>\n<p>For a vector <span class=\"m\">x = (x_1, \\dots, x_n)</span>:</p>\n<p>The <strong><span class=\"m\">L_2</span> norm</strong> (Euclidean norm) is the straight-line length:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_2 = \\sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \\dots + x_n^2}</span>\nThis is the default; when people write <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|</span> without a subscript, they usually mean <span class=\"m\">L_2</span>.</p>\n<p>The <strong><span class=\"m\">L_1</span> norm</strong> (taxicab or Manhattan norm) adds the absolute values:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_1 = |x_1| + |x_2| + \\dots + |x_n|</span></p>\n<p>The <strong><span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> norm</strong> (max norm or Chebyshev norm) takes the largest absolute value:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_\\infty = \\max_i |x_i|</span></p>\n<p>For <span class=\"m\">x = (3, -4)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_2 = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_1 = 3 + 4 = 7</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_\\infty = 4</span>. Notice that <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_\\infty \\le \\|x\\|_2 \\le \\|x\\|_1</span>. This ordering holds for every vector.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-whole-family-mathph25x-norms\" data-h=\"3\">The whole family: <span class=\"m\">L_p</span> norms</h3>\n<p>All three are members of one family. For any <span class=\"m\">p \\ge 1</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_p = \\left(|x_1|^p + \\dots + |x_n|^p\\right)^{1/p}</span>\nWith <span class=\"m\">p = 1</span> you get the taxicab norm and with <span class=\"m\">p = 2</span> the Euclidean norm. As <span class=\"m\">p</span> grows, the largest component dominates the sum more and more, and in the limit you get the max norm, which is why it's called <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span>. For <span class=\"m\">x = (3, 4)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_3 = (27 + 64)^{1/3} = 91^{1/3} \\approx 4.50</span>, between <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_2 = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_\\infty = 4</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-makes-something-a-norm\" data-h=\"3\">What makes something a norm</h3>\n<p>A <strong>norm</strong> is any function <span class=\"m\">\\|\\cdot\\|</span> that satisfies three rules. <strong>Positivity</strong>: <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\| \\ge 0</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\| = 0</span> only when <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span>. <strong>Scaling</strong>: <span class=\"m\">\\|cx\\| = |c|\\,\\|x\\|</span>; doubling a vector doubles its size. <strong>Triangle inequality</strong>: <span class=\"m\">\\|x + y\\| \\le \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|</span>; a detour is never shorter than going straight. All <span class=\"m\">L_p</span> norms with <span class=\"m\">p \\ge 1</span> pass these tests. With <span class=\"m\">p &lt; 1</span> the triangle inequality fails, so those are not true norms, even though people sometimes use them.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Norm</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A function <span class=\"m\">\\|\\cdot\\|</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\| \\ge 0</span> (zero only for <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span>), <span class=\"m\">\\|cx\\| = |c|\\,\\|x\\|</span>, and the triangle inequality <span class=\"m\">\\|x + y\\| \\le \\|x\\| + \\|y\\|</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Lp norm</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_p = \\left(\\sum_i |x_i|^p\\right)^{1/p}</span> for <span class=\"m\">p \\ge 1</span>. Special cases: <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> (sum of absolute values), <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> (Euclidean length), <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> (largest absolute value).</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"distances-from-norms\" data-h=\"3\">Distances from norms</h3>\n<p>Every norm gives a distance: the distance between <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\|x - y\\|</span>. So there's a Euclidean distance, a Manhattan distance and a Chebyshev distance. For the points <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(4, 5)</span>, the difference is <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>, so the distances are 5, 7 and 4 respectively. Which one you choose changes which points count as \"nearest neighbours\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"picturing-norms-unit-balls\" data-h=\"3\">Picturing norms: unit balls</h3>\n<p>A great way to see the difference is to draw every vector whose norm equals 1, the <strong>unit ball</strong> (its boundary, really). For <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> it's the familiar circle <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = 1</span>. For <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> it's <span class=\"m\">|x| + |y| = 1</span>, a diamond with corners at <span class=\"m\">(\\pm1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(0, \\pm1)</span>. For <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> it's <span class=\"m\">\\max(|x|, |y|) = 1</span>, a square with corners at <span class=\"m\">(\\pm1, \\pm1)</span>. As <span class=\"m\">p</span> goes from 1 to <span class=\"m\">\\infty</span>, the shape inflates smoothly from the diamond through the circle to the square. The sharp corners of the <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> diamond sit exactly on the axes, where one coordinate is zero, and that fact is the secret behind sparse models.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works-why-l1-gives-sparse-solutions\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works: why L1 gives sparse solutions</h3>\n<p>In machine learning we often minimize \"error + <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\cdot</span> size of the weights\". Picture the error's contour lines as ellipses and the size penalty as a norm ball that the solution must stay inside. The best solution is where the smallest error ellipse first touches the ball. A circle is smooth, so it is usually touched at a point where both coordinates are nonzero. A diamond has corners poking out along the axes, so an ellipse usually touches a corner first, and at a corner some weights are exactly zero. That's why <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> regularization (lasso) performs automatic feature selection, while <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> (ridge) only shrinks.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Forgetting the absolute values in <span class=\"m\">L_1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\|(3, -4)\\|_1 = 7</span>, not <span class=\"m\">-1</span>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the square root in <span class=\"m\">L_2</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_2^2</span> (squared norm) is common in ML formulas because it's easier to differentiate, but it is not itself a norm.</li>\n<li>Thinking <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> adds things up. It just takes the single largest absolute entry.</li>\n<li>Comparing distances computed with different norms. A 5 in <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> and a 5 in <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> mean different things.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A norm measures the size of a vector. <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> is straight-line length, <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> is taxicab length, <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> is the largest component, and the <span class=\"m\">L_p</span> family connects them. Every norm gives a distance <span class=\"m\">\\|x - y\\|</span>. Their unit balls are a diamond, a circle and a square, and the diamond's corners on the axes explain why <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> regularization produces sparse weights.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"norms\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the pink vector and change p. The dashed shapes are the L1 diamond, L2 circle and L∞ square.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Drag the pink vector to <span class=\"m\">(0.6, 0.8)</span>. Its <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> norm is 1, so it sits on the circle. Read its <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> norm (1.4) and <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> norm (0.8).\n2. Move it onto an axis, like <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>. All three norms agree. The shapes all touch at the axes.\n3. Move it to a diagonal like <span class=\"m\">(0.7, 0.7)</span>, where the norms disagree most.\n4. Slide <span class=\"m\">p</span> from 1 up to 8 and watch the shape grow from the diamond toward the square.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> norms agree on the axes and disagree most on diagonals, because they differ in how they combine several nonzero components. The diamond's pointed corners are on the axes, the picture behind sparse <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> solutions in the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_1 = \\sum_i |x_i| \\qquad \\|x\\|_2 = \\sqrt{\\textstyle\\sum_i x_i^2} \\qquad \\|x\\|_\\infty = \\max_i |x_i|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_p = \\left(\\textstyle\\sum_i |x_i|^p\\right)^{1/p}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_\\infty \\le \\|x\\|_2 \\le \\|x\\|_1 \\le n\\,\\|x\\|_\\infty</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Norm inequalities</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any <span class=\"m\">x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_\\infty \\le \\|x\\|_2 \\le \\|x\\|_1 \\le \\sqrt n\\,\\|x\\|_2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The largest <span class=\"m\">|x_i|^2</span> is at most the sum of all <span class=\"m\">x_i^2</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_\\infty \\le \\|x\\|_2</span>. Squaring <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_1</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\sum x_i^2 + \\sum_{i\\ne j}|x_i||x_j| \\ge \\sum x_i^2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_2 \\le \\|x\\|_1</span>. Finally <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\|_1 = (|x_1|, \\dots, |x_n|)\\cdot(1, \\dots, 1) \\le \\|x\\|_2\\,\\sqrt n</span> by Cauchy–Schwarz.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute the <span class=\"m\">L_1</span>, <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> norms of <span class=\"m\">x = (1, -2, 2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L_1</span>: <span class=\"m\">1 + 2 + 2 = 5</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L_2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{1 + 4 + 4} = 3</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span>: the largest absolute value is <span class=\"m\">2</span>.</li>\n<li>Check the ordering: <span class=\"m\">2 \\le 3 \\le 5</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Which point is closer to the origin, <span class=\"m\">a = (3, 3)</span> or <span class=\"m\">b = (4, 0)</span>?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>In <span class=\"m\">L_2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\|a\\|_2 = \\sqrt{18} \\approx 4.24</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|b\\|_2 = 4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">b</span> is closer.</li>\n<li>In <span class=\"m\">L_1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\|a\\|_1 = 6</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|b\\|_1 = 4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">b</span> is closer.</li>\n<li>In <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\|a\\|_\\infty = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|b\\|_\\infty = 4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">a</span> is closer.</li>\n<li>The answer depends on the norm.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Find the <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> distance and the <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> distance between <span class=\"m\">p = (2, -1, 3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">q = (5, 3, 3)</span>, and verify the triangle inequality for <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 2)</span>, <span class=\"m\">v = (2, -2)</span> in <span class=\"m\">L_2</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">q - p = (3, 4, 0)</span>. <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> distance <span class=\"m\">= 3 + 4 + 0 = 7</span>; <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> distance <span class=\"m\">= \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5</span>. For the triangle inequality: <span class=\"m\">u + v = (3, 0)</span> has length 3, while <span class=\"m\">\\|u\\| + \\|v\\| = \\sqrt5 + \\sqrt8 \\approx 2.236 + 2.828 = 5.06</span>. Indeed <span class=\"m\">3 \\le 5.06</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">L1 vs L2 regularization</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Adding <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\|w\\|_2^2</span> to a loss (<strong>ridge</strong> regression, <strong>weight decay</strong> in deep learning) shrinks all weights smoothly toward zero. Adding <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\|w\\|_1</span> (<strong>lasso</strong>) pushes many weights to exactly zero, giving sparse, interpretable models. Elastic net uses both. k-nearest-neighbours and k-means can use any distance; <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> is more robust to a single huge coordinate. Gradient clipping limits <span class=\"m\">\\|g\\|_2</span>, and adversarial robustness research often bounds perturbations in <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> (\"no pixel changes by more than <span class=\"m\">\\epsilon</span>\").</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Taxi and delivery apps in grid cities estimate travel with Manhattan distance. In chess, the number of moves a king needs between squares is the <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> distance. Quality control often uses the max norm: a part passes only if <strong>every</strong> measurement is within tolerance.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Compute <span class=\"m\">\\|(3, -4)\\|_1</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">|3| + |-4| = 7</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Compute <span class=\"m\">\\|(3, -4)\\|_\\infty</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The largest absolute value.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which regularizer tends to produce sparse weights (many exactly zero)?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">L1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">L2</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The corners of the L1 diamond lie on the axes.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The unit ball of the <span class=\"m\">L_\\infty</span> norm in 2-D is a...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">circle</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">diamond</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">square</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\max(|x|, |y|) = 1</span> is a square with corners <span class=\"m\">(\\pm1, \\pm1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Compute <span class=\"m\">\\|(1, -2, 2)\\|_2</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{1 + 4 + 4}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the Manhattan (<span class=\"m\">L_1</span>) distance between <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(4, 5)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">|4 - 1| + |5 - 1| = 3 + 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "matrices": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Why matrices?</h3>\n<p>Once you have many vectors, or many equations, writing them one by one gets messy. A <strong>matrix</strong> is a rectangular grid of numbers that packs a lot of information into one object. A spreadsheet of data is a matrix: each row is an example, each column is a feature. A system of equations is a matrix of coefficients. And, most importantly, a matrix is a machine that takes in a vector and produces a new vector. Every layer of a neural network is such a machine. This lesson teaches you how to read a matrix and how to multiply it by a vector, which is the single most important computation in this course.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you run a small bakery that sells cakes and pies. Each cake needs 2 eggs and 1 cup of sugar; each pie needs 1 egg and 3 cups of sugar. Tomorrow you plan to bake 5 cakes and 6 pies. How many eggs and how much sugar do you need? Eggs: <span class=\"m\">2\\cdot5 + 1\\cdot6 = 16</span>. Sugar: <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot5 + 3\\cdot6 = 23</span>. You just did a matrix-vector multiplication. The recipe table is the matrix, the order <span class=\"m\">(5, 6)</span> is the input vector, and the shopping list <span class=\"m\">(16, 23)</span> is the output vector:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 \\\\ 1 &amp; 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 5 \\\\ 6 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 16 \\\\ 23 \\end{bmatrix}</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-a-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">Reading a matrix</h3>\n<p>An <span class=\"m\">m \\times n</span> matrix (\"m by n\") has <span class=\"m\">m</span> rows and <span class=\"m\">n</span> columns. Rows run across, columns run down. The entry in row <span class=\"m\">i</span> and column <span class=\"m\">j</span> is written <span class=\"m\">A_{ij}</span>, always row first. For\n<span class=\"mb\">A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 \\\\ 3 &amp; -1 &amp; 4 \\end{bmatrix}</span>\nthe shape is <span class=\"m\">2 \\times 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">A_{12} = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">A_{23} = 4</span>. A matrix with the same number of rows and columns is <strong>square</strong>. A vector is a matrix with one column, so an <span class=\"m\">n</span>-vector is <span class=\"m\">n \\times 1</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A rectangular grid of numbers with <span class=\"m\">m</span> rows and <span class=\"m\">n</span> columns (an <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix). The entry in row <span class=\"m\">i</span>, column <span class=\"m\">j</span> is <span class=\"m\">A_{ij}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"multiplying-a-matrix-by-a-vector-the-row-view\" data-h=\"3\">Multiplying a matrix by a vector: the row view</h3>\n<p>To compute <span class=\"m\">Ax</span>, take each row of <span class=\"m\">A</span> and dot it with <span class=\"m\">x</span>. Each dot product gives one entry of the answer:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 2 \\\\ 3 &amp; 4 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 5 \\\\ 6 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1\\cdot5 + 2\\cdot6 \\\\ 3\\cdot5 + 4\\cdot6 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 17 \\\\ 39 \\end{bmatrix}</span>\nRow 1 is <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>, and <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)\\cdot(5, 6) = 17</span>. Row 2 is <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>, and <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)\\cdot(5, 6) = 39</span>. This is the bakery calculation: each row is one ingredient, and its dot product with the order gives the total of that ingredient.</p>\n<p>For the dot products to make sense, each row must have as many entries as <span class=\"m\">x</span>. So an <span class=\"m\">m \\times n</span> matrix can only multiply an <span class=\"m\">n</span>-vector, and the answer has one entry per row: an <span class=\"m\">m</span>-vector. In shapes: <span class=\"m\">(m \\times n)(n \\times 1) = (m \\times 1)</span>. The inner numbers must match and the outer numbers give the result.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-column-view-a-combination-of-columns\" data-h=\"3\">The column view: a combination of columns</h3>\n<p>There is a second, deeper way to see the same product. Look at what the arithmetic does to the columns:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 2 \\\\ 3 &amp; 4 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 5 \\\\ 6 \\end{bmatrix} = 5\\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\end{bmatrix} + 6\\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 \\\\ 15 \\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix} 12 \\\\ 24 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 17 \\\\ 39 \\end{bmatrix}</span>\nSame answer. <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> is a <strong>linear combination of the columns of <span class=\"m\">A</span></strong>, and the entries of <span class=\"m\">x</span> are the weights. In the bakery, column 1 is \"what one cake needs\" and column 2 is \"what one pie needs\". The order says \"5 of the first column plus 6 of the second\".</p>\n<p>This view connects matrices to everything from the vectors unit. The set of all possible outputs <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> is the span of the columns. Asking \"can I solve <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span>?\" is the same as asking \"is <span class=\"m\">b</span> a combination of the columns?\", the span question from before.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key idea</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Two views of one product. <strong>Row view:</strong> entry <span class=\"m\">i</span> of <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> is (row <span class=\"m\">i</span> of <span class=\"m\">A</span>) <span class=\"m\">\\cdot\\ x</span>. <strong>Column view:</strong> <span class=\"m\">Ax = x_1(\\text{col}_1) + \\dots + x_n(\\text{col}_n)</span>. The column view explains what outputs are possible; the row view is how you compute quickly by hand.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"linearity\" data-h=\"3\">Linearity</h3>\n<p>Matrix-vector multiplication respects addition and scaling: <span class=\"m\">A(x + y) = Ax + Ay</span> and <span class=\"m\">A(cx) = c(Ax)</span>. In the bakery, if you double the order, you double the shopping list; if you combine two orders, you combine the lists. This property is called <strong>linearity</strong>, and it is what puts the \"linear\" in linear algebra. It also means <span class=\"m\">A0 = 0</span>: ordering nothing needs no ingredients.</p>\n<h3 id=\"systems-of-equations-as-mathph58x\" data-h=\"3\">Systems of equations as <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span></h3>\n<p>Matrices let us write a whole system of equations at once. The system\n<span class=\"mb\">2x + y = 5, \\qquad x - y = 1</span>\nis exactly\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 \\\\ 1 &amp; -1 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}</span>\nbecause the row view of the left side reproduces the left sides of the equations. The solution is <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>; check with the column view: <span class=\"m\">2(2, 1) + 1(1, -1) = (5, 1)</span>. Later lessons show how to solve such systems systematically.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-identity-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">The identity matrix</h3>\n<p>The <strong>identity matrix</strong> <span class=\"m\">I</span> has 1s on the main diagonal (top-left to bottom-right) and 0s elsewhere. It does nothing: <span class=\"m\">Ix = x</span> for every <span class=\"m\">x</span>. In the column view, its columns are <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span>, and <span class=\"m\">x_1\\hat\\imath + x_2\\hat\\jmath = x</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Identity matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The square matrix <span class=\"m\">I</span> with 1 on the diagonal and 0 elsewhere. <span class=\"m\">Ix = x</span> for every vector <span class=\"m\">x</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Mixing up rows and columns in <span class=\"m\">A_{ij}</span>. The first index is always the row.</li>\n<li>Multiplying when the shapes don't match. A <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> matrix needs a 3-vector.</li>\n<li>Dotting columns of <span class=\"m\">A</span> with <span class=\"m\">x</span> instead of rows.</li>\n<li>Writing the answer with the wrong length. An <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix outputs a vector with <span class=\"m\">m</span> entries, one per row, not <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A matrix is a grid with <span class=\"m\">m</span> rows and <span class=\"m\">n</span> columns. It multiplies an <span class=\"m\">n</span>-vector to produce an <span class=\"m\">m</span>-vector. The row view computes each output entry as a dot product of a row with <span class=\"m\">x</span>. The column view shows <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> as a combination of the columns weighted by <span class=\"m\">x</span>, which is why solving <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> means writing <span class=\"m\">b</span> as a combination of columns. The product is linear, and the identity matrix leaves every vector unchanged.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;shear&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The matrix A acts on every vector of the plane. Its columns are where î and ĵ land. Drag them.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose the <strong>identity</strong> preset. The grid doesn't move and the columns are <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span>.\n2. Choose <strong>shear</strong>. The matrix is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>: the pink arrow (<span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span>) stays put and the blue arrow (<span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span>) lands at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>.\n3. Drag the pink arrow to <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> and the blue arrow to <span class=\"m\">(1, 3)</span>. You've built the bakery matrix <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> in column form.\n4. Find where the grid point <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> lands: it's the corner of the yellow square opposite the origin, column 1 + column 2.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the panel shows the columns of <span class=\"m\">A</span> are exactly where <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> go. Every other point <span class=\"m\">x</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">x_1(\\text{col}_1) + x_2(\\text{col}_2)</span>, which is the column view of <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> from the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">(Ax)_i = \\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij}x_j = (\\text{row } i)\\cdot x</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Ax = x_1 a_1 + x_2 a_2 + \\dots + x_n a_n \\quad (a_j = \\text{column } j)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(m\\times n)(n\\times1) = (m\\times 1)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A(x + y) = Ax + Ay, \\qquad A(cx) = cAx</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\"><span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> is solvable exactly when <span class=\"m\">b</span> is in the span of the columns</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The equation <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> has a solution if and only if <span class=\"m\">b</span> is a linear combination of the columns of <span class=\"m\">A</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By the column view, <span class=\"m\">Ax = x_1a_1 + \\dots + x_na_n</span>. So a vector <span class=\"m\">x</span> with <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> is exactly a set of weights that makes <span class=\"m\">b</span> out of the columns.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \\\\ -1 &amp; 3 &amp; 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Shapes: <span class=\"m\">(2\\times3)(3\\times1)</span> gives a 2-vector.</li>\n<li>Row 1: <span class=\"m\">(2, 0, 1)\\cdot(1, 2, 3) = 2 + 0 + 3 = 5</span>.</li>\n<li>Row 2: <span class=\"m\">(-1, 3, 2)\\cdot(1, 2, 3) = -1 + 6 + 6 = 11</span>.</li>\n<li>Result <span class=\"m\">(5, 11)</span>. Column check: <span class=\"m\">1(2, -1) + 2(0, 3) + 3(1, 2) = (2 + 0 + 3, -1 + 6 + 6) = (5, 11)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Write the system <span class=\"m\">x + 2y - z = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">3x - y = 7</span> as <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Unknowns <span class=\"m\">(x, y, z)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">A</span> has 3 columns; two equations, so 2 rows.</li>\n<li>Coefficients row by row (a missing variable has coefficient 0): <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; 2 &amp; -1\\\\ 3 &amp; -1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li>Right side <span class=\"m\">b = (4, 7)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> satisfies <span class=\"m\">A\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">A\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\4\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">A</span> and compute <span class=\"m\">A\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\-3\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">A\\hat\\imath</span> is the first column and <span class=\"m\">A\\hat\\jmath</span> the second, so <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 2\\\\-1 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>. By linearity <span class=\"m\">A(2, -3) = 2(3, -1) - 3(2, 4) = (6 - 6, -2 - 12) = (0, -14)</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A dense (fully connected) layer computes <span class=\"m\">y = Wx + b</span>. With 784 inputs and 128 outputs, <span class=\"m\">W</span> is <span class=\"m\">128 \\times 784</span>: 100,352 learned numbers. Each output neuron is one row of <span class=\"m\">W</span> dotted with the input (row view). A dataset with <span class=\"m\">n</span> examples and <span class=\"m\">d</span> features is an <span class=\"m\">n\\times d</span> matrix <span class=\"m\">X</span>, and a linear model's predictions for all examples at once are <span class=\"m\">Xw</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box code\"><div class=\"box-label\">In code</div><div class=\"box-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">import numpy as np\nA = np.array([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\nx = np.array([5, 6])\nA @ x          # array([17, 39])\nA.shape        # (2, 2)\n</code></pre></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Nutrition labels work like a matrix: rows are nutrients, columns are foods, and multiplying by the servings you ate gives your total intake. Factories use the same idea to turn a production plan into a list of required parts.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Compute <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix} 2 &amp; 0 \\\\ 1 &amp; 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{bmatrix}</span>. Give the second entry.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Row 2 dotted with <span class=\"m\">x</span>: <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot1 + 3\\cdot2 = 7</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A <span class=\"m\">3\\times5</span> matrix times a vector gives a vector of what length?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>One output entry per row.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\\\ 2 &amp; 5 &amp; 7 \\end{bmatrix}</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">A_{23}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Row 2, column 3.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the column view, <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a combination of the columns of <span class=\"m\">A</span> weighted by the entries of <span class=\"m\">x</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a combination of the rows of <span class=\"m\">x</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the dot product of <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">x</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">Ax = x_1a_1 + \\dots + x_na_n</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the first entry of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; -1 \\\\ 2 &amp; 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\\\ 5 \\end{bmatrix}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3 - 5 = -2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Can a <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> matrix multiply a 2-vector?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The number of columns (3) must equal the vector's length.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "transformations": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-think-of-matrices-as-motion\" data-h=\"3\">Why think of matrices as motion?</h3>\n<p>You now know how to compute <span class=\"m\">Ax</span>. But staring at a grid of numbers gives little feeling for what the matrix does. The breakthrough idea is to think of <span class=\"m\">A</span> as a <strong>function</strong> that moves every point of space to a new point. Some matrices rotate the plane, some stretch it, some shear it sideways, some flip it like a mirror, and some flatten it onto a line. Once you can see a matrix as a motion, determinants become \"how much area changes\", inverses become \"the motion that undoes it\", and eigenvectors become \"the directions that don't turn\". This picture is the heart of linear algebra.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine drawing a picture on a sheet of graph paper made of stretchy rubber. Now grab the sheet and deform it: stretch it wider, tilt it, slide the top edge sideways while holding the bottom still. There are many ways to deform rubber, but a <strong>linear</strong> transformation only allows the tidy ones. The grid lines must stay straight (no bending), stay parallel to each other, stay evenly spaced, and the origin must stay pinned in place. Every matrix is exactly one such tidy deformation, and every tidy deformation is exactly one matrix.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-linear-means\" data-h=\"3\">What \"linear\" means</h3>\n<p>A transformation <span class=\"m\">T</span> is <strong>linear</strong> if it respects addition and scaling:\n<span class=\"mb\">T(u + v) = T(u) + T(v), \\qquad T(cv) = c\\,T(v)</span>\nIn words: transforming a sum is the same as summing the transformed vectors, and transforming a stretched vector is the same as stretching the transformed one. Geometrically, this is the rubber-sheet rule: lines stay lines, parallel stays parallel, even spacing stays even, and <span class=\"m\">T(0) = 0</span> because <span class=\"m\">T(0) = T(0\\cdot v) = 0\\cdot T(v) = 0</span>.</p>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"m\">T(x, y) = (2x, x + y)</span> is linear. But <span class=\"m\">T(x, y) = (x + 1, y)</span> is not, since it moves the origin to <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>. And <span class=\"m\">T(x, y) = (x^2, y)</span> is not, since <span class=\"m\">T(2, 0) = (4, 0) \\ne 2T(1, 0) = (2, 0)</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Linear transformation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A function <span class=\"m\">T</span> from vectors to vectors with <span class=\"m\">T(u + v) = T(u) + T(v)</span> and <span class=\"m\">T(cv) = cT(v)</span>. It keeps the origin fixed and maps straight, evenly spaced grid lines to straight, evenly spaced grid lines.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-key-fact-watch-where-the-basis-vectors-go\" data-h=\"3\">The key fact: watch where the basis vectors go</h3>\n<p>Here is the idea that makes everything easy. Every vector in the plane can be written as <span class=\"m\">x\\hat\\imath + y\\hat\\jmath</span>. If <span class=\"m\">T</span> is linear, then\n<span class=\"mb\">T(x\\hat\\imath + y\\hat\\jmath) = x\\,T(\\hat\\imath) + y\\,T(\\hat\\jmath)</span>\nSo once you know where the two basis vectors land, you know where <strong>every</strong> vector lands. A linear transformation of the plane is completely described by just four numbers: the two coordinates of <span class=\"m\">T(\\hat\\imath)</span> and the two of <span class=\"m\">T(\\hat\\jmath)</span>.</p>\n<p>Put those landing spots in the columns of a matrix, and the formula above is exactly the column view of matrix-vector multiplication. <strong>The columns of a matrix are where the basis vectors land.</strong> For example, if a transformation sends <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> to <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> to <span class=\"m\">(-1, 2)</span>, its matrix is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. To find where <span class=\"m\">(3, 2)</span> goes: <span class=\"m\">3(1, 1) + 2(-1, 2) = (1, 7)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-gallery-of-transformations\" data-h=\"3\">A gallery of transformations</h3>\n<p><strong>Scaling.</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 0.5\\end{bmatrix}</span> sends <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> to <span class=\"m\">(2, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> to <span class=\"m\">(0, 0.5)</span>: it stretches horizontally by 2 and squashes vertically by half.</p>\n<p><strong>Rotation.</strong> To rotate by angle <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> counterclockwise, <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath = (1, 0)</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">(\\cos\\theta, \\sin\\theta)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath = (0, 1)</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">(-\\sin\\theta, \\cos\\theta)</span>. Those are the columns:\n<span class=\"mb\">R_\\theta = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos\\theta &amp; -\\sin\\theta\\\\ \\sin\\theta &amp; \\cos\\theta\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">90°</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>, which sends <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Shear.</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; k\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> keeps <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> fixed and pushes <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> sideways to <span class=\"m\">(k, 1)</span>. Squares become slanted parallelograms, like a deck of cards pushed sideways.</p>\n<p><strong>Reflection.</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}</span> flips across the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis: <span class=\"m\">(x, y) \\to (x, -y)</span>. Swapping the columns of the identity, <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>, reflects across the line <span class=\"m\">y = x</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Projection.</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span> sends <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> to zero, so <span class=\"m\">(x, y) \\to (x, 0)</span>: it flattens the whole plane onto the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis. Information is lost, and there is no way to undo it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>The reason four numbers suffice is linearity plus the basis. Since every vector is a unique combination of <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span>, and <span class=\"m\">T</span> preserves combinations, the image of any vector is the same combination of the images. Grid lines stay parallel and evenly spaced for the same reason: the line of points <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath + t\\hat\\jmath</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">T(\\hat\\imath) + t\\,T(\\hat\\jmath)</span>, again a straight line traced at a steady rate.</p>\n<div class=\"box intuition\"><div class=\"box-label\">Intuition</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>\"Multiply by a matrix\" and \"apply a transformation\" are the same sentence. When you see <span class=\"m\">Ax</span>, picture the whole plane being stretched, rotated or sheared, with <span class=\"m\">x</span> riding along.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Writing the landing spots as rows instead of columns. <span class=\"m\">T(\\hat\\imath)</span> is the <strong>first column</strong>.</li>\n<li>Thinking a shift like <span class=\"m\">x \\mapsto x + b</span> is linear. It moves the origin, so it is <strong>affine</strong>, not linear.</li>\n<li>Assuming every transformation can be undone. Projections squash space and cannot be reversed.</li>\n<li>Rotating clockwise by mistake. The standard <span class=\"m\">R_\\theta</span> rotates counterclockwise for positive <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A matrix is a linear transformation: it moves space while keeping grid lines straight, parallel and evenly spaced and the origin fixed. Because every vector is a combination of <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span>, knowing where those two land tells you everything, and those landing spots are the matrix's columns. Rotations, scalings, shears, reflections and projections are all matrices you can now write down from a picture.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;rotate&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Pick presets and press Animate to watch the grid move from the identity to A. Drag î and ĵ to build your own matrix.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose <strong>rotate</strong> and press <strong>Animate</strong>. Every grid square turns by <span class=\"m\">45°</span> without changing shape or size.\n2. Choose <strong>scale</strong>, then <strong>reflect</strong>, then <strong>shear</strong>, animating each. Note the matrix in the panel each time and match it to the gallery in the article.\n3. Choose <strong>project</strong>. The whole grid collapses onto the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis and the panel says it is not invertible.\n4. Drag <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> to <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> to <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span>. You've built the <span class=\"m\">90°</span> rotation by hand.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the grid lines always stay straight, parallel and evenly spaced, and the origin never moves. The columns in the panel are always the tips of the pink and blue arrows: where <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> land.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">T(u + v) = T(u) + T(v), \\qquad T(cv) = cT(v)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = \\big[\\,T(\\hat\\imath)\\ \\ T(\\hat\\jmath)\\,\\big]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">R_\\theta = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos\\theta &amp; -\\sin\\theta\\\\ \\sin\\theta &amp; \\cos\\theta\\end{bmatrix}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Every linear transformation of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> is a matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">T:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m</span> is linear, then <span class=\"m\">T(x) = Ax</span> where <span class=\"m\">A</span> is the <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix whose <span class=\"m\">j</span>-th column is <span class=\"m\">T(e_j)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Write <span class=\"m\">x = x_1e_1 + \\dots + x_ne_n</span>. By linearity <span class=\"m\">T(x) = x_1T(e_1) + \\dots + x_nT(e_n)</span>, which is exactly <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> by the column view.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the matrix that reflects the plane across the line <span class=\"m\">y = x</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Reflecting across <span class=\"m\">y = x</span> swaps coordinates. <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath = (1, 0)</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath = (0, 1)</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li>Columns: <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">A(3, 5) = (5, 3)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Rotate the point <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> by <span class=\"m\">90°</span> counterclockwise</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">R_{90°} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">R(2, 1) = (0\\cdot2 - 1\\cdot1,\\ 1\\cdot2 + 0\\cdot1) = (-1, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)\\cdot(-1, 2) = 0</span> (perpendicular) and both have length <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt5</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Is <span class=\"m\">T(x, y) = (x + 2y,\\ 3x)</span> linear? If so, find its matrix and <span class=\"m\">T(-1, 4)</span>. Is <span class=\"m\">S(x, y) = (xy, x)</span> linear?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">T</span> is linear: each output is a fixed combination of <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span>. <span class=\"m\">T(\\hat\\imath) = (1, 3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">T(\\hat\\jmath) = (2, 0)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\3 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">T(-1, 4) = (-1 + 8, -3) = (7, -3)</span>. <span class=\"m\">S</span> is not linear: <span class=\"m\">S(2, 2) = (4, 2)</span> but <span class=\"m\">2S(1, 1) = (2, 2)</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Data augmentation rotates, scales and shears training images with <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> matrices (plus a shift). Inside a network, each linear layer transforms its input space before a nonlinearity bends it; stacking these lets networks untangle data that isn't linearly separable at first. Spatial transformer networks learn the transformation matrix itself.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Computer graphics represents every rotation, scaling and camera projection as a matrix, and GPUs apply them to millions of vertices per frame. To include shifts, graphics uses <strong>homogeneous coordinates</strong>: a 2-D point <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> becomes <span class=\"m\">(x, y, 1)</span>, so a translation becomes a <span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span> matrix.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Photo-editing tools such as \"rotate\", \"flip horizontal\", \"skew\" and \"resize\" are each a <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> matrix applied to every pixel position. A camera taking a picture of a tilted page performs a projection.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which matrix rotates the plane by <span class=\"m\">90°</span> counterclockwise?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}-1 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath = (1,0)</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">(0,1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span>. Those are the columns.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is <span class=\"m\">T(x) = x + (1, 0)</span> linear?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It moves the origin, so <span class=\"m\">T(0) \\ne 0</span>. It's affine.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A transformation sends <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> to <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> to <span class=\"m\">(0, 3)</span>. What is the second entry of <span class=\"m\">T(1, 1)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">T(1, 1) = (2, 1) + (0, 3) = (2, 4)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The matrix <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span> performs a...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">rotation</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">shear</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">projection onto the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It sends <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(x, 0)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Under any linear transformation, the origin...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">stays at the origin</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">moves to <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">can go anywhere</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">T(0) = T(0\\cdot v) = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the first entry of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\7\\end{bmatrix}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Reflection across the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis keeps <span class=\"m\">x</span> and flips <span class=\"m\">y</span>: <span class=\"m\">(4, -7)</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "matmul": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-multiply-two-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Why multiply two matrices?</h3>\n<p>In the last lesson a matrix became a motion of space. A natural question follows: what happens if you do one motion and then another? Rotate a photo, then stretch it. Shear a shape, then reflect it. The result is again a tidy, grid-preserving motion, so it must be some single matrix. <strong>Matrix multiplication</strong> is the rule that finds that single matrix. This is why matrix multiplication is defined in a way that looks strange at first: it is designed so that multiplying two matrices gives you the matrix of \"do one, then the other\". Neural networks, computer graphics and almost every numerical algorithm are long chains of matrix multiplications, so this lesson is worth learning slowly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Go back to the bakery. Matrix <span class=\"m\">B</span> turns an order of cakes and pies into amounts of eggs and sugar. Now imagine a second table, matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>, that turns amounts of eggs and sugar into a cost in dollars and a weight in grams. If you want to go straight from \"cakes and pies ordered\" to \"dollars and grams\", you could apply <span class=\"m\">B</span> and then <span class=\"m\">A</span> every time, or you could combine the two tables once into a single table <span class=\"m\">AB</span> that does the whole job. Multiplying the matrices is precomputing the combined table. Doing <span class=\"m\">B</span> first and <span class=\"m\">A</span> second is written <span class=\"m\">AB</span>, with <span class=\"m\">B</span> on the right, because <span class=\"m\">B</span> is the one that touches the vector first: <span class=\"m\">A(Bx)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"building-the-product-column-by-column\" data-h=\"3\">Building the product column by column</h3>\n<p>Here is the cleanest way to see what <span class=\"m\">AB</span> must be. Recall that the columns of a matrix are where the basis vectors land. So to find the first column of <span class=\"m\">AB</span>, ask: where does <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> end up after doing <span class=\"m\">B</span> and then <span class=\"m\">A</span>? First <span class=\"m\">B</span> sends <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> to the first column of <span class=\"m\">B</span>, call it <span class=\"m\">b_1</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">A</span> sends <span class=\"m\">b_1</span> to <span class=\"m\">Ab_1</span>. So the first column of <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is <span class=\"m\">Ab_1</span>. In general:\n<span class=\"mb\">AB = \\big[\\,Ab_1\\ \\ Ab_2\\ \\ \\cdots\\ \\ Ab_p\\,\\big]</span>\nEach column of the product is <span class=\"m\">A</span> times the matching column of <span class=\"m\">B</span>. You already know how to do matrix-vector products, so you already know how to multiply matrices.</p>\n<p>Try it with <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. The first column of <span class=\"m\">B</span> is <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span>, and <span class=\"m\">A(3, 1) = (3 + 2, 1) = (5, 1)</span>. The second column of <span class=\"m\">B</span> is <span class=\"m\">(0, 2)</span>, and <span class=\"m\">A(0, 2) = (0 + 4, 2) = (4, 2)</span>. So\n<span class=\"mb\">AB = \\begin{bmatrix}5 &amp; 4\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-row-column-rule\" data-h=\"3\">The row-column rule</h3>\n<p>For fast hand computation there is a shortcut that gives each entry directly. The entry in row <span class=\"m\">i</span>, column <span class=\"m\">j</span> of <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is the dot product of row <span class=\"m\">i</span> of <span class=\"m\">A</span> with column <span class=\"m\">j</span> of <span class=\"m\">B</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">(AB)_{ij} = \\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}</span>\nIn the example, entry <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span> is row 1 of <span class=\"m\">A</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>, dotted with column 2 of <span class=\"m\">B</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">(0, 2)</span>: <span class=\"m\">0 + 4 = 4</span>. Entry <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> is <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)\\cdot(3, 1) = 1</span>. This rule is exactly the column view written out entry by entry.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;shear&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>A shear is one matrix. Composing it with a rotation gives another single matrix: the product.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"shapes-must-match\" data-h=\"3\">Shapes must match</h3>\n<p>The dot products only make sense when a row of <span class=\"m\">A</span> is as long as a column of <span class=\"m\">B</span>. So <span class=\"m\">A</span> must have as many columns as <span class=\"m\">B</span> has rows. If <span class=\"m\">A</span> is <span class=\"m\">m \\times n</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> is <span class=\"m\">n \\times p</span>, then <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is <span class=\"m\">m \\times p</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">(m\\times n)(n\\times p) = (m\\times p)</span>\nThe inner numbers must agree and disappear; the outer numbers give the shape. A <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> times a <span class=\"m\">3\\times4</span> is <span class=\"m\">2\\times4</span>. A <span class=\"m\">3\\times2</span> times a <span class=\"m\">3\\times2</span> is undefined.</p>\n<h3 id=\"order-matters\" data-h=\"3\">Order matters</h3>\n<p>With ordinary numbers, <span class=\"m\">3\\cdot5 = 5\\cdot3</span>. With matrices this usually fails: in general <span class=\"m\">AB \\ne BA</span>. In our example, <span class=\"m\">BA = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 6\\\\1 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>, which is not <span class=\"m\">AB</span>. Geometrically this makes sense: putting on socks then shoes is different from shoes then socks. Rotating a picture by <span class=\"m\">90°</span> and then shearing it gives a different result from shearing and then rotating. Sometimes <span class=\"m\">BA</span> is not even defined when <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is, because the shapes don't match.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-that-do-hold\" data-h=\"3\">Rules that do hold</h3>\n<p>Although order matters, grouping does not: <span class=\"m\">(AB)C = A(BC)</span>. This is <strong>associativity</strong>, and it holds because both sides mean \"do <span class=\"m\">C</span>, then <span class=\"m\">B</span>, then <span class=\"m\">A</span>\". Multiplication also distributes: <span class=\"m\">A(B + C) = AB + AC</span>. The identity acts like the number 1: <span class=\"m\">AI = IA = A</span>. Powers make sense for square matrices: <span class=\"m\">A^2 = AA</span>, <span class=\"m\">A^3 = AAA</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-transpose\" data-h=\"3\">The transpose</h3>\n<p>The <strong>transpose</strong> <span class=\"m\">A^T</span> flips a matrix across its diagonal, so rows become columns: <span class=\"m\">(A^T)_{ij} = A_{ji}</span>. If <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3\\\\4 &amp; 5 &amp; 6\\end{bmatrix}</span>, then <span class=\"m\">A^T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 4\\\\2 &amp; 5\\\\3 &amp; 6\\end{bmatrix}</span>, a <span class=\"m\">3\\times2</span> matrix. The transpose of a product reverses the order: <span class=\"m\">(AB)^T = B^TA^T</span>. The transpose also lets us write dot products as matrix products: for column vectors, <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v = u^Tv</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Matrix product</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">A</span> (<span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span>) and <span class=\"m\">B</span> (<span class=\"m\">n\\times p</span>), <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is the <span class=\"m\">m\\times p</span> matrix with <span class=\"m\">(AB)_{ij} = \\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}</span>. It represents doing <span class=\"m\">B</span> first, then <span class=\"m\">A</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Transpose</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A^T</span> is <span class=\"m\">A</span> flipped across its diagonal: <span class=\"m\">(A^T)_{ij} = A_{ji}</span>. An <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix has an <span class=\"m\">n\\times m</span> transpose.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Assuming <span class=\"m\">AB = BA</span>. Always keep the order.</li>\n<li>Reading <span class=\"m\">AB</span> as \"do <span class=\"m\">A</span> first\". The matrix on the right acts first.</li>\n<li>Multiplying entry by entry. That is a different operation (the Hadamard product, written <span class=\"m\">A\\odot B</span>).</li>\n<li>Writing <span class=\"m\">(AB)^T = A^TB^T</span>. The order reverses.</li>\n<li>Forgetting to check shapes before starting.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The product <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is the single matrix for \"do <span class=\"m\">B</span>, then <span class=\"m\">A</span>\". Its columns are <span class=\"m\">A</span> times the columns of <span class=\"m\">B</span>, and its entries are rows of <span class=\"m\">A</span> dotted with columns of <span class=\"m\">B</span>. An <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix times an <span class=\"m\">n\\times p</span> matrix gives <span class=\"m\">m\\times p</span>. Multiplication is associative and distributive but not commutative, and transposing a product reverses its order.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;rotate&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Build the matrices from the article by dragging î and ĵ, and compare the results of doing them in different orders.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose <strong>rotate</strong> and note the matrix <span class=\"m\">R</span>. Then choose <strong>shear</strong> and note <span class=\"m\">S</span>.\n2. Work out <span class=\"m\">SR</span> by hand with the column rule: where does <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> go after rotating then shearing?\n3. Drag <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> to the columns of your answer and compare the grid to what you predicted.\n4. Now do <span class=\"m\">RS</span> (shear first). Drag the arrows to its columns. The picture is different.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> each product is again a single grid-preserving motion, and its columns are where <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> land after both steps. Changing the order changes the picture, which is why <span class=\"m\">AB \\ne BA</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">(AB)_{ij} = \\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">AB = [\\,Ab_1 \\ \\cdots\\ Ab_p\\,]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(m\\times n)(n\\times p) = m\\times p</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(AB)C = A(BC), \\quad A(B + C) = AB + AC, \\quad AI = IA = A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(AB)^T = B^TA^T, \\qquad u\\cdot v = u^Tv</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Transpose of a product</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any matrices with <span class=\"m\">AB</span> defined, <span class=\"m\">(AB)^T = B^TA^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Entry <span class=\"m\">(i, j)</span> of <span class=\"m\">(AB)^T</span> is entry <span class=\"m\">(j, i)</span> of <span class=\"m\">AB</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">\\sum_k A_{jk}B_{ki}</span>. Entry <span class=\"m\">(i, j)</span> of <span class=\"m\">B^TA^T</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\sum_k (B^T)_{ik}(A^T)_{kj} = \\sum_k B_{ki}A_{jk}</span>. These are the same sum.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 4\\\\2 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Entry (1,1): <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)\\cdot(1, 2) = 4</span>.</li>\n<li>Entry (1,2): <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)\\cdot(4, 0) = 8</span>.</li>\n<li>Entry (2,1): <span class=\"m\">(0, 3)\\cdot(1, 2) = 6</span>.</li>\n<li>Entry (2,2): <span class=\"m\">(0, 3)\\cdot(4, 0) = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>Result: <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 8\\\\6 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the single matrix for \"rotate <span class=\"m\">90°</span> counterclockwise, then shear with <span class=\"m\">S = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>\"</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Rotation first, so the product is <span class=\"m\">SR</span> with <span class=\"m\">R = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li>Column 1: <span class=\"m\">S(0, 1) = (1, 1)</span>. Column 2: <span class=\"m\">S(-1, 0) = (-1, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">SR = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>. In the other order, <span class=\"m\">RS = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, which is different.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Shapes: <span class=\"m\">A</span> is <span class=\"m\">3\\times2</span>, <span class=\"m\">B</span> is <span class=\"m\">2\\times5</span>, <span class=\"m\">C</span> is <span class=\"m\">5\\times1</span>. Which products exist?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">AB</span>: inner 2 and 2 match, shape <span class=\"m\">3\\times5</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">BC</span>: inner 5 and 5 match, shape <span class=\"m\">2\\times1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">ABC</span>: <span class=\"m\">3\\times1</span>. <span class=\"m\">BA</span>: inner 5 and 3 don't match, undefined.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\3 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Compute <span class=\"m\">AP</span> and <span class=\"m\">PA</span> and describe in words what multiplying by <span class=\"m\">P</span> on each side does.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">AP = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\4 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>: multiplying on the right swaps the <strong>columns</strong> of <span class=\"m\">A</span>. <span class=\"m\">PA = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 4\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>: multiplying on the left swaps the <strong>rows</strong>. Since <span class=\"m\">AP \\ne PA</span>, this is another example of non-commutativity.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A batch of <span class=\"m\">B</span> inputs is stacked as the rows of <span class=\"m\">X</span> (<span class=\"m\">B\\times d</span>), and a layer computes <span class=\"m\">XW^T + b</span> for the whole batch in one matrix multiply. A deep network without nonlinearities collapses: <span class=\"m\">W_3W_2W_1x</span> is just one matrix <span class=\"m\">W = W_3W_2W_1</span> times <span class=\"m\">x</span>, which is why activation functions are essential.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Multiplying two <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrices with the row-column rule takes <span class=\"m\">n^3</span> multiplications. GPUs and libraries such as BLAS exist to do this fast. The order of a chain matters for cost: for an <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> <span class=\"m\">A</span>, <span class=\"m\">B</span> and vector <span class=\"m\">x</span>, computing <span class=\"m\">A(Bx)</span> costs about <span class=\"m\">2n^2</span> operations, while <span class=\"m\">(AB)x</span> costs about <span class=\"m\">n^3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Animation software stacks transformations for a character's arm: shoulder rotation, elbow rotation, wrist rotation. The hand's final position is the product of those matrices applied to the hand's local coordinates.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box code\"><div class=\"box-label\">In code</div><div class=\"box-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">import numpy as np\nA = np.array([[1, 2], [0, 1]]); B = np.array([[3, 0], [1, 2]])\nA @ B        # [[5, 4], [1, 2]]\nB @ A        # [[3, 6], [1, 4]]  (different)\nA.T          # transpose\n</code></pre></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">A</span> is <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> is <span class=\"m\">3\\times4</span>, what shape is <span class=\"m\">AB</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">2\\times4</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">4\\times2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">undefined</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Inner dimensions 3 and 3 match; the outer ones give <span class=\"m\">2\\times4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Simplify <span class=\"m\">(AB)^T</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A^TB^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">B^TA^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">AB</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Transposing reverses the order.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Compute entry (2,1) of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\2 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"11\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Row 2 of <span class=\"m\">A</span> is <span class=\"m\">(2, 3)</span>, column 1 of <span class=\"m\">B</span> is <span class=\"m\">(4, 1)</span>: <span class=\"m\">8 + 3 = 11</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In <span class=\"m\">AB</span>, which matrix acts on a vector first?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">B</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(AB)x = A(Bx)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">B</span> touches <span class=\"m\">x</span> first.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For square matrices, is <span class=\"m\">AB = BA</span> always true?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Example: <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span> give different products.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many multiplications does the row-column rule use for two <span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span> matrices?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"27\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each of the 9 entries needs 3 multiplications: <span class=\"m\">3^3 = 27</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "special-matrices": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-special-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Why special matrices?</h3>\n<p>A general matrix can do almost anything, which makes it expensive to work with. But many matrices that appear in practice have a special pattern: lots of zeros in predictable places, or a mirror symmetry, or columns that are perfectly perpendicular. These patterns are not just cosmetic. A diagonal system can be solved in one step, a triangular one in a few, and an orthogonal matrix can be inverted by simply flipping it. Recognising these shapes saves enormous amounts of computation, and nearly every algorithm later in this course works by turning a general matrix into a product of special ones.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Think of a team of workers. In a general team, everybody's output depends on everybody else's: hard to manage. In a <strong>diagonal</strong> team, each worker handles one job alone, so you can scale each person's output independently. In a <strong>triangular</strong> team, worker 1 works alone, worker 2 depends only on worker 1, worker 3 on workers 1 and 2, and so on, so you can sort everything out in order. An <strong>orthogonal</strong> team is a perfectly rotated copy of the original, with no stretching at all. Each pattern is a kind of order that makes life easier.</p>\n<h3 id=\"diagonal-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Diagonal matrices</h3>\n<p>A <strong>diagonal matrix</strong> has nonzero entries only on the main diagonal. It scales each axis by its own factor:\n<span class=\"mb\">D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad D\\begin{bmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2x\\\\3y\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nEverything about diagonal matrices is easy. Multiplying two diagonal matrices multiplies their diagonals. Powers are powers of the diagonal: <span class=\"m\">D^3 = \\begin{bmatrix}8 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 27\\end{bmatrix}</span>. The inverse divides: <span class=\"m\">D^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1/2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 1/3\\end{bmatrix}</span>, provided no diagonal entry is zero. The identity is the diagonal matrix with all ones.</p>\n<h3 id=\"triangular-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Triangular matrices</h3>\n<p>An <strong>upper triangular</strong> matrix has zeros below the diagonal, and a <strong>lower triangular</strong> matrix has zeros above it. Systems with triangular matrices are solved by substitution, one unknown at a time. For\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_1\\\\x_2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\5\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nrow 1 says <span class=\"m\">2x_1 = 4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 2</span>. Row 2 says <span class=\"m\">x_1 + 3x_2 = 5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">3x_2 = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1</span>. This is <strong>forward substitution</strong>; with an upper triangular matrix you start from the bottom row instead, which is <strong>back substitution</strong>. Elimination, in a later lesson, is exactly the process of turning a general system into a triangular one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"symmetric-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Symmetric matrices</h3>\n<p>A matrix is <strong>symmetric</strong> if it equals its own transpose, <span class=\"m\">A = A^T</span>, which means <span class=\"m\">A_{ij} = A_{ji}</span>: the entries mirror across the diagonal. For example <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> is symmetric and <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> is not. Symmetric matrices appear everywhere in data: the table of distances between cities is symmetric (A to B equals B to A), and so is a covariance matrix. A very useful fact is that <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span> is symmetric for any matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>, even a rectangular one. Symmetric matrices have the nicest possible eigenvalues and eigenvectors, as you'll see later.</p>\n<h3 id=\"orthogonal-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Orthogonal matrices</h3>\n<p>A square matrix <span class=\"m\">Q</span> is <strong>orthogonal</strong> if its columns are orthonormal: each has length 1 and every pair is perpendicular. In matrix form this is <span class=\"m\">Q^TQ = I</span>, because entry <span class=\"m\">(i, j)</span> of <span class=\"m\">Q^TQ</span> is the dot product of column <span class=\"m\">i</span> with column <span class=\"m\">j</span>. That gives a wonderful shortcut: <span class=\"m\">Q^{-1} = Q^T</span>. Rotations and reflections are orthogonal. For example the <span class=\"m\">45°</span> rotation\n<span class=\"mb\">Q = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nhas columns of length <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{1/2 + 1/2} = 1</span> whose dot product is <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(-1 + 1) = 0</span>. Orthogonal matrices preserve lengths and angles: <span class=\"m\">\\|Qx\\| = \\|x\\|</span>. They move things rigidly without distortion, which makes them numerically stable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"permutation-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Permutation matrices</h3>\n<p>A <strong>permutation matrix</strong> is the identity with its rows reordered. Multiplying by it reorders the entries of a vector: <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}(a, b) = (b, a)</span>. Permutation matrices are orthogonal, so undoing a shuffle is just the transpose.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-trace\" data-h=\"3\">The trace</h3>\n<p>The <strong>trace</strong> of a square matrix is the sum of its diagonal entries: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix} = 5</span>. It is a single number summarising the matrix, and it has the surprising property <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(AB) = \\operatorname{tr}(BA)</span> even though <span class=\"m\">AB \\ne BA</span>. Later you'll learn that the trace equals the sum of the eigenvalues.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Symmetric matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A square matrix with <span class=\"m\">A^T = A</span>, i.e. <span class=\"m\">A_{ij} = A_{ji}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Orthogonal matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A square matrix <span class=\"m\">Q</span> with orthonormal columns, equivalently <span class=\"m\">Q^TQ = I</span>, so <span class=\"m\">Q^{-1} = Q^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Trace</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(A) = \\sum_i A_{ii}</span>, the sum of the diagonal entries of a square matrix.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Calling a matrix orthogonal when its columns are perpendicular but not unit length. They must be orthonormal.</li>\n<li>Confusing symmetric (<span class=\"m\">A = A^T</span>) with orthogonal (<span class=\"m\">A^TA = I</span>). The identity is both, but most matrices are neither.</li>\n<li>Inverting a diagonal matrix with a zero on the diagonal. It has no inverse.</li>\n<li>Taking the trace of a non-square matrix. It's only defined for square ones.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"combining-special-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Combining special matrices</h3>\n<p>Special matrices behave well together. The product of two diagonal matrices is diagonal, the product of two upper triangular matrices is upper triangular, and the product of two orthogonal matrices is orthogonal, since <span class=\"m\">(Q_1Q_2)^T(Q_1Q_2) = Q_2^TQ_1^TQ_1Q_2 = I</span>. The sum of two symmetric matrices is symmetric. These closure facts are why algorithms can safely build long chains of special matrices: rotating twice is still a rotation, and eliminating in several steps still gives a triangular result.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Diagonal matrices scale each axis independently and are trivial to multiply, power and invert. Triangular matrices give systems that are solved by substitution. Symmetric matrices mirror across the diagonal, and <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span> is always symmetric. Orthogonal matrices are rigid motions with <span class=\"m\">Q^{-1} = Q^T</span> and preserve length. Permutations reorder entries, and the trace sums the diagonal.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;symmetric&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Try the presets and identify which special type each matrix is.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose <strong>scale</strong>. The matrix is diagonal: the grid is stretched along the axes without turning.\n2. Choose <strong>symmetric</strong>. The off-diagonal entries match, and the grid stretches along two perpendicular (non-axis) directions.\n3. Choose <strong>rotate</strong> and <strong>reflect</strong>. Both are orthogonal: the yellow square keeps its size and shape.\n4. Choose <strong>shear</strong>. It is upper triangular but not orthogonal: the square changes shape.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> orthogonal matrices never distort the square, matching <span class=\"m\">\\|Qx\\| = \\|x\\|</span> from the article. Diagonal matrices only stretch along the axes.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">D^k = \\operatorname{diag}(d_1^k, \\dots, d_n^k), \\qquad D^{-1} = \\operatorname{diag}(1/d_1, \\dots, 1/d_n)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A \\text{ symmetric} \\iff A = A^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Q^TQ = I \\iff Q^{-1} = Q^T, \\qquad \\|Qx\\| = \\|x\\|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{tr}(A) = \\sum_i A_{ii}, \\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(AB) = \\operatorname{tr}(BA)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\"><span class=\"m\">A^TA</span> is always symmetric</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>, the <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrix <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span> is symmetric.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">(A^TA)^T = A^T(A^T)^T = A^TA</span>, using <span class=\"m\">(XY)^T = Y^TX^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Orthogonal matrices preserve length</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">Q^TQ = I</span> then <span class=\"m\">\\|Qx\\| = \\|x\\|</span> for every <span class=\"m\">x</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\|Qx\\|^2 = (Qx)^T(Qx) = x^TQ^TQx = x^Tx = \\|x\\|^2</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Solve the upper triangular system <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 3 &amp; 3\\\\0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}x = \\begin{bmatrix}8\\\\9\\\\4\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Bottom row: <span class=\"m\">2x_3 = 4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x_3 = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Middle row: <span class=\"m\">3x_2 + 3(2) = 9</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1</span>.</li>\n<li>Top row: <span class=\"m\">x_1 + 2(1) + 2 = 8</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 4</span>. Solution <span class=\"m\">(4, 1, 2)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Check that <span class=\"m\">Q = \\begin{bmatrix}0.6 &amp; -0.8\\\\0.8 &amp; 0.6\\end{bmatrix}</span> is orthogonal and find <span class=\"m\">Q^{-1}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Column lengths: <span class=\"m\">0.36 + 0.64 = 1</span> for both.</li>\n<li>Dot product of columns: <span class=\"m\">0.6(-0.8) + 0.8(0.6) = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">Q</span> is orthogonal and <span class=\"m\">Q^{-1} = Q^T = \\begin{bmatrix}0.6 &amp; 0.8\\\\-0.8 &amp; 0.6\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\0 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Compute <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>, verify it is symmetric, and find its trace.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">A^T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Entry (1,1) is column 1 dotted with itself: <span class=\"m\">1 + 0 + 1 = 2</span>. Entry (1,2) is column 1 dotted with column 2: <span class=\"m\">2 + 0 + 0 = 2</span>. Entry (2,2): <span class=\"m\">4 + 1 + 0 = 5</span>. So <span class=\"m\">A^TA = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>, which is symmetric, with trace <span class=\"m\">7</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Covariance matrices and Hessians (second-derivative matrices) are symmetric. Diagonal matrices appear in optimisers such as Adam, which scales each parameter's step by its own factor. Orthogonal weight initialisation keeps signal lengths stable through deep networks, and the trace shows up in regularisers like <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(W^TW) = \\|W\\|_F^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Sparse and structured matrices are stored without their zeros: a diagonal <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrix needs <span class=\"m\">n</span> numbers, not <span class=\"m\">n^2</span>. Triangular solves cost <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span> instead of <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span>. Permutation matrices describe row swaps inside library routines like LU with pivoting.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A road-distance table between cities is symmetric. A GPS turning a map to face north applies an orthogonal rotation that keeps every distance on the map correct.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">An orthogonal matrix <span class=\"m\">Q</span> satisfies...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">Q^{-1} = Q^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">Q = Q^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\det Q = 0</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">Q^TQ = I</span> means <span class=\"m\">Q^T</span> undoes <span class=\"m\">Q</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> symmetric?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The off-diagonal entries are equal, so <span class=\"m\">A = A^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>, what is the bottom-right entry of <span class=\"m\">D^2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Square each diagonal entry: <span class=\"m\">3^2 = 9</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the trace of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 7\\\\-1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">4 + 2 = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Solve <span class=\"m\">2x_1 = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_1 + 4x_2 = 11</span> for <span class=\"m\">x_2</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Forward substitution: <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 3</span>, then <span class=\"m\">4x_2 = 8</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">Q</span> is orthogonal and <span class=\"m\">\\|x\\| = 5</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">\\|Qx\\|</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Orthogonal matrices preserve length.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "elimination": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-learn-elimination\" data-h=\"3\">Why learn elimination?</h3>\n<p>Systems of linear equations are everywhere: balancing chemical reactions, fitting models to data, computing currents in circuits, finding where two lines meet. With two unknowns you can often guess or substitute, but with ten or ten thousand unknowns you need a method that always works and that a computer can follow blindly. That method is <strong>Gaussian elimination</strong>. It is over two thousand years old (a version appears in an ancient Chinese text), and it is still the algorithm running inside <code>numpy.linalg.solve</code>. Every idea later in this unit, from inverses to rank to determinants, can be computed with it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine buying fruit at a market. Two apples and one banana cost 5 dollars. One apple and three bananas cost 5 dollars as well. What does each fruit cost? You might think: \"if I double the second purchase I get 2 apples and 6 bananas for 10 dollars; compared with the first purchase, the extra 5 bananas cost an extra 5 dollars, so a banana costs 1 dollar and an apple costs 2.\" That reasoning is elimination: you combined equations to make one unknown disappear, solved for the other, and then went back to finish. The algorithm just makes this tidy and systematic.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-setup-an-augmented-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">The setup: an augmented matrix</h3>\n<p>A system like\n<span class=\"mb\">x + y + z = 6, \\qquad 2x + 3y + z = 11, \\qquad x - y + 2z = 5</span>\nis <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> with <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 3 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; -1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">b = (6, 11, 5)</span>. Since the variable names never change, we drop them and write the <strong>augmented matrix</strong> <span class=\"m\">[A \\mid b]</span>, with the right-hand side as an extra column:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 6\\\\2 &amp; 3 &amp; 1 &amp; 11\\\\1 &amp; -1 &amp; 2 &amp; 5\\end{array}\\right]</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-allowed-moves\" data-h=\"3\">The three allowed moves</h3>\n<p>Elimination uses three <strong>row operations</strong>, none of which changes the solutions: swap two rows (reorder the equations); multiply a row by a nonzero number (scale an equation); and add a multiple of one row to another (combine equations). The third move is the workhorse. Each is reversible, which is why the solution set stays the same.</p>\n<h3 id=\"forward-elimination-making-zeros-below-the-pivots\" data-h=\"3\">Forward elimination: making zeros below the pivots</h3>\n<p>The goal is to reach <strong>upper triangular</strong> (staircase) form. The first nonzero entry we use in each row is called a <strong>pivot</strong>. Here the first pivot is the 1 in the top-left corner. To clear the entries below it: replace row 2 by row 2 minus 2 times row 1, giving <span class=\"m\">(0, 1, -1 \\mid -1)</span>, and replace row 3 by row 3 minus row 1, giving <span class=\"m\">(0, -2, 1 \\mid -1)</span>.</p>\n<p>The second pivot is the 1 in row 2, column 2. Clear below it: row 3 plus 2 times row 2 gives <span class=\"m\">(0, 0, -1 \\mid -3)</span>. The system is now\n<span class=\"mb\">\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 &amp; 6\\\\0 &amp; 1 &amp; -1 &amp; -1\\\\0 &amp; 0 &amp; -1 &amp; -3\\end{array}\\right]</span>\nThe pivots are <span class=\"m\">1, 1, -1</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"back-substitution\" data-h=\"3\">Back substitution</h3>\n<p>Now solve from the bottom up. The last row says <span class=\"m\">-z = -3</span>, so <span class=\"m\">z = 3</span>. The middle row says <span class=\"m\">y - z = -1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">y = 2</span>. The top row says <span class=\"m\">x + y + z = 6</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>. The solution is <span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 3)</span>. Always check in an original equation: <span class=\"m\">2(1) + 3(2) + 3 = 11</span>. Correct.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>Each row operation produces an equivalent system: any solution of the old equations solves the new ones, and because each move can be undone, the reverse is true too. Elimination never changes the answer; it only rewrites the question until the answer can be read off. Geometrically, with two unknowns each equation is a line and the solution is where they cross. Row operations replace the lines with different lines through the same crossing point.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"system2\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Two equations in two unknowns are two lines. Elimination replaces them with simpler lines through the same intersection.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"three-possible-outcomes\" data-h=\"3\">Three possible outcomes</h3>\n<p>A linear system has exactly one solution, no solution, or infinitely many. Elimination tells you which. If every column gets a pivot, there is <strong>one solution</strong>. If you reach a row like <span class=\"m\">0 = 1</span>, the equations contradict each other and there is <strong>no solution</strong>: for <span class=\"m\">x + y = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">2x + 2y = 5</span>, subtracting twice the first from the second gives <span class=\"m\">0 = 1</span> (two parallel lines). If you reach a row <span class=\"m\">0 = 0</span> and some column has no pivot, there are <strong>infinitely many solutions</strong>: for <span class=\"m\">x + y = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">2x + 2y = 4</span> you get <span class=\"m\">0 = 0</span>, and <span class=\"m\">y</span> is a <strong>free variable</strong>. Every choice <span class=\"m\">y = t</span> gives a solution <span class=\"m\">x = 2 - t</span> (the same line twice).</p>\n<h3 id=\"lu-factorisation\" data-h=\"3\">LU factorisation</h3>\n<p>If you record the multipliers you used, elimination factors the matrix. For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\4 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>, subtracting 2 times row 1 from row 2 gives <span class=\"m\">U = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Put the multiplier 2 below the diagonal of an identity to get <span class=\"m\">L = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">A = LU</span>: check that <span class=\"m\">LU = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\4 &amp; 2 + 3\\end{bmatrix} = A</span>. Once you have <span class=\"m\">L</span> and <span class=\"m\">U</span>, solving <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> for many different <span class=\"m\">b</span> is cheap: forward substitution with <span class=\"m\">L</span>, then back substitution with <span class=\"m\">U</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Pivot</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The leading nonzero entry used in a row during elimination. The number of pivots is the rank of the matrix.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Free variable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A variable whose column has no pivot after elimination. It can take any value, giving infinitely many solutions.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">LU factorisation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Writing <span class=\"m\">A = LU</span> with <span class=\"m\">L</span> lower triangular (the elimination multipliers, ones on the diagonal) and <span class=\"m\">U</span> upper triangular (the result of elimination).</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Changing only the left side of a row and forgetting the right-hand column.</li>\n<li>Subtracting in the wrong direction and getting sign errors. Write \"<span class=\"m\">R_2 \\to R_2 - 2R_1</span>\" every time.</li>\n<li>Dividing by a zero pivot. If a pivot position holds 0, swap in a lower row first.</li>\n<li>Treating <span class=\"m\">0 = 0</span> as \"no solution\". It means a free variable, so infinitely many solutions (if nothing contradicts).</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Write the system as an augmented matrix. Use row operations to clear entries below each pivot until the matrix is upper triangular, then back-substitute from the bottom. A row <span class=\"m\">0 = c</span> with <span class=\"m\">c \\ne 0</span> means no solution; a missing pivot means free variables and infinitely many solutions; a pivot in every column means exactly one solution. Recording the multipliers gives <span class=\"m\">A = LU</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"system2\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Two lines in the plane. Change the equations to see one crossing, parallel lines, or the same line twice.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Make the lines cross at a single point. Read off the solution: this is the \"pivot in every column\" case.\n2. Make the second line's slope equal the first's but with a different intercept. The lines are parallel: no solution, the <span class=\"m\">0 = 1</span> case.\n3. Make the second equation an exact multiple of the first. The lines coincide: infinitely many solutions, a free variable.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> these are the only three pictures possible for two lines, matching the three outcomes of elimination. Row operations change which lines you draw but never move the intersection.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Row ops: } R_i \\leftrightarrow R_j, \\quad R_i \\to cR_i\\ (c \\ne 0), \\quad R_i \\to R_i + cR_j</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = LU \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad Ly = b,\\ \\ Ux = y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Cost of elimination on } n\\times n: \\ \\approx \\tfrac23 n^3 \\text{ operations}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Number of solutions</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A linear system <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> has either no solution, exactly one solution, or infinitely many solutions.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Suppose <span class=\"m\">x_1 \\ne x_2</span> are both solutions. Then for any <span class=\"m\">t</span>, <span class=\"m\">A(x_1 + t(x_2 - x_1)) = b + t(b - b) = b</span>, so every point on the line through <span class=\"m\">x_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_2</span> is a solution. Two solutions force infinitely many.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Solve <span class=\"m\">x + 2y = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">3x - y = 1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">R_2 \\to R_2 - 3R_1</span>: <span class=\"m\">(3 - 3)x + (-1 - 6)y = 1 - 15</span>, so <span class=\"m\">-7y = -14</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">y = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Back-substitute: <span class=\"m\">x = 5 - 2(2) = 1</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">3(1) - 2 = 1</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decide the number of solutions of <span class=\"m\">x - y + z = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">2x - 2y + 3z = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">3x - 3y + 4z = 6</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">R_2 \\to R_2 - 2R_1</span>: <span class=\"m\">(0, 0, 1 \\mid 3)</span>. <span class=\"m\">R_3 \\to R_3 - 3R_1</span>: <span class=\"m\">(0, 0, 1 \\mid 3)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">R_3 \\to R_3 - R_2</span>: <span class=\"m\">(0, 0, 0 \\mid 0)</span>. No contradiction.</li>\n<li>Pivots in columns 1 and 3 only, so <span class=\"m\">y</span> is free. <span class=\"m\">z = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = 1 + y - 3 = y - 2</span>: infinitely many solutions <span class=\"m\">(t - 2, t, 3)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For which values of <span class=\"m\">k</span> does the system <span class=\"m\">x + 2y = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">2x + ky = 6</span> have exactly one solution? What happens otherwise?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">R_2 \\to R_2 - 2R_1</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(k - 4)y = 0</span>. If <span class=\"m\">k \\ne 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span>: exactly one solution. If <span class=\"m\">k = 4</span>, the row becomes <span class=\"m\">0 = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">y</span> is free and there are infinitely many solutions <span class=\"m\">x = 3 - 2t</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = t</span>. There is no value of <span class=\"m\">k</span> with no solution, because the right side <span class=\"m\">6</span> is exactly twice <span class=\"m\">3</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Solving normal equations in linear regression, Newton steps in optimisation and Gaussian-process predictions all require solving <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span>. Libraries factor <span class=\"m\">A</span> once (LU, or Cholesky for symmetric positive definite <span class=\"m\">A</span>) and reuse the factors.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Elimination on an <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> system costs about <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac23n^3</span> operations, so a <span class=\"m\">1000\\times1000</span> system needs under a billion: well under a second. Partial pivoting (swapping in the largest available pivot) keeps rounding errors small and is what LAPACK does.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Balancing a chemical equation such as <span class=\"m\">a\\,\\text{H}_2 + b\\,\\text{O}_2 \\to c\\,\\text{H}_2\\text{O}</span> is a linear system: matching hydrogen gives <span class=\"m\">2a = 2c</span> and oxygen gives <span class=\"m\">2b = c</span>. Elimination gives <span class=\"m\">a = 2, b = 1, c = 2</span>. Circuit simulators solve huge systems from Kirchhoff's laws the same way.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Solve <span class=\"m\">x + y = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">x - y = 1</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">x</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Adding the equations gives <span class=\"m\">2x = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Elimination produces the row <span class=\"m\">0\\ 0\\ \\mid\\ 4</span>. The system has...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">no solution</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">one solution</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">infinitely many solutions</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The row says <span class=\"m\">0 = 4</span>, a contradiction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">After elimination on 3 unknowns there are 2 pivots and no contradiction. How many free variables are there?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Free variables = unknowns minus pivots <span class=\"m\">= 3 - 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the system <span class=\"m\">2x + y = 7</span>, <span class=\"m\">6x + 5y = 25</span>, what multiple of row 1 is subtracted from row 2 to clear <span class=\"m\">x</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">6/2 = 3</span>. This number is also the entry of <span class=\"m\">L</span> in <span class=\"m\">A = LU</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which row operation is NOT allowed?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Swap two rows</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Add 3 times row 1 to row 2</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Multiply a row by 0</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Scaling by 0 destroys an equation and cannot be undone.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Using <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\4 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix} = LU</span>, what is the bottom-right entry of <span class=\"m\">U</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">5 - 2\\cdot1 = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "inverse": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-inverses\" data-h=\"3\">Why inverses?</h3>\n<p>With numbers, solving <span class=\"m\">3x = 12</span> is easy: multiply both sides by <span class=\"m\">1/3</span>. It would be lovely to do the same for <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span>: multiply by \"one over <span class=\"m\">A</span>\" and read off <span class=\"m\">x</span>. For matrices, the role of <span class=\"m\">1/3</span> is played by the <strong>inverse matrix</strong> <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span>, the transformation that undoes <span class=\"m\">A</span>. Inverses give a clean formula for solutions, explain when a system has a unique answer, and appear throughout theory (change of basis, diagonalisation, the normal equations). In practice computers rarely build <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span> explicitly, but understanding it is essential.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a secret code that scrambles a pair of numbers <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> into <span class=\"m\">(2x + y,\\ 5x + 3y)</span>. Your friend receives <span class=\"m\">(4, 11)</span>. To read the message she needs a decoding rule that reverses the scrambling exactly. That decoder is the inverse matrix. Not every scrambler can be reversed: a rule like <span class=\"m\">(x + 2y,\\ 2x + 4y)</span> always produces a second number that is twice the first, so different messages can produce the same code, and nothing can tell them apart. That scrambler has no inverse.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>A square matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> is <strong>invertible</strong> if there is a matrix <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span> with\n<span class=\"mb\">A^{-1}A = I \\quad\\text{and}\\quad AA^{-1} = I</span>\nGeometrically, <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span> is the motion that puts every point back where it started. The inverse of a <span class=\"m\">90°</span> counterclockwise rotation is a <span class=\"m\">90°</span> clockwise rotation. The inverse of stretching by 2 horizontally is squashing by 2 horizontally. The inverse of a shear that pushes right is the shear that pushes left. If <span class=\"m\">A</span> is invertible, then <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> has exactly one solution, <span class=\"m\">x = A^{-1}b</span>, found by multiplying both sides on the left by <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Inverse matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a square matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>, the matrix <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span> with <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}A = AA^{-1} = I</span>. It exists only when <span class=\"m\">A</span> is invertible (nonsingular).</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"when-does-an-inverse-fail-to-exist\" data-h=\"3\">When does an inverse fail to exist?</h3>\n<p>A matrix can be undone only if it never sends two different points to the same place. A projection flattens the plane onto a line, so many points land on top of each other and there is no way to separate them again. Such a matrix is called <strong>singular</strong>. For a <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> matrix, singular means the columns lie on one line (one is a multiple of the other), which flattens the plane. The matrix <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span> is singular because column 2 is twice column 1. The quick test is the <strong>determinant</strong>: for <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix}</span> it is <span class=\"m\">ad - bc</span>, and the matrix is invertible exactly when this is not zero. For <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">ad - bc = 4 - 4 = 0</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-2x2-formula\" data-h=\"3\">The 2x2 formula</h3>\n<p>For a <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> matrix with <span class=\"m\">ad - bc \\ne 0</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d &amp; -b\\\\-c &amp; a\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nIn words: swap the diagonal entries, negate the off-diagonal entries, divide by the determinant. For the secret code, <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\5 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> has <span class=\"m\">ad - bc = 6 - 5 = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; -1\\\\-5 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Decode <span class=\"m\">(4, 11)</span>: <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}(4, 11) = (12 - 11,\\ -20 + 22) = (1, 2)</span>. Check by scrambling <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>: <span class=\"m\">(2 + 2,\\ 5 + 6) = (4, 11)</span>. The message was <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"gauss-jordan-inverses-of-any-size\" data-h=\"3\">Gauss-Jordan: inverses of any size</h3>\n<p>For larger matrices, put <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">I</span> side by side as <span class=\"m\">[A \\mid I]</span> and row-reduce until the left half becomes <span class=\"m\">I</span>. The right half is then <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span>. This works because the row operations that turn <span class=\"m\">A</span> into <span class=\"m\">I</span>, applied to <span class=\"m\">I</span>, build up exactly the matrix that undoes <span class=\"m\">A</span>. For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\3 &amp; 7\\end{bmatrix}</span>: start with <span class=\"m\">\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\\\3 &amp; 7 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\\end{array}\\right]</span>. <span class=\"m\">R_2 \\to R_2 - 3R_1</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(0, 1 \\mid -3, 1)</span>. <span class=\"m\">R_1 \\to R_1 - 2R_2</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(1, 0 \\mid 7, -2)</span>. So <span class=\"m\">A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 &amp; -2\\\\-3 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, which agrees with the formula since <span class=\"m\">ad - bc = 1</span>. If the left half develops a row of zeros, <span class=\"m\">A</span> is singular and there is no inverse.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-for-inverses\" data-h=\"3\">Rules for inverses</h3>\n<p>The inverse of a product reverses the order: <span class=\"m\">(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}</span>. This is the socks-and-shoes rule again: to undo \"socks then shoes\", you remove shoes first. Also <span class=\"m\">(A^{-1})^{-1} = A</span>, <span class=\"m\">(A^T)^{-1} = (A^{-1})^T</span>, and for orthogonal matrices <span class=\"m\">Q^{-1} = Q^T</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-invertible-matrix-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">The invertible matrix theorem</h3>\n<p>For a square <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrix, all of the following say the same thing: <span class=\"m\">A</span> is invertible; <span class=\"m\">\\det A \\ne 0</span>; <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> has exactly one solution for every <span class=\"m\">b</span>; <span class=\"m\">Ax = 0</span> only has the solution <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span>; elimination finds <span class=\"m\">n</span> pivots; the columns are linearly independent; the columns span <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span>; and <span class=\"m\">0</span> is not an eigenvalue. If any one is true, all are true. This single theorem ties together nearly everything in the course so far.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Writing <span class=\"m\">(AB)^{-1} = A^{-1}B^{-1}</span>. The order reverses.</li>\n<li>Forgetting to divide by <span class=\"m\">ad - bc</span> in the <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> formula, or swapping the wrong entries.</li>\n<li>Trying to invert a non-square matrix. Only square matrices have two-sided inverses.</li>\n<li>Computing <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}b</span> in code instead of solving <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> directly. Solving is faster and more accurate.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"left-and-right-inverses-for-rectangular-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Left and right inverses for rectangular matrices</h3>\n<p>A non-square matrix cannot have a two-sided inverse, but it can have a one-sided one. A tall matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> with independent columns has a <strong>left inverse</strong> <span class=\"m\">(A^TA)^{-1}A^T</span>, which satisfies <span class=\"m\">(A^TA)^{-1}A^TA = I</span>. This exact expression will reappear in the least-squares lesson, where it produces the best approximate solution of an equation that has no exact one. A wide matrix with independent rows has a right inverse instead. The general tool covering every case is the pseudoinverse, built from the SVD at the end of this course.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The inverse <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span> undoes <span class=\"m\">A</span>: <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}A = I</span>. It exists exactly when <span class=\"m\">A</span> does not flatten space, which for <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> matrices means <span class=\"m\">ad - bc \\ne 0</span>. Use the swap-negate-divide formula for <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> and Gauss-Jordan on <span class=\"m\">[A \\mid I]</span> in general. Then <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> has the unique solution <span class=\"m\">x = A^{-1}b</span>, and products invert in reverse order.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;shear&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Find a matrix, then imagine the motion that puts the grid back. Try the singular preset, which cannot be undone.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <strong>shear</strong> (<span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath \\to (1, 1)</span>), work out the inverse with the formula: <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Drag <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> to <span class=\"m\">(-1, 1)</span> to see the opposite shear.\n2. Choose <strong>rotate</strong>. Its inverse is the rotation by the same angle the other way.\n3. Choose <strong>singular</strong> or <strong>project</strong>. The grid collapses to a line, and the panel says the matrix is not invertible.\n4. Drag <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> slowly toward the line through <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span>. Watch the yellow square's area shrink to zero just as invertibility is lost.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> a matrix can be undone exactly when it keeps the plane two-dimensional. The moment the columns line up, <span class=\"m\">ad - bc = 0</span> and information is lost.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">AA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d &amp; -b\\\\-c &amp; a\\end{bmatrix}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Ax = b \\Rightarrow x = A^{-1}b</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}, \\quad (A^T)^{-1} = (A^{-1})^T</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Inverse of a product</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> are invertible <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrices, then <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is invertible and <span class=\"m\">(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">(AB)(B^{-1}A^{-1}) = A(BB^{-1})A^{-1} = AIA^{-1} = AA^{-1} = I</span>, and similarly <span class=\"m\">(B^{-1}A^{-1})(AB) = I</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">The 2x2 inverse formula</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">ad - bc \\ne 0</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{ad - bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d &amp; -b\\\\-c &amp; a\\end{bmatrix}</span> is the inverse of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}d &amp; -b\\\\-c &amp; a\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}ad - bc &amp; -ab + ba\\\\cd - dc &amp; -cb + da\\end{bmatrix} = (ad - bc)I</span>. Dividing by <span class=\"m\">ad - bc</span> gives <span class=\"m\">I</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Solve <span class=\"m\">2x + y = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">5x + 3y = 11</span> using the inverse</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\5 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">ad - bc = 6 - 5 = 1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; -1\\\\-5 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">x = A^{-1}(4, 11) = (12 - 11, -20 + 22) = (1, 2)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the inverse of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2\\\\0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> by Gauss-Jordan</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Start with <span class=\"m\">[A \\mid I]</span>. Rows 2 and 3 are already identity rows.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">R_1 \\to R_1 - 2R_3</span> turns row 1 into <span class=\"m\">(1, 0, 0 \\mid 1, 0, -2)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0 &amp; -2\\\\0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">AA^{-1}</span> has row 1 equal to <span class=\"m\">(1, 0, -2 + 2) = (1, 0, 0)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Find the inverse of <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 7\\\\2 &amp; 6\\end{bmatrix}</span> and use it to solve <span class=\"m\">Ax = (1, 2)</span>. For which <span class=\"m\">t</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}t &amp; 2\\\\3 &amp; 6\\end{bmatrix}</span> singular?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">ad - bc = 24 - 14 = 10</span>, so <span class=\"m\">A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 &amp; -7\\\\-2 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">x = \\frac1{10}(6 - 14,\\ -2 + 8) = (-0.8, 0.6)</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">4(-0.8) + 7(0.6) = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">2(-0.8) + 6(0.6) = 2</span>. The second matrix is singular when <span class=\"m\">6t - 6 = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The closed-form solution of linear regression is <span class=\"m\">w = (X^TX)^{-1}X^Ty</span>, and Gaussian processes use <span class=\"m\">K^{-1}y</span>. In code you always call a solver (<code>np.linalg.solve</code>) rather than forming the inverse. Normalising flows, a type of generative model, need each layer to be invertible so that samples can be mapped back to the data.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Graphics engines invert the camera matrix to turn screen coordinates back into world coordinates, for example to figure out which object a mouse click hit. Because rotation matrices are orthogonal, their inverse is just a transpose, which is cheap.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The Hill cipher, a classical encryption method, encrypts blocks of letters by multiplying by a matrix and decrypts by multiplying by its inverse (working modulo 26). A key matrix that is singular is useless because messages can't be recovered.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">ad - bc</span> for <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3 - 2 = 1</span>, so the matrix is invertible.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the bottom-right entry of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Swap the diagonal: the inverse is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\-2 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> (divided by 1).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 6\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> invertible?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">ad - bc = 6 - 6 = 0</span>. Column 2 is three times column 1.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Simplify <span class=\"m\">(AB)^{-1}</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A^{-1}B^{-1}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">B^{-1}A^{-1}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">AB</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Undo the last step first.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the top-left entry of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/2|0.5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A diagonal matrix inverts entry by entry.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">A</span> is invertible, how many solutions does <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Exactly one: <span class=\"m\">x = A^{-1}b</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "determinants": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-determinants\" data-h=\"3\">Why determinants?</h3>\n<p>A matrix is a transformation, and some transformations stretch space while others squash it. It would be useful to have one number that says \"by how much\". That number is the <strong>determinant</strong>. It tells you the factor by which a matrix scales areas (in 2-D) or volumes (in 3-D), whether it flips space over like a mirror, and, most importantly, whether the matrix flattens space completely, which is exactly when it has no inverse. Determinants appear in the formula for eigenvalues, in the change-of-variables rule of calculus, and in probability densities used by generative models.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine printing a photo on a sheet of rubber and then stretching it with a machine. Before stretching, a little square on the photo has area 1 square centimetre. After stretching, it becomes a parallelogram. If every little square becomes a parallelogram of area 6, the whole photo has grown by a factor of 6. Because the stretch is linear, every region grows by the same factor, so a single number describes the whole machine. If the machine squashes the photo flat onto a line, every area becomes 0, and the picture can never be recovered.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-2x2-determinant\" data-h=\"3\">The 2x2 determinant</h3>\n<p>For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix}</span>, the determinant is\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det A = ad - bc</span>\nThe columns <span class=\"m\">(a, c)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(b, d)</span> are where <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> land, so the unit square (area 1) becomes the parallelogram spanned by these two columns. Its area is <span class=\"m\">|ad - bc|</span>. For example, <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span> has determinant <span class=\"m\">6 - 1 = 5</span>: the unit square becomes a parallelogram of area 5, and any shape's area is multiplied by 5. A diagonal matrix <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> has determinant 6, which makes sense: it stretches width by 2 and height by 3.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-formula-gives-the-area\" data-h=\"3\">Why the formula gives the area</h3>\n<p>Here is a picture-proof for the case where everything is positive. The parallelogram with sides <span class=\"m\">(a, c)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(b, d)</span> sits inside a rectangle of width <span class=\"m\">a + b</span> and height <span class=\"m\">c + d</span>. The leftover pieces of the rectangle are two triangles of area <span class=\"m\">\\frac12ac</span>, two triangles of area <span class=\"m\">\\frac12bd</span> and two small rectangles of area <span class=\"m\">bc</span>. Subtracting: <span class=\"m\">(a + b)(c + d) - ac - bd - 2bc = ad - bc</span>. The algebra matches the geometry.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;scale&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The unit square (yellow) becomes a parallelogram. Its area is the absolute value of the determinant.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-sign-orientation\" data-h=\"3\">The sign: orientation</h3>\n<p>The determinant can be negative. A negative sign means the transformation flips orientation, like looking in a mirror: <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> swap their clockwise order. The reflection <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}</span> has determinant <span class=\"m\">-1</span>: areas are unchanged but space is flipped. Rotations always have determinant <span class=\"m\">+1</span> because they neither stretch nor flip: <span class=\"m\">\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"zero-determinant-flattening\" data-h=\"3\">Zero determinant: flattening</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">\\det A = 0</span>, the unit square becomes a parallelogram with no area, so the columns lie on a single line and the plane is crushed onto a line or a point. Information is lost and <span class=\"m\">A</span> cannot be inverted. This gives the most-used test in linear algebra: <strong><span class=\"m\">A</span> is invertible if and only if <span class=\"m\">\\det A \\ne 0</span></strong>. For example <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 4\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span> has determinant <span class=\"m\">4 - 4 = 0</span>, and indeed column 2 is twice column 1.</p>\n<h3 id=\"determinants-of-3x3-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Determinants of 3x3 matrices</h3>\n<p>In 3-D the determinant is the signed volume of the box (parallelepiped) spanned by the three columns. To compute it, expand along the first row, using <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> determinants of what remains after crossing out each entry's row and column, with signs <span class=\"m\">+, -, +</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b &amp; c\\\\d &amp; e &amp; f\\\\g &amp; h &amp; i\\end{bmatrix} = a(ei - fh) - b(di - fg) + c(dh - eg)</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 3 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>: <span class=\"m\">2(6 - 1) - 1(2 - 0) + 0 = 10 - 2 = 8</span>. This is called <strong>cofactor expansion</strong>, and it works along any row or column with the checkerboard of signs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"properties-that-make-life-easy\" data-h=\"3\">Properties that make life easy</h3>\n<p>The determinant of a triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal entries, so <span class=\"m\">\\det\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 5 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 3 &amp; 7\\\\0 &amp; 0 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix} = 24</span>. This gives a fast method for big matrices: eliminate to triangular form, keeping track of row operations. Adding a multiple of one row to another does not change the determinant; swapping two rows flips its sign; scaling a row by <span class=\"m\">c</span> scales the determinant by <span class=\"m\">c</span>. Most important of all, determinants multiply:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(AB) = \\det A\\,\\det B</span>\nwhich makes sense, since doing <span class=\"m\">B</span> scales areas by <span class=\"m\">\\det B</span> and then <span class=\"m\">A</span> scales them by <span class=\"m\">\\det A</span>. It follows that <span class=\"m\">\\det(A^{-1}) = 1/\\det A</span>. Also <span class=\"m\">\\det(A^T) = \\det A</span>, and for an <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrix, <span class=\"m\">\\det(cA) = c^n\\det A</span>, because each of the <span class=\"m\">n</span> directions gets scaled by <span class=\"m\">c</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Determinant</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A number <span class=\"m\">\\det A</span> attached to a square matrix. <span class=\"m\">|\\det A|</span> is the factor by which <span class=\"m\">A</span> scales area or volume; its sign says whether orientation is flipped; <span class=\"m\">\\det A = 0</span> means <span class=\"m\">A</span> is singular.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Singular matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A square matrix with determinant 0. It flattens space into a lower dimension and has no inverse.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Computing <span class=\"m\">ad + bc</span> or <span class=\"m\">ab - cd</span>. It is \"main diagonal minus off-diagonal\": <span class=\"m\">ad - bc</span>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the alternating signs in cofactor expansion.</li>\n<li>Writing <span class=\"m\">\\det(A + B) = \\det A + \\det B</span>. This is false in general; only products behave nicely.</li>\n<li>Writing <span class=\"m\">\\det(2A) = 2\\det A</span> for a <span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span> matrix. It is <span class=\"m\">2^3\\det A = 8\\det A</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The determinant measures how a matrix scales area or volume: <span class=\"m\">\\det\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix} = ad - bc</span>. A negative determinant means a flip; zero means space is flattened and the matrix has no inverse. Triangular matrices have determinant equal to the product of the diagonal, determinants multiply, and <span class=\"m\">\\det(A^{-1}) = 1/\\det A</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;scale&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The yellow square shows the image of the unit square. Its area is the absolute value of det A, shown in the panel.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start with <strong>scale</strong>. Check that the panel's determinant equals the product of the two stretch factors.\n2. Choose <strong>rotate</strong>. The determinant is 1: the square turns but keeps its area.\n3. Choose <strong>reflect</strong>. The determinant is <span class=\"m\">-1</span>: same area, flipped orientation.\n4. Drag <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> onto the line through <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span>, or choose <strong>singular</strong>. The square collapses and the determinant becomes 0.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the size of the yellow region is always <span class=\"m\">|\\det A|</span>, and the determinant changes sign at the exact moment the two columns pass through each other, matching the orientation discussion in the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\det\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix} = ad - bc</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(AB) = \\det A\\det B, \\quad \\det(A^{-1}) = \\frac{1}{\\det A}, \\quad \\det(A^T) = \\det A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(cA) = c^n\\det A \\ (n\\times n), \\qquad \\det(\\text{triangular}) = \\prod_i A_{ii}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A \\text{ invertible} \\iff \\det A \\ne 0</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Determinant of an inverse</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A</span> is invertible, then <span class=\"m\">\\det(A^{-1}) = 1/\\det A</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">AA^{-1} = I</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\det A\\det(A^{-1}) = \\det I = 1</span> by the product rule. Since the product is 1, <span class=\"m\">\\det A \\ne 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\det(A^{-1}) = 1/\\det A</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the area of the parallelogram with sides <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Put the sides in the columns: <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\det A = 3\\cdot2 - 1\\cdot1 = 5</span>.</li>\n<li>Area <span class=\"m\">= |5| = 5</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute <span class=\"m\">\\det\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3\\\\0 &amp; 4 &amp; 5\\\\1 &amp; 0 &amp; 6\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Expand along row 1: <span class=\"m\">1(4\\cdot6 - 5\\cdot0) - 2(0\\cdot6 - 5\\cdot1) + 3(0\\cdot0 - 4\\cdot1)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">= 24 - 2(-5) + 3(-4) = 24 + 10 - 12</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">= 22</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> are <span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\det A = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\det B = -2</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">\\det(AB)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\det(2A)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\det(A^{-1})</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\det(A^TB)</span>. Is <span class=\"m\">AB</span> invertible?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">\\det(AB) = 3(-2) = -6</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\det(2A) = 2^3\\cdot3 = 24</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\det(A^{-1}) = 1/3</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\det(A^TB) = \\det A\\det B = -6</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">\\det(AB) = -6 \\ne 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is invertible.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Normalising flows compute probability densities with the change-of-variables formula, which divides by <span class=\"m\">|\\det J|</span>, the determinant of a Jacobian matrix. The determinant of a covariance matrix appears in the Gaussian density <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2\\pi)^n\\det\\Sigma}}e^{-\\frac12 x^T\\Sigma^{-1}x}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\det\\Sigma</span> measures how spread out the data is.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Computing a determinant by cofactor expansion takes <span class=\"m\">n!</span> steps, hopeless for <span class=\"m\">n = 20</span>. Libraries use LU factorisation instead: <span class=\"m\">\\det A = \\pm\\prod U_{ii}</span>, which costs <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span>. Computational geometry uses the sign of a <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> determinant to test whether three points turn left or right.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>When you zoom a map by a factor of 2 in both directions, every region's area grows by <span class=\"m\">\\det\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix} = 4</span>. Surveyors compute land areas from corner coordinates with the shoelace formula, which is a sum of <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> determinants.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Compute <span class=\"m\">\\det\\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 2\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">4\\cdot3 - 2\\cdot1 = 10</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the determinant of an upper triangular matrix with diagonal 2, 3, 4?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"24\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The product of the diagonal entries.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">\\det A = 0</span>, then <span class=\"m\">A</span>...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">has no inverse</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">is the identity</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">is orthogonal</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It flattens space, so it cannot be undone.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For the <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> identity scaled by 3, what is <span class=\"m\">\\det(3I)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3^2\\cdot1 = 9</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Swapping two rows of a matrix with determinant 5 gives determinant...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A row swap flips the sign.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A transformation has determinant <span class=\"m\">-2</span>. A region of area 3 is mapped to a region of what area?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Area scales by <span class=\"m\">|\\det A| = 2</span>. The negative sign only means orientation flips.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "rank": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-rank\" data-h=\"3\">Why rank?</h3>\n<p>A <span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span> matrix looks like it moves 3-D space to 3-D space. But it might secretly squash everything onto a plane or a line. The <strong>rank</strong> of a matrix is the true number of dimensions in its output, and it answers the big practical questions about <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span>: is there a solution for every <span class=\"m\">b</span>? If there is a solution, is it the only one? Rank also tells you how much information a dataset really contains, which is the starting point for compression, PCA and recommender systems.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a recipe app that tracks three numbers per dish: grams of flour, grams of sugar, and total grams of flour plus sugar. It seems to store three pieces of information, but the third column is always the sum of the first two, so really there are only two independent pieces. The table of dishes has rank 2, not 3. Rank counts the genuinely independent directions, ignoring the redundant ones.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-column-space-and-rank\" data-h=\"3\">The column space and rank</h3>\n<p>The <strong>column space</strong> of <span class=\"m\">A</span> is the span of its columns: every possible output <span class=\"m\">Ax</span>. The <strong>rank</strong> is its dimension, which equals the number of linearly independent columns and also the number of pivots elimination finds. For\n<span class=\"mb\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 4 &amp; 3\\\\3 &amp; 6 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>\ncolumn 2 is twice column 1, so it adds nothing new, while column 3 is not a multiple of column 1. Elimination confirms it: <span class=\"m\">R_2 - 2R_1 = (0, 0, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">R_3 - 3R_1 = (0, 0, 1)</span>, and then <span class=\"m\">R_3 - R_2 = (0, 0, 0)</span>. Two pivots, so rank 2: the outputs fill a plane inside 3-D space, not all of it. <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> is solvable only when <span class=\"m\">b</span> lies in that plane.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-null-space\" data-h=\"3\">The null space</h3>\n<p>The <strong>null space</strong> of <span class=\"m\">A</span> is the set of all inputs that <span class=\"m\">A</span> sends to zero: all <span class=\"m\">x</span> with <span class=\"m\">Ax = 0</span>. It is always a subspace (it contains <span class=\"m\">0</span>, and sums and multiples of solutions are solutions). For the matrix above, the reduced equations are <span class=\"m\">x_1 + 2x_2 + x_3 = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_3 = 0</span>. So <span class=\"m\">x_3 = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_1 = -2x_2</span>, and <span class=\"m\">x_2</span> is free. Every null-space vector is a multiple of <span class=\"m\">(-2, 1, 0)</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">A(-2, 1, 0) = (-2 + 2,\\ -4 + 4,\\ -6 + 6) = 0</span>. The null space is a line, dimension 1. Its dimension is called the <strong>nullity</strong>.</p>\n<p>The null space measures how much information <span class=\"m\">A</span> destroys. If <span class=\"m\">Ax_0 = b</span>, then <span class=\"m\">A(x_0 + n) = b</span> for every <span class=\"m\">n</span> in the null space too. So when the null space is just <span class=\"m\">\\{0\\}</span>, solutions are unique; when it is bigger, solutions come in whole families.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rank-plus-nullity\" data-h=\"3\">Rank plus nullity</h3>\n<p>In the example, rank 2 plus nullity 1 equals 3, the number of columns. This always happens. Each column either gets a pivot (contributing to the rank) or is free (contributing a null-space direction):\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{rank}(A) + \\operatorname{nullity}(A) = n \\quad (\\text{number of columns})</span>\nIt is a conservation law: input dimensions are either kept (show up in the output) or crushed (sent to zero). A <span class=\"m\">3\\times5</span> matrix with rank 3 must have a 2-dimensional null space.</p>\n<h3 id=\"row-space-and-left-null-space\" data-h=\"3\">Row space and left null space</h3>\n<p>The <strong>row space</strong> is the span of the rows, a subspace of the input space <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span>. Surprisingly, its dimension also equals the rank: row rank equals column rank. The <strong>left null space</strong> is the null space of <span class=\"m\">A^T</span>, a subspace of the output space <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^m</span>, of dimension <span class=\"m\">m - r</span>. Together these are the <strong>four fundamental subspaces</strong>. In the input space, the row space and null space are perpendicular: if <span class=\"m\">Ax = 0</span>, every row dotted with <span class=\"m\">x</span> is 0, so <span class=\"m\">x</span> is perpendicular to every row. In the output space, the column space and left null space are perpendicular in the same way.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"span\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Two vectors span a plane only when they are independent. Rank counts how many independent directions the columns really have.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"reading-off-solvability\" data-h=\"3\">Reading off solvability</h3>\n<p>For an <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix of rank <span class=\"m\">r</span>: <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> has a solution for every <span class=\"m\">b</span> exactly when <span class=\"m\">r = m</span> (the columns fill the output space). Solutions are unique, when they exist, exactly when <span class=\"m\">r = n</span> (no free variables, null space is <span class=\"m\">\\{0\\}</span>). A square matrix with <span class=\"m\">r = n</span> is invertible. A matrix with <span class=\"m\">r = \\min(m, n)</span> is called <strong>full rank</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Rank</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The dimension of the column space of <span class=\"m\">A</span>: the number of independent columns, equal to the number of pivots and to the dimension of the row space.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Null space</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>All vectors <span class=\"m\">x</span> with <span class=\"m\">Ax = 0</span>. Its dimension is the nullity.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Column space</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The span of the columns of <span class=\"m\">A</span>; the set of all outputs <span class=\"m\">Ax</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Counting nonzero columns instead of independent columns. Redundant columns don't add to the rank.</li>\n<li>Thinking the null space is empty when <span class=\"m\">Ax = 0</span> has only the trivial solution. It contains the zero vector, so it is <span class=\"m\">\\{0\\}</span> with dimension 0.</li>\n<li>Using the number of rows in rank-nullity. It's the number of <strong>columns</strong>.</li>\n<li>Mixing up which space each subspace lives in. Column space is in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^m</span>; null space is in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The rank of <span class=\"m\">A</span> is the number of independent columns, the number of pivots, and the dimension of both the column space and the row space. The null space holds the inputs sent to zero, and rank plus nullity equals the number of columns. Full row rank means every <span class=\"m\">b</span> is reachable; full column rank means solutions are unique. The four fundamental subspaces pair up as perpendicular partners.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"span\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Two vectors in the plane. Make them independent (rank 2) or line them up (rank 1) and watch the span change.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">w</span> pointing in different directions. Their span is the whole plane: the matrix <span class=\"m\">[v\\ w]</span> has rank 2.\n2. Make <span class=\"m\">w</span> a multiple of <span class=\"m\">v</span>. The span collapses to a line: rank 1.\n3. In the rank-1 case, find weights <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span> other than <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> with <span class=\"m\">av + bw = 0</span>, for example <span class=\"m\">w = 2v</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(2, -1)</span>. That is a null-space vector.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> losing a dimension of span (rank) is exactly gaining a dimension of null space, the rank-nullity balance <span class=\"m\">r + \\text{nullity} = 2</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{rank}(A) = \\dim C(A) = \\dim C(A^T) = \\#\\text{pivots}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{rank}(A) + \\dim N(A) = n</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\dim N(A^T) = m - r</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">N(A) \\perp C(A^T), \\qquad N(A^T) \\perp C(A)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Rank-nullity theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{rank}(A) + \\dim N(A) = n</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>After elimination, each of the <span class=\"m\">n</span> columns either contains a pivot or corresponds to a free variable. The pivot columns number <span class=\"m\">r = \\operatorname{rank}(A)</span>. Each free variable gives one independent null-space vector (set it to 1, the other free variables to 0, and solve for the pivots), so the nullity is <span class=\"m\">n - r</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the rank and a null-space basis for <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3\\\\2 &amp; 4 &amp; 6\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">R_2 - 2R_1 = (0, 0, 0)</span>. One pivot, so rank 1.</li>\n<li>Nullity <span class=\"m\">= 3 - 1 = 2</span>. The equation is <span class=\"m\">x_1 + 2x_2 + 3x_3 = 0</span> with <span class=\"m\">x_2, x_3</span> free.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1, x_3 = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(-2, 1, 0)</span>; <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 0, x_3 = 1</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(-3, 0, 1)</span>. These form a basis.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A <span class=\"m\">4\\times6</span> matrix has rank 4. Describe its solution sets</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Nullity <span class=\"m\">= 6 - 4 = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Rank equals the number of rows (4), so the column space is all of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^4</span>: <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> is solvable for every <span class=\"m\">b</span>.</li>\n<li>Each solution set is a 2-dimensional plane of solutions <span class=\"m\">x_0 + N(A)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1 &amp; 2\\\\1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3\\\\2 &amp; 3 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Find the rank, a basis for the null space, and decide whether <span class=\"m\">Ax = (1, 0, 0)</span> has a solution.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Column 3 = column 1 + column 2. Elimination: <span class=\"m\">R_2 - R_1 = (0, 1, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">R_3 - 2R_1 = (0, 1, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">R_3 - R_2 = 0</span>. Rank 2, nullity 1. Null space: <span class=\"m\">x_3 = t</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_2 = -t</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_1 = -x_2 - 2x_3 = -t</span>: basis <span class=\"m\">(-1, -1, 1)</span>. For <span class=\"m\">b = (1, 0, 0)</span> the same steps on the right side give <span class=\"m\">1, -1, -2</span> then <span class=\"m\">-2 - (-1) = -1</span>, so the last row reads <span class=\"m\">0 = -1</span>: no solution. Indeed every column has third entry equal to the sum of the first two, but <span class=\"m\">b</span> does not (<span class=\"m\">0 \\ne 1 + 0</span>).</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Low-rank structure is everywhere in ML. A ratings matrix of users by movies is approximately low rank (a few hidden tastes explain most ratings), which is the idea behind matrix-factorisation recommenders. LoRA fine-tunes large language models by adding a low-rank update <span class=\"m\">BA</span> to each weight matrix, training far fewer parameters. A feature matrix with rank less than the number of features means some features are redundant (multicollinearity), and <span class=\"m\">X^TX</span> is not invertible.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Error-correcting codes are built from null spaces: valid codewords <span class=\"m\">c</span> satisfy <span class=\"m\">Hc = 0</span> for a parity-check matrix <span class=\"m\">H</span>, and a nonzero <span class=\"m\">Hc</span> flags an error. Rank computations over finite fields are used in coding theory and cryptography.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A survey asks height in centimetres and height in inches. The two columns are proportional, so they contribute rank 1, not 2: the survey contains less information than it seems.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the rank of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Row 2 is twice row 1, so there is one pivot.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A <span class=\"m\">4\\times6</span> matrix has rank 4. What is the dimension of its null space?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">6 - 4 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the rank of the <span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span> identity matrix?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Three independent columns.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The null space of <span class=\"m\">A</span> is the set of <span class=\"m\">x</span> with...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">Ax = 0</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A = 0</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">x = 0</span> only</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It's every input sent to zero.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A <span class=\"m\">5\\times3</span> matrix has rank 3. Are solutions of <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> unique when they exist?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Rank equals the number of columns, so there are no free variables.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A <span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span> matrix has a null space of dimension 1. What is its rank?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3 - 1 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "change-of-basis": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-change-basis\" data-h=\"3\">Why change basis?</h3>\n<p>So far every vector has been described with the standard basis <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span>: \"go 3 right and 1 up\". But this choice is arbitrary. Sometimes a different set of directions makes a problem dramatically simpler. A matrix that looks complicated in the standard basis might just stretch along two special directions, and in a basis built from those directions it becomes a simple diagonal matrix. Changing basis is the idea behind diagonalisation, PCA, the Fourier transform and the SVD, so it is worth understanding on its own.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a city whose streets run diagonally, at <span class=\"m\">45°</span> to north. A tourist with a compass gives directions as \"3 blocks east and 1 block north\". A local would say \"2 blocks along Main Street and 1 block back along Oak Avenue\", because the streets are her natural directions. Both describe the same destination; they just use different measuring sticks. A <strong>basis</strong> is a choice of measuring sticks, and the <strong>coordinates</strong> of a point are the counts of each stick needed to reach it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"coordinates-in-a-basis\" data-h=\"3\">Coordinates in a basis</h3>\n<p>Recall that a basis of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^2</span> is any two independent vectors <span class=\"m\">b_1, b_2</span>. Every vector <span class=\"m\">v</span> can be written in exactly one way as <span class=\"m\">v = c_1b_1 + c_2b_2</span>, and the numbers <span class=\"m\">(c_1, c_2)</span> are the <strong>coordinates of <span class=\"m\">v</span> in the basis <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{B}</span></strong>, written <span class=\"m\">[v]_\\mathcal{B}</span>. Take <span class=\"m\">b_1 = (1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">b_2 = (-1, 1)</span>, and <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 1)</span>. We need <span class=\"m\">c_1(1, 1) + c_2(-1, 1) = (3, 1)</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">c_1 - c_2 = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">c_1 + c_2 = 1</span>. Adding gives <span class=\"m\">2c_1 = 4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">c_1 = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">c_2 = -1</span>. So <span class=\"m\">[v]_\\mathcal{B} = (2, -1)</span>: two of <span class=\"m\">b_1</span> and minus one of <span class=\"m\">b_2</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">2(1, 1) - (-1, 1) = (3, 1)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-change-of-basis-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">The change-of-basis matrix</h3>\n<p>Put the basis vectors in the columns of a matrix <span class=\"m\">B = [\\,b_1\\ b_2\\,]</span>. The equation <span class=\"m\">v = c_1b_1 + c_2b_2</span> is exactly <span class=\"m\">v = B[v]_\\mathcal{B}</span> by the column view. So <span class=\"m\">B</span> converts new coordinates into standard coordinates, and its inverse goes the other way:\n<span class=\"mb\">v = B\\,[v]_\\mathcal{B}, \\qquad [v]_\\mathcal{B} = B^{-1}v</span>\nIn the example, <span class=\"m\">B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> with determinant 2, so <span class=\"m\">B^{-1} = \\frac12\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\-1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}(3, 1) = \\frac12(4, -2) = (2, -1)</span>, as before. The basis must be independent for <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}</span> to exist, which is exactly why a basis needs independent vectors.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g03-basis\" data-args='{&quot;b1x&quot;: 1, &quot;b1y&quot;: 1, &quot;b2x&quot;: -1, &quot;b2y&quot;: 1, &quot;vx&quot;: 3, &quot;vy&quot;: 1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The same vector v measured with the standard grid and with the grid built from b₁ and b₂.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"orthonormal-bases-make-it-easy\" data-h=\"3\">Orthonormal bases make it easy</h3>\n<p>If the basis vectors are orthonormal (unit length, perpendicular), then <span class=\"m\">B</span> is orthogonal and <span class=\"m\">B^{-1} = B^T</span>. Each coordinate is just a dot product: <span class=\"m\">c_i = b_i\\cdot v</span>. With <span class=\"m\">q_1 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">q_2 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(-1, 1)</span>, the vector <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span> has coordinates <span class=\"m\">q_1\\cdot v = 4/\\sqrt2 = 2\\sqrt2</span> and <span class=\"m\">q_2\\cdot v = -2/\\sqrt2 = -\\sqrt2</span>. No system to solve, which is why orthonormal bases are so popular.</p>\n<h3 id=\"transformations-in-a-new-basis\" data-h=\"3\">Transformations in a new basis</h3>\n<p>Now the powerful part. Suppose a matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> describes a transformation in standard coordinates. How does the same transformation look to someone using basis <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{B}</span>? To apply it to a vector given in <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{B}</span>-coordinates, convert to standard (<span class=\"m\">B</span>), apply <span class=\"m\">A</span>, and convert back (<span class=\"m\">B^{-1}</span>):\n<span class=\"mb\">A_\\mathcal{B} = B^{-1}AB</span>\nMatrices related this way are called <strong>similar</strong>: they are the same transformation seen in different bases. Take <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span> and the basis above. <span class=\"m\">A(1, 1) = (3, 3) = 3b_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">A(-1, 1) = (-1, 1) = 1b_2</span>. So in this basis the transformation just triples the <span class=\"m\">b_1</span>-coordinate and leaves the <span class=\"m\">b_2</span>-coordinate alone: <span class=\"m\">A_\\mathcal{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>. A matrix full of numbers became a simple stretch, simply by looking along the right directions. Those special directions are eigenvectors, the subject of a later unit.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>Coordinates are not the vector; they are a description of it relative to chosen sticks. The arrow itself doesn't change when you change sticks. <span class=\"m\">B</span> is a dictionary that translates between two languages, and <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}AB</span> means \"translate into standard, act, translate back\". Similar matrices share everything that doesn't depend on the description: determinant, trace, rank and eigenvalues.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Coordinates in a basis</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a basis <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{B} = (b_1, \\dots, b_n)</span>, the unique numbers <span class=\"m\">c_i</span> with <span class=\"m\">v = \\sum c_ib_i</span>, written <span class=\"m\">[v]_\\mathcal{B}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Similar matrices</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">C</span> are similar if <span class=\"m\">C = B^{-1}AB</span> for some invertible <span class=\"m\">B</span>. They represent the same transformation in different bases.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Using <span class=\"m\">B</span> when you need <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}</span>. <span class=\"m\">B</span> turns new coordinates into standard ones; <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}</span> turns standard into new.</li>\n<li>Writing <span class=\"m\">BAB^{-1}</span> instead of <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}AB</span> for the new-basis matrix. Read right to left: convert in, act, convert out.</li>\n<li>Taking dot products for coordinates when the basis is not orthonormal. That shortcut only works for orthonormal bases.</li>\n<li>Thinking a change of basis moves the vector. It only changes the numbers used to describe it.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The coordinates of <span class=\"m\">v</span> in a basis <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{B}</span> are the weights that build <span class=\"m\">v</span> from the basis vectors. With the basis in the columns of <span class=\"m\">B</span>, <span class=\"m\">v = B[v]_\\mathcal{B}</span> and <span class=\"m\">[v]_\\mathcal{B} = B^{-1}v</span>; for an orthonormal basis, coordinates are dot products. A transformation <span class=\"m\">A</span> looks like <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}AB</span> in the new basis, and a good choice of basis can turn it into a diagonal matrix.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g03-basis\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag b₁, b₂ and v. The panel shows v's standard coordinates and its coordinates in the basis (b₁, b₂).</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Leave the default basis and read the two coordinate pairs for <span class=\"m\">v</span> in the panel. Count steps along the tilted grid to confirm them.\n2. Drag <span class=\"m\">b_1</span> to <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_2</span> to <span class=\"m\">(-1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> to <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span>. The panel should show <span class=\"m\">(2, -1)</span>, as in the article.\n3. Make <span class=\"m\">b_2</span> almost parallel to <span class=\"m\">b_1</span>. The tilted grid becomes long and thin and the coordinates blow up.\n4. Make <span class=\"m\">b_2</span> exactly parallel to <span class=\"m\">b_1</span>. The basis fails and no coordinates exist.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the purple arrow <span class=\"m\">v</span> never changes when you move the basis; only its coordinates do. The coordinates are computed with <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}</span>, which is why the basis must be independent.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">v = B[v]_\\mathcal{B}, \\qquad [v]_\\mathcal{B} = B^{-1}v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Orthonormal basis: } [v]_\\mathcal{B} = Q^Tv, \\ \\ c_i = q_i\\cdot v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A_\\mathcal{B} = B^{-1}AB</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(B^{-1}AB) = \\det A, \\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(B^{-1}AB) = \\operatorname{tr}A</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Similar matrices have the same determinant</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">C = B^{-1}AB</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\det C = \\det A</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\det C = \\det(B^{-1})\\det A\\det B = \\frac{1}{\\det B}\\det A\\det B = \\det A</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the coordinates of <span class=\"m\">v = (5, 3)</span> in the basis <span class=\"m\">b_1 = (2, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_2 = (1, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\det B = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">B^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\-1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">[v]_\\mathcal{B} = B^{-1}(5, 3) = (5 - 3, -5 + 6) = (2, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"m\">2(2, 1) + 1(1, 1) = (5, 3)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}AB</span> for <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">AB = [\\,A(1, 1)\\ \\ A(-1, 1)\\,] = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; -1\\\\3 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">B^{-1} = \\frac12\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\-1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">B^{-1}AB = \\frac12\\begin{bmatrix}6 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Trace <span class=\"m\">4</span> and determinant <span class=\"m\">3</span> match <span class=\"m\">A</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In the orthonormal basis <span class=\"m\">q_1 = (0.6, 0.8)</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_2 = (-0.8, 0.6)</span>, find the coordinates of <span class=\"m\">v = (2, 1)</span> and then rebuild <span class=\"m\">v</span> from them.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">c_1 = q_1\\cdot v = 1.2 + 0.8 = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">c_2 = q_2\\cdot v = -1.6 + 0.6 = -1</span>. Rebuild: <span class=\"m\">2(0.6, 0.8) - 1(-0.8, 0.6) = (1.2 + 0.8, 1.6 - 0.6) = (2, 1)</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>PCA rotates data into the basis of principal directions, where features are uncorrelated. Word embeddings and neural-network hidden layers are often analysed by changing basis to directions that mean something (\"gender direction\", \"sentiment direction\"). Whitening transforms data into a basis where its covariance is the identity.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The Fourier transform and JPEG compression are changes of basis: an image is rewritten in a basis of waves (cosines), where most coordinates are tiny and can be dropped. Robotics converts between the world frame, the robot frame and the camera frame with change-of-basis matrices.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Converting a colour from RGB to YCbCr (brightness plus two colour differences) is a change of basis in colour space, used by every video format so that brightness can be stored at higher detail than colour.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the basis <span class=\"m\">b_1 = (1, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_2 = (1, 1)</span>, the vector <span class=\"m\">(3, 2)</span> has which first coordinate?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">c_1(1, 0) + c_2(1, 1) = (3, 2)</span> gives <span class=\"m\">c_2 = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">c_1 = 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">To convert standard coordinates into <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{B}</span>-coordinates, multiply by...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">B</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">B^{-1}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">B^T</span> always</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">v = B[v]_\\mathcal{B}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">[v]_\\mathcal{B} = B^{-1}v</span>. (<span class=\"m\">B^T</span> works only for orthonormal bases.)</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">A</span> has determinant 7. What is <span class=\"m\">\\det(B^{-1}AB)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Similar matrices share their determinant.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In an orthonormal basis <span class=\"m\">q_1 = (0, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_2 = (1, 0)</span>, what is the first coordinate of <span class=\"m\">(4, 9)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">q_1\\cdot v = 9</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The matrix of a transformation in basis <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{B}</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">B^{-1}AB</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">BAB^{-1}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A^{-1}BA</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Convert in with <span class=\"m\">B</span>, act with <span class=\"m\">A</span>, convert out with <span class=\"m\">B^{-1}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A transformation sends <span class=\"m\">b_1 \\to 4b_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">b_2 \\to -b_2</span>. What is the top-left entry of its matrix in basis <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{B}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>In <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{B}</span> it is <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{diag}(4, -1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "projections": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-projections\" data-h=\"3\">Why projections?</h3>\n<p>Many problems in data science boil down to one question: \"what is the closest point I can actually reach?\" You have a target vector, but you're only allowed to use vectors from some line or plane, and you want the best approximation. The answer is always to drop a perpendicular, and the foot of that perpendicular is called the <strong>orthogonal projection</strong>. Projections are the geometry behind least-squares regression, Gram-Schmidt, PCA, and attention in transformers. This lesson builds them from the dot product you already know.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine the sun directly overhead at noon, and a pole leaning in a field. Its shadow on the flat ground is the projection of the pole onto the ground: each point of the pole drops straight down. The shadow is the part of the pole that lies \"along\" the ground, and the vertical distance from the tip of the pole down to the tip of the shadow is the part that the ground can't capture. Notice that the drop is perpendicular to the ground; that's what makes the shadow tip the closest point on the ground to the pole's tip.</p>\n<h3 id=\"projecting-onto-a-line\" data-h=\"3\">Projecting onto a line</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">u</span> be a nonzero vector pointing along a line through the origin, and let <span class=\"m\">v</span> be any vector. We want the point <span class=\"m\">p</span> on the line closest to <span class=\"m\">v</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">p</span> is on the line, <span class=\"m\">p = c\\,u</span> for some number <span class=\"m\">c</span>. The error <span class=\"m\">e = v - p</span> should be perpendicular to the line, so <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot(v - cu) = 0</span>. Expanding, <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v - c\\,(u\\cdot u) = 0</span>, which gives\n<span class=\"mb\">c = \\frac{u\\cdot v}{u\\cdot u}, \\qquad p = \\frac{u\\cdot v}{u\\cdot u}\\,u</span>\nTry <span class=\"m\">u = (2, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 4)</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v = 6 + 4 = 10</span> and <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot u = 4 + 1 = 5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">c = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">p = (4, 2)</span>. The error is <span class=\"m\">e = (3, 4) - (4, 2) = (-1, 2)</span>. Check perpendicularity: <span class=\"m\">e\\cdot u = -2 + 2 = 0</span>. The distance from <span class=\"m\">v</span> to the line is <span class=\"m\">\\|e\\| = \\sqrt{1 + 4} = \\sqrt5</span>.</p>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">u</span> is a unit vector, <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot u = 1</span> and the formula simplifies to <span class=\"m\">p = (u\\cdot v)\\,u</span>. The number <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v</span> is then the <strong>scalar projection</strong>: the signed length of the shadow.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"dot\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The green bar is the projection of one vector onto the other. The dashed drop line is perpendicular.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-perpendicular-means-closest\" data-h=\"3\">Why perpendicular means closest</h3>\n<p>Take any other point <span class=\"m\">q = c'u</span> on the line. The vector from <span class=\"m\">q</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span> is <span class=\"m\">(v - p) + (p - q)</span>, a sum of the error <span class=\"m\">e</span> and a vector along the line. These two pieces are perpendicular, so by Pythagoras <span class=\"m\">\\|v - q\\|^2 = \\|e\\|^2 + \\|p - q\\|^2</span>. This is smallest when <span class=\"m\">p - q = 0</span>, that is when <span class=\"m\">q = p</span>. Perpendicular dropping always gives the closest point, and this argument works in any number of dimensions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-projection-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">The projection matrix</h3>\n<p>Projection is a linear transformation, so it has a matrix. Writing <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac{u(u^Tv)}{u^Tu} = \\frac{uu^T}{u^Tu}v</span>, the matrix is\n<span class=\"mb\">P = \\frac{uu^T}{u^Tu}</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">u = (2, 1)</span>: <span class=\"m\">uu^T = \\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P = \\frac15\\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">P(3, 4) = \\frac15(12 + 8, 6 + 4) = (4, 2)</span>. Projection matrices have two signature properties. They are symmetric, <span class=\"m\">P^T = P</span>, and they are <strong>idempotent</strong>, <span class=\"m\">P^2 = P</span>: projecting something that is already on the line leaves it there. Their rank equals the dimension of the space you project onto, so this <span class=\"m\">P</span> has rank 1 and determinant 0, which makes sense: it flattens the plane onto a line.</p>\n<h3 id=\"projecting-onto-a-subspace\" data-h=\"3\">Projecting onto a subspace</h3>\n<p>To project onto a plane or any subspace, put a basis of the subspace in the columns of a matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>. The projection is <span class=\"m\">p = A\\hat x</span> for some weights <span class=\"m\">\\hat x</span>, and the error must be perpendicular to every column: <span class=\"m\">A^T(v - A\\hat x) = 0</span>. Rearranging gives the <strong>normal equations</strong> <span class=\"m\">A^TA\\hat x = A^Tv</span>, so\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat x = (A^TA)^{-1}A^Tv, \\qquad P = A(A^TA)^{-1}A^T</span>\nWhen the columns of <span class=\"m\">A</span> are orthonormal (<span class=\"m\">A = Q</span>), <span class=\"m\">Q^TQ = I</span> and everything simplifies to <span class=\"m\">P = QQ^T</span>: just add up the projections onto each basis vector. For the <span class=\"m\">xy</span>-plane in 3-D, <span class=\"m\">Q</span> has columns <span class=\"m\">(1, 0, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(0, 1, 0)</span>, and projecting <span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 3)</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 0)</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Orthogonal projection</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The point <span class=\"m\">p</span> in a subspace closest to <span class=\"m\">v</span>, characterised by the error <span class=\"m\">v - p</span> being perpendicular to the subspace.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Projection matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A matrix <span class=\"m\">P</span> with <span class=\"m\">P^2 = P</span> and <span class=\"m\">P^T = P</span>. Onto the line through <span class=\"m\">u</span>, <span class=\"m\">P = uu^T/(u^Tu)</span>; onto the column space of <span class=\"m\">A</span>, <span class=\"m\">P = A(A^TA)^{-1}A^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Forgetting to divide by <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot u</span> when <span class=\"m\">u</span> is not a unit vector.</li>\n<li>Projecting <span class=\"m\">u</span> onto <span class=\"m\">v</span> when you meant <span class=\"m\">v</span> onto <span class=\"m\">u</span>. The vector you project onto goes in the denominator and gives the direction.</li>\n<li>Thinking the error is <span class=\"m\">p - v</span> and getting signs mixed. Either way it is perpendicular, but <span class=\"m\">v = p + e</span> uses <span class=\"m\">e = v - p</span>.</li>\n<li>Using <span class=\"m\">P = QQ^T</span> when the columns are not orthonormal.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"splitting-a-vector-into-two-pieces\" data-h=\"3\">Splitting a vector into two pieces</h3>\n<p>Projection always splits <span class=\"m\">v</span> into two perpendicular pieces: <span class=\"m\">v = p + e</span>, with <span class=\"m\">p</span> along the line and <span class=\"m\">e</span> across it. Because the pieces are perpendicular, Pythagoras applies to their lengths: <span class=\"m\">\\|v\\|^2 = \\|p\\|^2 + \\|e\\|^2</span>. In the example, <span class=\"m\">\\|(3, 4)\\|^2 = 25</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\|(4, 2)\\|^2 = 20</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|(-1, 2)\\|^2 = 5</span>, and indeed <span class=\"m\">25 = 20 + 5</span>. This tells you that a projection can never be longer than the original vector, and it gives a measure of how well the line explains <span class=\"m\">v</span>: the fraction <span class=\"m\">\\|p\\|^2/\\|v\\|^2 = 20/25 = 0.8</span> of the squared length is captured by the line. The same split, applied to data, becomes the \"explained variance\" of PCA and the <span class=\"m\">R^2</span> of regression later in the course.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The orthogonal projection of <span class=\"m\">v</span> onto the line through <span class=\"m\">u</span> is <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac{u\\cdot v}{u\\cdot u}u</span>; the error <span class=\"m\">v - p</span> is perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">u</span>, and <span class=\"m\">p</span> is the closest point on the line. Projection matrices are symmetric and satisfy <span class=\"m\">P^2 = P</span>. Onto a subspace with basis in the columns of <span class=\"m\">A</span>, solve <span class=\"m\">A^TA\\hat x = A^Tv</span>; with orthonormal columns, <span class=\"m\">P = QQ^T</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"dot\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The green bar is the projection of v onto u. The dashed line from v to the bar is perpendicular to u.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">u = (2, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 4)</span>. The projection should be <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span>, twice <span class=\"m\">u</span>.\n2. Rotate <span class=\"m\">v</span> until it is perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">u</span>. The projection shrinks to zero.\n3. Point <span class=\"m\">v</span> more than <span class=\"m\">90°</span> away from <span class=\"m\">u</span>. The projection now points backwards: <span class=\"m\">c</span> is negative.\n4. Make <span class=\"m\">u</span> longer without changing its direction. The projection stays exactly the same.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the projection depends only on the direction of <span class=\"m\">u</span>, because dividing by <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot u</span> cancels its length. The drop line always meets the line through <span class=\"m\">u</span> at a right angle, which is what makes <span class=\"m\">p</span> the closest point.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">p = \\frac{u\\cdot v}{u\\cdot u}u, \\qquad e = v - p \\perp u</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P = \\frac{uu^T}{u^Tu}, \\qquad P^2 = P, \\qquad P^T = P</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A^TA\\hat x = A^Tv, \\qquad P = A(A^TA)^{-1}A^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P = QQ^T \\ \\ (\\text{orthonormal columns})</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">The projection is the closest point</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">p</span> is the orthogonal projection of <span class=\"m\">v</span> onto a subspace <span class=\"m\">S</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\|v - p\\| \\le \\|v - q\\|</span> for every <span class=\"m\">q</span> in <span class=\"m\">S</span>, with equality only when <span class=\"m\">q = p</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">v - q = (v - p) + (p - q)</span>. The first term is perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">S</span> and the second lies in <span class=\"m\">S</span>, so they are perpendicular and <span class=\"m\">\\|v - q\\|^2 = \\|v - p\\|^2 + \\|p - q\\|^2 \\ge \\|v - p\\|^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Projection matrices are idempotent</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">P = A(A^TA)^{-1}A^T</span>, <span class=\"m\">P^2 = P</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">P^2 = A(A^TA)^{-1}(A^TA)(A^TA)^{-1}A^T = A(A^TA)^{-1}A^T = P</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Project <span class=\"m\">v = (1, 2, 3)</span> onto the line through <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 1, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot u = 3</span>, so <span class=\"m\">c = 2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">p = 2(1, 1, 1) = (2, 2, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Error <span class=\"m\">e = (-1, 0, 1)</span>. Check <span class=\"m\">e\\cdot u = -1 + 0 + 1 = 0</span>. Distance to the line <span class=\"m\">= \\sqrt2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the projection matrix onto the line through <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 2)</span> and apply it to <span class=\"m\">(5, 0)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">u^Tu = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">uu^T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P = \\frac15\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(5, 0) = \\frac15(5, 10) = (1, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Check with the formula: <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v/u\\cdot u = 5/5 = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">p = u = (1, 2)</span>. Trace of <span class=\"m\">P</span> is <span class=\"m\">1</span>, its rank.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Find the distance from the point <span class=\"m\">v = (4, 0, 2)</span> to the line through the origin with direction <span class=\"m\">u = (1, 2, 2)</span>, and give the closest point on the line.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v = 4 + 0 + 4 = 8</span>, <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot u = 9</span>, so <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac89(1, 2, 2) = (\\frac89, \\frac{16}9, \\frac{16}9)</span>. Error <span class=\"m\">e = v - p = (\\frac{28}9, -\\frac{16}9, \\frac29)</span>. Check <span class=\"m\">e\\cdot u = \\frac{28 - 32 + 4}{9} = 0</span>. Distance <span class=\"m\">\\|e\\| = \\frac19\\sqrt{784 + 256 + 4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{1044}}{9} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{29}}{3} \\approx 3.59</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Linear regression's predictions <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = X\\hat w</span> are the projection of the target vector <span class=\"m\">y</span> onto the column space of the feature matrix; <span class=\"m\">P = X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T</span> is called the \"hat matrix\". PCA projects data onto the top principal directions. Attention in transformers computes dot products between queries and keys, which are (unnormalised) scalar projections.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>3-D graphics projects the world onto the 2-D screen. Collision detection projects shapes onto axes (the separating axis theorem): if the shadows on some axis don't overlap, the shapes don't touch.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A ramp problem in physics splits gravity into the part along the ramp (the projection, which makes the box slide) and the part into the ramp (balanced by the surface). The shadow of a building at noon is a projection onto the ground.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the first entry of the projection of <span class=\"m\">(4, 3)</span> onto <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">p = \\frac{4}{1}(1, 0) = (4, 0)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Projecting <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 4)</span> onto <span class=\"m\">u = (2, 1)</span> gives <span class=\"m\">p = cu</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">c</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v/u\\cdot u = 10/5 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A projection matrix satisfies...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">P^2 = P</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">P^2 = I</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">P^2 = 0</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Projecting twice is the same as projecting once.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The error <span class=\"m\">v - p</span> of an orthogonal projection onto a line through <span class=\"m\">u</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">u</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">parallel to <span class=\"m\">u</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">always zero</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>That is what \"orthogonal\" projection means.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the rank of a projection matrix onto a line in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^3</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Every output lies on the line.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the distance from <span class=\"m\">(0, 5)</span> to the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis, computed as <span class=\"m\">\\|v - p\\|</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">p = (0, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\|(0, 5)\\| = 5</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "gram-schmidt": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-orthonormal-bases\" data-h=\"3\">Why orthonormal bases?</h3>\n<p>Some bases are much nicer than others. If your basis vectors have length 1 and are all perpendicular to each other, then finding coordinates needs no equation solving, just dot products. Lengths and angles are computed exactly as in the standard basis, and the matrix holding the basis is orthogonal, so its inverse is free. Numerical algorithms love such bases because they don't amplify rounding errors. The <strong>Gram-Schmidt process</strong> is a simple recipe that takes any basis and straightens it into an orthonormal one with the same span. It leads directly to the <strong>QR factorisation</strong>, used to solve least-squares problems stably and to compute eigenvalues.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine giving directions in a city where the two main avenues meet at a sharp <span class=\"m\">30°</span> angle. Distances measured along them are confusing: walking \"1 block along each\" doesn't form a nice square. A planner decides to keep the first avenue but lay a new street exactly perpendicular to it. Now the grid is square, and every direction can be described simply. Gram-Schmidt does exactly that: keep the first direction, and from each later direction remove the parts that lean along the earlier ones, leaving only the genuinely new, perpendicular part.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-orthonormal-means\" data-h=\"3\">What orthonormal means</h3>\n<p>Vectors <span class=\"m\">q_1, \\dots, q_k</span> are <strong>orthonormal</strong> if each has length 1 and every pair is perpendicular: <span class=\"m\">q_i\\cdot q_j = 0</span> for <span class=\"m\">i \\ne j</span> and <span class=\"m\">q_i\\cdot q_i = 1</span>. In matrix form, <span class=\"m\">Q^TQ = I</span> when the <span class=\"m\">q</span>'s are the columns of <span class=\"m\">Q</span>. If they form a basis, then any vector <span class=\"m\">v</span> has coordinates <span class=\"m\">c_i = q_i\\cdot v</span>, so\n<span class=\"mb\">v = (q_1\\cdot v)q_1 + (q_2\\cdot v)q_2 + \\dots + (q_n\\cdot v)q_n</span>\nEach term is the projection of <span class=\"m\">v</span> onto one basis direction, and because the directions are perpendicular the projections don't interfere with each other. For a general basis you'd have to solve a linear system; here you just take dot products.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-gram-schmidt-process-step-by-step\" data-h=\"3\">The Gram-Schmidt process, step by step</h3>\n<p>Start with independent vectors <span class=\"m\">a_1, a_2, \\dots</span>. Step 1: normalise the first, <span class=\"m\">q_1 = a_1/\\|a_1\\|</span>. Step 2: take <span class=\"m\">a_2</span> and subtract its projection onto <span class=\"m\">q_1</span>, leaving the part perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">q_1</span>: <span class=\"m\">v_2 = a_2 - (q_1\\cdot a_2)q_1</span>. Then normalise: <span class=\"m\">q_2 = v_2/\\|v_2\\|</span>. Step 3: from <span class=\"m\">a_3</span> subtract its projections onto both <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">q_2</span>, then normalise. Continue: each new vector has the shadows on all earlier <span class=\"m\">q</span>'s removed.</p>\n<p>Let's do a 2-D example with <span class=\"m\">a_1 = (3, 4)</span> and <span class=\"m\">a_2 = (1, 0)</span>. First, <span class=\"m\">\\|a_1\\| = 5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">q_1 = (0.6, 0.8)</span>. Next, <span class=\"m\">q_1\\cdot a_2 = 0.6</span>, so <span class=\"m\">v_2 = (1, 0) - 0.6(0.6, 0.8) = (1 - 0.36,\\ -0.48) = (0.64, -0.48)</span>. Its length is <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{0.4096 + 0.2304} = \\sqrt{0.64} = 0.8</span>, so <span class=\"m\">q_2 = (0.8, -0.6)</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">q_1\\cdot q_2 = 0.48 - 0.48 = 0</span>, and both have length 1.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"gram-schmidt\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>q₂ is what remains of b after removing its shadow on q₁, then rescaled to length 1.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>At each step, the vector <span class=\"m\">v_k</span> is <span class=\"m\">a_k</span> minus its projections onto the earlier <span class=\"m\">q</span>'s. By the projection lesson, what remains is perpendicular to each of them: for example <span class=\"m\">q_1\\cdot v_2 = q_1\\cdot a_2 - (q_1\\cdot a_2)(q_1\\cdot q_1) = 0</span>. Also <span class=\"m\">v_k</span> is never zero, because <span class=\"m\">a_k</span> is independent of the earlier vectors and so cannot be fully made from them. And since each <span class=\"m\">q_k</span> is a combination of <span class=\"m\">a_1, \\dots, a_k</span> (and vice versa), the span never changes. You get an orthonormal basis for exactly the same subspace.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-qr-factorisation\" data-h=\"3\">The QR factorisation</h3>\n<p>Record what Gram-Schmidt did. Each original vector is a combination of the <span class=\"m\">q</span>'s built so far: <span class=\"m\">a_1 = \\|a_1\\|q_1</span>, and <span class=\"m\">a_2 = (q_1\\cdot a_2)q_1 + \\|v_2\\|q_2</span>. In matrix form, <span class=\"m\">A = QR</span>, where <span class=\"m\">Q</span> has orthonormal columns and <span class=\"m\">R</span> is upper triangular with entries <span class=\"m\">R_{ij} = q_i\\cdot a_j</span>. In the example, <span class=\"m\">R = \\begin{bmatrix}5 &amp; 0.6\\\\0 &amp; 0.8\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Check column 2: <span class=\"m\">0.6(0.6, 0.8) + 0.8(0.8, -0.6) = (0.36 + 0.64,\\ 0.48 - 0.48) = (1, 0) = a_2</span>. QR is how libraries solve least squares without forming <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Orthonormal set</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Vectors that each have length 1 and are pairwise perpendicular: <span class=\"m\">q_i\\cdot q_j = 0</span> for <span class=\"m\">i \\ne j</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\|q_i\\| = 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gram-Schmidt process</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An algorithm that turns independent vectors <span class=\"m\">a_1, \\dots, a_k</span> into orthonormal <span class=\"m\">q_1, \\dots, q_k</span> with the same span, by subtracting projections onto earlier <span class=\"m\">q</span>'s and normalising.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">QR factorisation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A = QR</span> with <span class=\"m\">Q</span> having orthonormal columns and <span class=\"m\">R</span> upper triangular, <span class=\"m\">R_{ij} = q_i\\cdot a_j</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Forgetting to normalise at the end of each step, which breaks later projection formulas.</li>\n<li>Subtracting the projection onto the original <span class=\"m\">a_1</span> instead of the unit <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> without dividing by <span class=\"m\">a_1\\cdot a_1</span>.</li>\n<li>Projecting only onto the previous vector instead of all earlier ones in 3 or more dimensions.</li>\n<li>Feeding in dependent vectors; some <span class=\"m\">v_k</span> becomes zero and cannot be normalised.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"when-you-have-a-full-basis\" data-h=\"3\">When you have a full basis</h3>\n<p>If you run Gram-Schmidt on <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent vectors in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span>, you get <span class=\"m\">n</span> orthonormal vectors, and the square matrix <span class=\"m\">Q</span> holding them is an orthogonal matrix. Then <span class=\"m\">Q^{-1} = Q^T</span>, every vector has coordinates <span class=\"m\">Q^Tv</span>, and lengths are preserved, <span class=\"m\">\\|Q^Tv\\| = \\|v\\|</span>. In the 2-D example, <span class=\"m\">Q = \\begin{bmatrix}0.6 &amp; 0.8\\\\0.8 &amp; -0.6\\end{bmatrix}</span>, and the vector <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> has coordinates <span class=\"m\">(0.6, 0.8)</span> in the new basis, a vector of length 1 as it should be. If you only have <span class=\"m\">k &lt; n</span> vectors, <span class=\"m\">Q</span> is tall and thin, <span class=\"m\">Q^TQ = I</span> still holds, but <span class=\"m\">QQ^T</span> is not the identity: it is the projection onto the subspace the vectors span, exactly the <span class=\"m\">P = QQ^T</span> from the projection lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>An orthonormal basis has unit, pairwise perpendicular vectors, so coordinates are dot products and <span class=\"m\">Q^TQ = I</span>. Gram-Schmidt builds one from any independent set: normalise the first vector, then from each next vector subtract its projections onto all earlier <span class=\"m\">q</span>'s and normalise. Recording the coefficients gives <span class=\"m\">A = QR</span> with <span class=\"m\">R</span> upper triangular.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gram-schmidt\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag a and b. q₁ follows a; q₂ is what's left of b after removing its shadow on q₁.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">a</span> and <span class=\"m\">b</span> nearly perpendicular. <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> points almost the same way as <span class=\"m\">b</span>, because there is little shadow to remove.\n2. Make <span class=\"m\">b</span> nearly parallel to <span class=\"m\">a</span>. The leftover part is tiny before normalising: the process is close to dividing by zero.\n3. Swap roles by dragging <span class=\"m\">b</span> to where <span class=\"m\">a</span> was. The resulting <span class=\"m\">q</span>'s change: Gram-Schmidt depends on the order of the inputs.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> are always perpendicular unit vectors spanning the same plane as <span class=\"m\">a</span> and <span class=\"m\">b</span>, exactly the \"remove the shadow, then rescale\" step of the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">q_1 = \\frac{a_1}{\\|a_1\\|}, \\qquad v_k = a_k - \\sum_{i&lt;k}(q_i\\cdot a_k)q_i, \\qquad q_k = \\frac{v_k}{\\|v_k\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">v = \\sum_i (q_i\\cdot v)\\,q_i \\ \\ (\\text{orthonormal basis})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = QR, \\qquad Q^TQ = I, \\qquad R_{ij} = q_i\\cdot a_j</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gram-Schmidt preserves span and produces orthonormal vectors</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">a_1, \\dots, a_k</span> are independent, the vectors <span class=\"m\">q_1, \\dots, q_k</span> produced by Gram-Schmidt are orthonormal and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{span}(q_1, \\dots, q_j) = \\operatorname{span}(a_1, \\dots, a_j)</span> for every <span class=\"m\">j</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By induction. Assume <span class=\"m\">q_1, \\dots, q_{k-1}</span> are orthonormal and span the same space as <span class=\"m\">a_1, \\dots, a_{k-1}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">j &lt; k</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_j\\cdot v_k = q_j\\cdot a_k - (q_j\\cdot a_k)(q_j\\cdot q_j) = 0</span>, since the other terms vanish by orthogonality. <span class=\"m\">v_k \\ne 0</span> because <span class=\"m\">a_k</span> is not in the span of the earlier vectors. Normalising gives unit length, and <span class=\"m\">v_k</span> is <span class=\"m\">a_k</span> plus a combination of earlier vectors, so the spans agree.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Apply Gram-Schmidt to <span class=\"m\">a_1 = (1, 1, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">a_2 = (1, 0, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\|a_1\\| = \\sqrt2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">q_1 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(1, 1, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">q_1\\cdot a_2 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">v_2 = (1, 0, 1) - \\frac12(1, 1, 0) = (\\frac12, -\\frac12, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\|v_2\\| = \\sqrt{\\frac14 + \\frac14 + 1} = \\sqrt{\\frac32}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">q_2 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt6}(1, -1, 2)</span>. Check <span class=\"m\">q_1\\cdot q_2 = \\frac{1 - 1 + 0}{\\sqrt{12}} = 0</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the coordinates of <span class=\"m\">v = (2, 5)</span> in the orthonormal basis <span class=\"m\">q_1 = (0.6, 0.8)</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_2 = (0.8, -0.6)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">c_1 = q_1\\cdot v = 1.2 + 4 = 5.2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">c_2 = q_2\\cdot v = 1.6 - 3 = -1.4</span>.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"m\">5.2(0.6, 0.8) - 1.4(0.8, -0.6) = (3.12 - 1.12, 4.16 + 0.84) = (2, 5)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Find the QR factorisation of <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">a_1 = (1, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\|a_1\\| = \\sqrt2</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_1 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, 1)</span>. <span class=\"m\">q_1\\cdot a_2 = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt2}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">v_2 = (1, 2) - \\frac32(1, 1) = (-\\frac12, \\frac12)</span>, with length <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{\\sqrt2}</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">q_2 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(-1, 1)</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">Q = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">R = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt2 &amp; 3/\\sqrt2\\\\0 &amp; 1/\\sqrt2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Check column 2: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{3}{\\sqrt2}q_1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}q_2 = \\frac32(1, 1) + \\frac12(-1, 1) = (1, 2)</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Least-squares solvers use QR: from <span class=\"m\">A = QR</span>, the normal equations become <span class=\"m\">R\\hat x = Q^Ty</span>, a triangular system, which is much more stable than inverting <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>. Orthogonal weight initialisation, orthogonal regularisation and some recurrent networks keep weight matrices close to orthonormal so gradients neither explode nor vanish.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The QR algorithm, the standard method for computing all eigenvalues of a matrix, repeatedly factors <span class=\"m\">A = QR</span> and forms <span class=\"m\">RQ</span>. Numerical libraries use the \"modified\" Gram-Schmidt or Householder reflections because classical Gram-Schmidt can lose orthogonality to rounding errors.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Surveyors and architects set up perpendicular reference axes on uneven sites: one baseline is chosen, then a second line is laid exactly at a right angle to it, which is one step of Gram-Schmidt in the field.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Normalise <span class=\"m\">a_1 = (0, 2)</span>. What is the second entry of <span class=\"m\">q_1</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">q_1 = (0, 2)/2 = (0, 1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With <span class=\"m\">q_1 = (1, 0)</span>, the part of <span class=\"m\">a_2 = (2, 3)</span> perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> is <span class=\"m\">(0, ?)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">a_2 - (q_1\\cdot a_2)q_1 = (2, 3) - 2(1, 0) = (0, 3)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">Q</span> has orthonormal columns, <span class=\"m\">Q^TQ</span> equals...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">I</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">0</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">Q</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Entry <span class=\"m\">(i, j)</span> is <span class=\"m\">q_i\\cdot q_j</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In <span class=\"m\">A = QR</span> from Gram-Schmidt, <span class=\"m\">R</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">upper triangular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">diagonal</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">orthogonal</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">a_k</span> only uses <span class=\"m\">q_1, \\dots, q_k</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In an orthonormal basis, the coordinate <span class=\"m\">c_i</span> of <span class=\"m\">v</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">q_i\\cdot v</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\|v\\|</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">found only by elimination</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Orthonormality makes coordinates dot products.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">a_1 = (3, 4)</span>, what is the length that <span class=\"m\">a_1</span> is divided by?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "least-squares": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-least-squares\" data-h=\"3\">Why least squares?</h3>\n<p>Real data never fits a model perfectly. Measurements have noise, and you usually have far more data points than unknowns. If you try to draw a straight line through 100 scattered points, there is no line through all of them: the system <span class=\"m\">Xw = y</span> has no solution. Instead of giving up, we ask for the line that is <strong>wrong by the least amount</strong>, measured by the sum of squared errors. This idea, invented by Gauss and Legendre around 1800 to predict the orbits of planets and comets, is now the foundation of linear regression, the first model every machine learning course teaches.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you record how many hours you study and your quiz score: 1 hour gave 1 point, 2 hours gave 2 points, 3 hours gave 2 points. You want a rule \"score <span class=\"m\">= c + m\\cdot</span> hours\" to predict future scores. No single line passes through all three points. For any candidate line, each point has a <strong>residual</strong>: actual score minus predicted score. Some residuals are positive, some negative. We square them (so negatives don't cancel positives, and big misses count extra) and add them up. The best line makes this total as small as possible.</p>\n<h3 id=\"setting-it-up-as-a-matrix-problem\" data-h=\"3\">Setting it up as a matrix problem</h3>\n<p>Each data point gives one equation <span class=\"m\">c + m x_i = y_i</span>. For the three points:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c\\\\m\\end{bmatrix} \\approx \\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nThe matrix <span class=\"m\">X</span> has a column of ones (for the intercept) and a column of <span class=\"m\">x</span>-values. Three equations, two unknowns: no exact solution. We minimise <span class=\"m\">\\|y - Xw\\|^2</span>, the squared length of the residual vector.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-geometry-projection-again\" data-h=\"3\">The geometry: projection again</h3>\n<p>Every vector <span class=\"m\">Xw</span> lies in the column space of <span class=\"m\">X</span>, a plane inside <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^3</span>. The target <span class=\"m\">y</span> sticks out of that plane. The closest point in the plane is the orthogonal projection of <span class=\"m\">y</span>, so the best <span class=\"m\">Xw</span> is the projection, and the residual <span class=\"m\">y - Xw</span> must be perpendicular to every column of <span class=\"m\">X</span>. That condition is <span class=\"m\">X^T(y - Xw) = 0</span>, giving the <strong>normal equations</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">X^TX\\,\\hat w = X^Ty</span>\nThe word \"normal\" means perpendicular, from the residual being normal to the column space.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"lstsq\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The best-fit line minimises the total area of the squared-error boxes.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"solving-the-example\" data-h=\"3\">Solving the example</h3>\n<p>Compute <span class=\"m\">X^TX = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 6\\\\6 &amp; 14\\end{bmatrix}</span> (entries: number of points 3, <span class=\"m\">\\sum x_i = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum x_i^2 = 14</span>) and <span class=\"m\">X^Ty = (5, 11)</span> (entries: <span class=\"m\">\\sum y_i = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum x_iy_i = 1 + 4 + 6 = 11</span>). The determinant is <span class=\"m\">42 - 36 = 6</span>, so\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat w = \\frac16\\begin{bmatrix}14 &amp; -6\\\\-6 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\\\11\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac16\\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2/3\\\\1/2\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nThe best line is <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac23 + \\frac12x</span>. Its predictions are <span class=\"m\">\\frac76, \\frac{10}6, \\frac{13}6</span> and the residuals are <span class=\"m\">-\\frac16, \\frac26, -\\frac16</span>. They add to zero, and <span class=\"m\">\\sum x_ir_i = -\\frac16 + \\frac46 - \\frac36 = 0</span>: the residual is perpendicular to both columns, as the geometry promised.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-squares\" data-h=\"3\">Why squares?</h3>\n<p>Squaring makes the error smooth and gives a single best answer with a clean formula. It also has a statistical meaning: if the noise is Gaussian, least squares gives the most likely line. A simple special case shows the flavour: the best constant prediction for data <span class=\"m\">2, 4, 9</span> minimises <span class=\"m\">(2 - c)^2 + (4 - c)^2 + (9 - c)^2</span>, and the answer is the mean, <span class=\"m\">c = 5</span>. The mean is the least-squares summary of a list of numbers. If you used absolute values instead, you would get the median.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-the-normal-equations-fail\" data-h=\"3\">When the normal equations fail</h3>\n<p>If the columns of <span class=\"m\">X</span> are dependent (for example two identical features), <span class=\"m\">X^TX</span> is singular and there are many equally good <span class=\"m\">w</span>'s. <strong>Ridge regression</strong> fixes this by adding a penalty <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\|w\\|^2</span>, which changes the equations to <span class=\"m\">(X^TX + \\lambda I)\\hat w = X^Ty</span>, always solvable for <span class=\"m\">\\lambda &gt; 0</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Least-squares solution</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A vector <span class=\"m\">\\hat w</span> that minimises <span class=\"m\">\\|y - Xw\\|^2</span>. It satisfies the normal equations <span class=\"m\">X^TX\\hat w = X^Ty</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Residual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The difference <span class=\"m\">r = y - X\\hat w</span> between actual and predicted values. For the least-squares fit it is perpendicular to every column of <span class=\"m\">X</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Forgetting the column of ones, which forces the line through the origin.</li>\n<li>Minimising vertical distances but describing them as perpendicular distances to the line. Least squares uses vertical (output) errors.</li>\n<li>Computing <span class=\"m\">(X^TX)^{-1}</span> explicitly in code. Use a least-squares solver; it is faster and more stable.</li>\n<li>Assuming a small total error means the model is right. Always look at the residuals for patterns.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"fitting-more-than-a-straight-line\" data-h=\"3\">Fitting more than a straight line</h3>\n<p>Least squares is far more flexible than it first appears. The model only has to be linear in the unknown weights, not in <span class=\"m\">x</span>. To fit a parabola <span class=\"m\">y = w_0 + w_1x + w_2x^2</span>, give <span class=\"m\">X</span> three columns: ones, the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-values, and the squared <span class=\"m\">x</span>-values. The normal equations are unchanged. To predict a house price from size, age and number of rooms, give <span class=\"m\">X</span> one column per feature plus the ones column. With <span class=\"m\">d</span> features, <span class=\"m\">X^TX</span> is a <span class=\"m\">(d + 1)\\times(d + 1)</span> matrix, and the same projection picture holds in higher dimensions: the prediction vector <span class=\"m\">X\\hat w</span> is the shadow of <span class=\"m\">y</span> on the space spanned by the feature columns. This is why linear regression, polynomial regression and many basis-function models all share one formula.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>When <span class=\"m\">Xw = y</span> has no exact solution, least squares picks <span class=\"m\">\\hat w</span> minimising the sum of squared residuals. Geometrically, <span class=\"m\">X\\hat w</span> is the projection of <span class=\"m\">y</span> onto the column space, so the residual is perpendicular to the columns, which gives <span class=\"m\">X^TX\\hat w = X^Ty</span>. For a line, <span class=\"m\">X</span> has a column of ones and a column of <span class=\"m\">x</span>-values. The mean is least squares with one constant, and ridge regression adds <span class=\"m\">\\lambda I</span> to keep things solvable.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"lstsq\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the data points. The pink squares are squared errors; the best-fit line minimises their total area. Try fitting by hand.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Use the controls to fit a line by hand and watch the total squared error. Then show the best fit and compare.\n2. Drag one point far away from the others. The best line swings toward it: squared errors punish big misses heavily.\n3. Line the points up exactly. The squares vanish and the residual is zero.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the best line always balances the residuals so they sum to zero, and a single outlier has a big effect because its error is squared. This is the \"squares\" in least squares from the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\hat w = \\arg\\min_w \\|y - Xw\\|^2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">X^TX\\hat w = X^Ty, \\qquad \\hat w = (X^TX)^{-1}X^Ty</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Line } y = c + mx: \\ \\begin{bmatrix}n &amp; \\sum x_i\\\\\\sum x_i &amp; \\sum x_i^2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c\\\\m\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sum y_i\\\\\\sum x_iy_i\\end{bmatrix}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Ridge: } (X^TX + \\lambda I)\\hat w = X^Ty</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">The normal equations give the minimum</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\hat w</span> minimises <span class=\"m\">\\|y - Xw\\|^2</span> if and only if <span class=\"m\">X^TX\\hat w = X^Ty</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The set of all <span class=\"m\">Xw</span> is the column space of <span class=\"m\">X</span>. By the projection theorem, the closest point to <span class=\"m\">y</span> is the <span class=\"m\">X\\hat w</span> with <span class=\"m\">y - X\\hat w</span> perpendicular to the column space, i.e. perpendicular to every column: <span class=\"m\">X^T(y - X\\hat w) = 0</span>, which rearranges to the normal equations.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Fit <span class=\"m\">y = c + mx</span> to <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(1, 3)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(2, 4)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">n = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum x = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum x^2 = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum y = 8</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum xy = 0 + 3 + 8 = 11</span>.</li>\n<li>Solve <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 3\\\\3 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c\\\\m\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}8\\\\11\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Determinant <span class=\"m\">6</span>; <span class=\"m\">c = \\frac{5\\cdot8 - 3\\cdot11}{6} = \\frac76</span>, <span class=\"m\">m = \\frac{3\\cdot11 - 3\\cdot8}{6} = \\frac32</span>.</li>\n<li>Line <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac76 + \\frac32x</span>. Residuals <span class=\"m\">-\\frac16, \\frac13, -\\frac16</span> sum to zero.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Fit a line through the origin, <span class=\"m\">y = mx</span>, to <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(2, 3)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">X</span> is the single column <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">X^TX = 1 + 4 = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">X^Ty = 1 + 6 = 7</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">m = 7/5 = 1.4</span>.</li>\n<li>Residuals <span class=\"m\">1 - 1.4 = -0.4</span> and <span class=\"m\">3 - 2.8 = 0.2</span>; check <span class=\"m\">1(-0.4) + 2(0.2) = 0</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Fit <span class=\"m\">y = c + mx</span> by least squares to the points <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(3, 2)</span>, and verify the residuals are perpendicular to both columns of <span class=\"m\">X</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">n = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum x = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum x^2 = 14</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum y = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum xy = 0 + 1 + 2 + 6 = 9</span>. Solve <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 6\\\\6 &amp; 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c\\\\m\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\9\\end{bmatrix}</span>: determinant <span class=\"m\">56 - 36 = 20</span>, <span class=\"m\">c = \\frac{14\\cdot4 - 6\\cdot9}{20} = 0.1</span>, <span class=\"m\">m = \\frac{4\\cdot9 - 6\\cdot4}{20} = 0.6</span>. Predictions <span class=\"m\">0.1, 0.7, 1.3, 1.9</span>; residuals <span class=\"m\">-0.1, 0.3, -0.3, 0.1</span>. Their sum is <span class=\"m\">0</span> (perpendicular to the ones column) and <span class=\"m\">\\sum x_ir_i = 0 + 0.3 - 0.6 + 0.3 = 0</span> (perpendicular to the <span class=\"m\">x</span> column).</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Linear regression is least squares, and it is also the last layer of many networks trained with mean squared error. The mean squared error loss <span class=\"m\">\\frac1n\\|y - Xw\\|^2</span> has gradient <span class=\"m\">-\\frac2nX^T(y - Xw)</span>; setting it to zero gives the normal equations, and gradient descent approaches the same answer iteratively. Ridge regression (<span class=\"m\">L_2</span> regularisation) is weight decay.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box code\"><div class=\"box-label\">In code</div><div class=\"box-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">import numpy as np\nX = np.array([[1, 1], [1, 2], [1, 3]]); y = np.array([1, 2, 2])\nw, *_ = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None)\nw   # array([0.6667, 0.5])\n</code></pre></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>GPS receivers hear more satellites than the minimum needed and solve an overdetermined system by least squares to estimate position. Economists fit demand curves, and engineers calibrate sensors, the same way.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The best constant fit (least squares) to the data <span class=\"m\">2, 4, 9</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The mean minimises the sum of squared deviations.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Fit <span class=\"m\">y = mx</span> through the origin to <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(2, 3)</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">m</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7/5|1.4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">m = \\sum xy/\\sum x^2 = 7/5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The normal equations are...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">X^TX\\hat w = X^Ty</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">X\\hat w = y</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">XX^T\\hat w = y</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>They come from the residual being perpendicular to the columns.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The least-squares residual is perpendicular to...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the column space of <span class=\"m\">X</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the vector <span class=\"m\">y</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the vector <span class=\"m\">\\hat w</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">X\\hat w</span> is the projection of <span class=\"m\">y</span> onto the column space.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For 5 data points and the model <span class=\"m\">y = c + mx</span>, what is the top-left entry of <span class=\"m\">X^TX</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is <span class=\"m\">\\sum 1 = n</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why are errors squared rather than just added?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">So positive and negative errors don't cancel, and the minimum has a clean formula</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Because squares are always smaller</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">To make big errors matter less</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Squaring makes every error count and gives a smooth objective. Big errors matter more, not less.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "eigen": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-eigenvectors\" data-h=\"3\">Why eigenvectors?</h3>\n<p>When a matrix acts on the plane, most vectors get knocked off their original direction: they are rotated as well as stretched. But for many matrices there are a few special directions where the vector is only stretched or shrunk, never turned. These directions are <strong>eigenvectors</strong>, and the stretch factors are <strong>eigenvalues</strong> (from the German <em>eigen</em>, meaning \"own\" or \"characteristic\"). They reveal the hidden skeleton of a matrix. Google's original PageRank was an eigenvector. PCA finds eigenvectors of a covariance matrix. The stability of a dynamical system, the vibration modes of a bridge, and the long-run behaviour of a Markov chain are all eigenvalue questions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine stretching a rubber sheet with a picture of many arrows drawn on it, pulling it along one diagonal. After the stretch, almost every arrow points somewhere new. But the arrows lying exactly along the pulling diagonal still point the same way; they just got longer. And the arrows along the other diagonal might keep their direction too, perhaps shrinking. Those unchanging directions are the eigenvectors of the stretch, and \"how much longer\" is the eigenvalue.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>A nonzero vector <span class=\"m\">v</span> is an <strong>eigenvector</strong> of a square matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> with <strong>eigenvalue</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> if\n<span class=\"mb\">Av = \\lambda v</span>\nThe output points along the same line as the input, scaled by <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>. If <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 3</span> the vector triples; if <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 0.5</span> it halves; if <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = -1</span> it flips to point backwards; if <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 0</span> it is crushed to zero. For example, with <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">A(1, 1) = (3, 3) = 3(1, 1)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue 3. And <span class=\"m\">A(1, -1) = (1, -1)</span>, eigenvalue 1. But <span class=\"m\">A(1, 0) = (2, 1)</span> is not a multiple of <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> is not an eigenvector. Any nonzero multiple of an eigenvector is also an eigenvector with the same eigenvalue, so really we find eigen-<strong>directions</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Eigenvector and eigenvalue</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A nonzero vector <span class=\"m\">v</span> with <span class=\"m\">Av = \\lambda v</span>. The scalar <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> is the eigenvalue; <span class=\"m\">v</span> only gets scaled, not turned.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"finding-eigenvalues-the-characteristic-equation\" data-h=\"3\">Finding eigenvalues: the characteristic equation</h3>\n<p>Rewrite <span class=\"m\">Av = \\lambda v</span> as <span class=\"m\">(A - \\lambda I)v = 0</span>. We need a nonzero <span class=\"m\">v</span> in the null space of <span class=\"m\">A - \\lambda I</span>, which happens exactly when that matrix is singular, that is when\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(A - \\lambda I) = 0</span>\nThis is the <strong>characteristic equation</strong>. For a <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> matrix it is a quadratic in <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>. For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det\\begin{bmatrix}4 - \\lambda &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 3 - \\lambda\\end{bmatrix} = (4 - \\lambda)(3 - \\lambda) - 2 = \\lambda^2 - 7\\lambda + 10 = (\\lambda - 5)(\\lambda - 2)</span>\nSo the eigenvalues are <span class=\"m\">5</span> and <span class=\"m\">2</span>. For any <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> matrix the characteristic polynomial is <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 - (\\operatorname{tr}A)\\lambda + \\det A</span>, here <span class=\"m\">7</span> and <span class=\"m\">10</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"finding-eigenvectors\" data-h=\"3\">Finding eigenvectors</h3>\n<p>For each eigenvalue, solve <span class=\"m\">(A - \\lambda I)v = 0</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 5</span>: <span class=\"m\">A - 5I = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; -2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Row 1 says <span class=\"m\">-v_1 + v_2 = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">v = (1, 1)</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">A(1, 1) = (5, 5)</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">A - 2I = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">2v_1 + v_2 = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = (1, -2)</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">A(1, -2) = (4 - 2, 2 - 6) = (2, -4) = 2(1, -2)</span>. The rows of <span class=\"m\">A - \\lambda I</span> are always dependent when <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> is a true eigenvalue; if you find only <span class=\"m\">v = 0</span>, recheck your eigenvalue.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;symmetric&quot;, &quot;eigen&quot;: 1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The green dashed lines are the eigenvector directions: vectors on them are stretched but not rotated.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"useful-facts-and-shortcuts\" data-h=\"3\">Useful facts and shortcuts</h3>\n<p>The eigenvalues of a triangular matrix are its diagonal entries. The sum of the eigenvalues equals the trace and their product equals the determinant (check: <span class=\"m\">5 + 2 = 7</span> and <span class=\"m\">5\\cdot2 = 10</span>). A matrix is singular exactly when <span class=\"m\">0</span> is an eigenvalue. If <span class=\"m\">Av = \\lambda v</span>, then <span class=\"m\">A^2v = \\lambda^2v</span>, and in general <span class=\"m\">A^kv = \\lambda^kv</span>; if <span class=\"m\">A</span> is invertible, <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}v = \\frac1\\lambda v</span>. So the eigenvectors of <span class=\"m\">A</span> also serve <span class=\"m\">A^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">A^{10}</span> and <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span>.</p>\n<p>Not every real matrix has real eigenvalues. The <span class=\"m\">90°</span> rotation <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span> turns every vector, so no direction survives; its characteristic equation <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 + 1 = 0</span> has complex roots <span class=\"m\">\\pm i</span>. Symmetric matrices, on the other hand, are always well behaved: their eigenvalues are real and their eigenvectors for different eigenvalues are perpendicular, as <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, -1)</span> were above. This is the <strong>spectral theorem</strong>, central to PCA.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>Eigenvectors are directions the transformation leaves on their own line. The determinant test finds the special stretch factors <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> for which <span class=\"m\">A - \\lambda I</span> collapses some direction to zero; that collapsed direction is exactly a vector that <span class=\"m\">A</span> maps to <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> times itself.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Accepting <span class=\"m\">v = 0</span> as an eigenvector. Eigenvectors must be nonzero.</li>\n<li>Subtracting <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> from every entry instead of only the diagonal. It is <span class=\"m\">A - \\lambda I</span>.</li>\n<li>Thinking eigenvectors must have length 1. Any nonzero multiple works.</li>\n<li>Assuming eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">A + B</span> or <span class=\"m\">AB</span> are sums or products of the separate eigenvalues. That is false in general.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"eigenvalues-as-a-fingerprint\" data-h=\"3\">Eigenvalues as a fingerprint</h3>\n<p>Two matrices can look completely different and still share eigenvalues. If <span class=\"m\">C = B^{-1}AB</span> is the same transformation in another basis, then <span class=\"m\">C</span> has exactly the same eigenvalues as <span class=\"m\">A</span>, because stretching factors are a property of the motion, not of the coordinates used to describe it. This is why eigenvalues are called characteristic values. They answer questions that do not depend on your choice of axes: does repeated application blow up (some <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda| &gt; 1</span>) or die out (all <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda| &lt; 1</span>)? Does the transformation flip some direction (a negative <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>)? Does it destroy information (a zero <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>)?</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>An eigenvector of <span class=\"m\">A</span> is a nonzero vector that <span class=\"m\">A</span> only scales: <span class=\"m\">Av = \\lambda v</span>. Find eigenvalues by solving <span class=\"m\">\\det(A - \\lambda I) = 0</span>, which for <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 - (\\operatorname{tr}A)\\lambda + \\det A = 0</span>, then find eigenvectors from the null space of <span class=\"m\">A - \\lambda I</span>. Eigenvalues sum to the trace and multiply to the determinant, powers of <span class=\"m\">A</span> have eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^k</span>, and symmetric matrices have real eigenvalues with perpendicular eigenvectors.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"eigen\" data-args='{&quot;a&quot;: 2, &quot;b&quot;: 1, &quot;c&quot;: 1, &quot;d&quot;: 2}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Rotate x around the circle and watch Ax. When they line up, x is an eigenvector.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Slowly rotate <span class=\"m\">x</span> around the circle. Most of the time <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> points in a different direction.\n2. Find the angle where <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> points exactly along <span class=\"m\">x</span> (<span class=\"m\">45°</span> for this matrix). Read the stretch: it should be 3.\n3. Keep rotating until they line up again at <span class=\"m\">135°</span>: the stretch is 1.\n4. Change the matrix to <span class=\"m\">a = 0, b = -1, c = 1, d = 0</span> (a rotation). You will never get <span class=\"m\">Ax</span> along <span class=\"m\">x</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the two lined-up directions are perpendicular, as the spectral theorem promises for symmetric matrices, and the stretch factors <span class=\"m\">3</span> and <span class=\"m\">1</span> add to the trace <span class=\"m\">4</span> and multiply to the determinant <span class=\"m\">3</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">Av = \\lambda v, \\quad v \\ne 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(A - \\lambda I) = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">2\\times2: \\ \\lambda^2 - (\\operatorname{tr}A)\\lambda + \\det A = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum\\lambda_i = \\operatorname{tr}A, \\qquad \\prod\\lambda_i = \\det A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A^kv = \\lambda^kv, \\qquad A^{-1}v = \\lambda^{-1}v</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Eigenvalues and the determinant</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> is an eigenvalue of <span class=\"m\">A</span> if and only if <span class=\"m\">\\det(A - \\lambda I) = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">Av = \\lambda v</span> with <span class=\"m\">v \\ne 0</span> means <span class=\"m\">(A - \\lambda I)v = 0</span> has a nonzero solution, which happens exactly when <span class=\"m\">A - \\lambda I</span> is singular, i.e. its determinant is zero.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Spectral theorem (real symmetric matrices)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A = A^T</span>, then all eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">A</span> are real, and <span class=\"m\">A</span> has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Partial proof of perpendicularity: if <span class=\"m\">Au = \\lambda u</span> and <span class=\"m\">Av = \\mu v</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda \\ne \\mu</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\lambda(u\\cdot v) = (Au)\\cdot v = u\\cdot(A^Tv) = u\\cdot(Av) = \\mu(u\\cdot v)</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">\\lambda \\ne \\mu</span>, <span class=\"m\">u\\cdot v = 0</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Trace <span class=\"m\">2</span>, determinant <span class=\"m\">1 - 4 = -3</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda - 3 = (\\lambda - 3)(\\lambda + 1) = 0</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 3</span>: <span class=\"m\">A - 3I = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; -2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">v = (1, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\lambda = -1</span>: <span class=\"m\">A + I = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">v = (1, -1)</span>. Perpendicular, as <span class=\"m\">A</span> is symmetric.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 7 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; -2 &amp; 5\\\\0 &amp; 0 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>The matrix is upper triangular, so <span class=\"m\">\\det(A - \\lambda I) = (3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda)(4 - \\lambda)</span>.</li>\n<li>Eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">3, -2, 4</span>.</li>\n<li>Check: sum <span class=\"m\">5</span> equals the trace <span class=\"m\">3 - 2 + 4</span>; product <span class=\"m\">-24</span> equals the determinant.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 &amp; -2\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Find its eigenvalues and an eigenvector for each, then give the eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">A^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Trace <span class=\"m\">7</span>, determinant <span class=\"m\">10 + 2 = 12</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 - 7\\lambda + 12 = (\\lambda - 3)(\\lambda - 4)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 3, 4</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 3</span>: <span class=\"m\">A - 3I = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; -2\\\\1 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}</span> gives <span class=\"m\">v = (1, 1)</span>; check <span class=\"m\">A(1, 1) = (3, 3)</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 4</span>: <span class=\"m\">A - 4I = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -2\\\\1 &amp; -2\\end{bmatrix}</span> gives <span class=\"m\">v = (2, 1)</span>; check <span class=\"m\">A(2, 1) = (8, 4)</span>. <span class=\"m\">A^2</span> has eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">9, 16</span>, and <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}</span> has <span class=\"m\">\\frac13, \\frac14</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>PCA's principal directions are eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, and the eigenvalues are the variances along them. Spectral clustering uses eigenvectors of a graph Laplacian. The eigenvalues of a Hessian tell an optimiser whether it is at a minimum (all positive), a maximum, or a saddle, and the ratio of largest to smallest controls how fast gradient descent converges.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>PageRank is the eigenvector with eigenvalue 1 of the web's link matrix, computed by power iteration: repeatedly multiply a vector by the matrix and it lines up with the dominant eigenvector. In RNNs, if the recurrent matrix has eigenvalues above 1 in size, signals explode over time; below 1, they vanish.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Engineers compute the eigenvalues of a structure's stiffness matrix to find its natural vibration frequencies, so a bridge or building is not driven into resonance by wind, footsteps or earthquakes.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the larger eigenvalue of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A diagonal matrix's eigenvalues are its diagonal entries.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">A(1, 2) = (3, 6)</span>. What eigenvalue does the eigenvector <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span> have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(3, 6) = 3(1, 2)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the product of the eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The product equals the determinant, <span class=\"m\">4 - 1 = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Can <span class=\"m\">v = 0</span> be an eigenvector?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>By definition eigenvectors are nonzero; <span class=\"m\">A0 = \\lambda 0</span> for every <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 2</span> is an eigenvalue of <span class=\"m\">A</span>, which number is an eigenvalue of <span class=\"m\">A^3</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"8\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">A^3v = 2^3v</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The <span class=\"m\">90°</span> rotation matrix has...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">no real eigenvalues</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">1</span> and <span class=\"m\">1</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">eigenvalue <span class=\"m\">0</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It turns every direction; <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 + 1 = 0</span> has only complex roots.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "diagonalization": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-diagonalise\" data-h=\"3\">Why diagonalise?</h3>\n<p>Diagonal matrices are the easiest matrices there are: you can power them, invert them and understand them at a glance. Most matrices are not diagonal, but many of them are diagonal <strong>in disguise</strong>: if you look at them in a basis of eigenvectors, they just stretch each axis. Diagonalisation makes this precise by factoring <span class=\"m\">A = PDP^{-1}</span>. It turns hard problems, like computing <span class=\"m\">A^{100}</span> or predicting the long-run state of a system, into easy ones. It explains the closed formula for Fibonacci numbers, the steady state of Markov chains, and the behaviour of linear dynamical systems.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a town where each day the weather is sunny or rainy. If today is sunny, tomorrow is sunny with probability 0.9; if today is rainy, tomorrow is sunny with probability 0.5. What fraction of days are sunny in the long run? Today's probabilities form a vector, and tomorrow's are that vector multiplied by a fixed matrix. A year from now means multiplying by the matrix 365 times. Doing that directly is tedious, but eigenvectors tell you the answer almost instantly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-factorisation\" data-h=\"3\">The factorisation</h3>\n<p>Suppose an <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> has <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent eigenvectors <span class=\"m\">v_1, \\dots, v_n</span> with eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_1, \\dots, \\lambda_n</span>. Put the eigenvectors in the columns of <span class=\"m\">P</span> and the eigenvalues on the diagonal of <span class=\"m\">D</span>. The equations <span class=\"m\">Av_i = \\lambda_iv_i</span>, written side by side, say <span class=\"m\">AP = PD</span>. Since the columns are independent, <span class=\"m\">P</span> is invertible, so\n<span class=\"mb\">A = PDP^{-1}</span>\nRead right to left, this says: <span class=\"m\">P^{-1}</span> converts to eigenvector coordinates, <span class=\"m\">D</span> stretches each coordinate by its eigenvalue, and <span class=\"m\">P</span> converts back. It is exactly the change-of-basis formula <span class=\"m\">D = P^{-1}AP</span> from earlier, with the eigenvector basis chosen so that the new matrix is diagonal.</p>\n<p>For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span> we found eigenvalues 3 and 1 with eigenvectors <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, -1)</span>. So <span class=\"m\">P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">P^{-1} = \\frac12\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"powers-become-easy\" data-h=\"3\">Powers become easy</h3>\n<p>The real payoff comes with powers. In <span class=\"m\">A^2 = PDP^{-1}PDP^{-1}</span>, the middle <span class=\"m\">P^{-1}P</span> cancels to <span class=\"m\">I</span>, leaving <span class=\"m\">PD^2P^{-1}</span>. In general\n<span class=\"mb\">A^k = PD^kP^{-1}</span>\nand <span class=\"m\">D^k</span> just raises each eigenvalue to the <span class=\"m\">k</span>-th power. For our matrix,\n<span class=\"mb\">A^k = \\frac12\\begin{bmatrix}3^k + 1 &amp; 3^k - 1\\\\3^k - 1 &amp; 3^k + 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nCheck with <span class=\"m\">k = 2</span>: the formula gives <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\begin{bmatrix}10 &amp; 8\\\\8 &amp; 10\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 &amp; 4\\\\4 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>, and direct multiplication gives <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}4 + 1 &amp; 2 + 2\\\\2 + 2 &amp; 1 + 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>, the same. For <span class=\"m\">k = 100</span> the formula is just as easy, while multiplying 100 times is not.</p>\n<h3 id=\"long-run-behaviour\" data-h=\"3\">Long-run behaviour</h3>\n<p>When <span class=\"m\">k</span> is large, the biggest eigenvalue dominates. If <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda_1|</span> is larger than all the others, <span class=\"m\">A^kx</span> points more and more along <span class=\"m\">v_1</span>. If every <span class=\"m\">|\\lambda| &lt; 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">A^kx \\to 0</span>. If the largest is exactly 1, <span class=\"m\">A^kx</span> settles down to a steady state along that eigenvector. In the weather example, the matrix is <span class=\"m\">M = \\begin{bmatrix}0.9 &amp; 0.5\\\\0.1 &amp; 0.5\\end{bmatrix}</span> (columns are \"from sunny\" and \"from rainy\"). Its eigenvalues are <span class=\"m\">1</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.4</span> (trace <span class=\"m\">1.4</span>, determinant <span class=\"m\">0.45 - 0.05 = 0.4</span>). For <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">(M - I)v = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">-0.1v_1 + 0.5v_2 = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">v = (5, 1)</span>. Scaling to probabilities, <span class=\"m\">(\\frac56, \\frac16)</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">0.4^k \\to 0</span>, the other part dies out, and in the long run <span class=\"m\">\\frac56</span> of days are sunny, whatever today's weather.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"eigen\" data-args='{&quot;a&quot;: 2, &quot;b&quot;: 1, &quot;c&quot;: 1, &quot;d&quot;: 2}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Along an eigenvector, A only stretches. Diagonalisation uses these directions as the new axes.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"when-diagonalisation-fails\" data-h=\"3\">When diagonalisation fails</h3>\n<p>Not every matrix can be diagonalised. The shear <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> has eigenvalue 1 twice, but <span class=\"m\">A - I = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span> has only a one-dimensional null space, so there is only one eigenvector direction, <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>. With too few eigenvectors, <span class=\"m\">P</span> is not invertible. Good news: if all <span class=\"m\">n</span> eigenvalues are distinct, the eigenvectors are automatically independent and <span class=\"m\">A</span> is diagonalisable. And every symmetric matrix is diagonalisable with an orthogonal <span class=\"m\">P</span>: <span class=\"m\">A = Q\\Lambda Q^T</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Diagonalisable matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A square matrix that can be written <span class=\"m\">A = PDP^{-1}</span> with <span class=\"m\">D</span> diagonal. The columns of <span class=\"m\">P</span> are eigenvectors and the diagonal of <span class=\"m\">D</span> holds the eigenvalues.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Markov matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A square matrix with nonnegative entries whose columns each sum to 1. It moves probability vectors to probability vectors and always has eigenvalue 1.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Putting eigenvectors in the rows of <span class=\"m\">P</span>. They go in the columns, in the same order as the eigenvalues in <span class=\"m\">D</span>.</li>\n<li>Writing <span class=\"m\">A^k = P^kD^kP^{-k}</span>. Only <span class=\"m\">D</span> gets the power.</li>\n<li>Assuming every matrix is diagonalisable. Repeated eigenvalues can leave too few eigenvectors.</li>\n<li>Forgetting to invert <span class=\"m\">P</span>; <span class=\"m\">A = PDP^T</span> only when <span class=\"m\">P</span> is orthogonal.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"seeing-diagonalisation-geometrically\" data-h=\"3\">Seeing diagonalisation geometrically</h3>\n<p>It helps to picture what <span class=\"m\">A = PDP^{-1}</span> does to a single vector. Take <span class=\"m\">x = (2, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. In eigenvector coordinates, <span class=\"m\">x = 1\\cdot(1, 1) + 1\\cdot(1, -1)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">P^{-1}x = (1, 1)</span>. Now <span class=\"m\">D</span> multiplies the first coordinate by 3 and the second by 1, giving <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span>. Converting back, <span class=\"m\">3(1, 1) + 1(1, -1) = (4, 2)</span>, and indeed <span class=\"m\">Ax = (4, 2)</span>. After <span class=\"m\">k</span> steps the coordinates are <span class=\"m\">(3^k, 1)</span>, so the <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> part grows rapidly while the <span class=\"m\">(1, -1)</span> part stays the same size. That is why <span class=\"m\">A^kx</span> swings around to point almost exactly along <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> for large <span class=\"m\">k</span>: the eigen-direction with the biggest eigenvalue wins.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">A</span> has <span class=\"m\">n</span> independent eigenvectors, then <span class=\"m\">A = PDP^{-1}</span> with eigenvectors in <span class=\"m\">P</span> and eigenvalues in <span class=\"m\">D</span>. Powers become <span class=\"m\">A^k = PD^kP^{-1}</span>, so large powers are dominated by the largest eigenvalue, which explains the long-run behaviour of Markov chains and other repeated processes. Distinct eigenvalues guarantee diagonalisability, symmetric matrices diagonalise as <span class=\"m\">Q\\Lambda Q^T</span>, and some matrices, like the shear, can't be diagonalised.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;symmetric&quot;, &quot;eigen&quot;: 1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The green dashed lines are the eigenvector axes. Press Animate: points on them only slide outward or inward.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press <strong>Animate</strong>. Watch points on the green dashed lines: they move straight out or in along their line.\n2. Drag <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> to make <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. The eigen-axes are the diagonals <span class=\"m\">y = x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = -x</span>.\n3. Choose <strong>shear</strong>. Only one eigen-direction remains: the shear cannot be diagonalised.\n4. Choose <strong>rotate</strong>. No eigen-directions at all (over the real numbers).</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> when two eigen-axes exist, the matrix is a pure stretch along them, which is what <span class=\"m\">A = PDP^{-1}</span> says. Missing axes mean the factorisation fails.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">A = PDP^{-1}, \\qquad D = P^{-1}AP</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A^k = PD^kP^{-1}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Symmetric: } A = Q\\Lambda Q^T, \\ Q^TQ = I</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Markov steady state: } Mv = v</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Distinct eigenvalues give independent eigenvectors</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Eigenvectors belonging to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent. In particular, an <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrix with <span class=\"m\">n</span> distinct eigenvalues is diagonalisable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For two vectors: suppose <span class=\"m\">c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 = 0</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_1 \\ne \\lambda_2</span>. Apply <span class=\"m\">A</span>: <span class=\"m\">c_1\\lambda_1v_1 + c_2\\lambda_2v_2 = 0</span>. Subtract <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_2</span> times the first equation: <span class=\"m\">c_1(\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2)v_1 = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">c_1 = 0</span>, and then <span class=\"m\">c_2 = 0</span>. The general case follows by induction on the number of vectors.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Powers of a diagonalisable matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A = PDP^{-1}</span>, then <span class=\"m\">A^k = PD^kP^{-1}</span> for every positive integer <span class=\"m\">k</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A^k = (PDP^{-1})(PDP^{-1})\\cdots(PDP^{-1})</span>. Every inner <span class=\"m\">P^{-1}P</span> equals <span class=\"m\">I</span>, leaving <span class=\"m\">PD\\cdots DP^{-1} = PD^kP^{-1}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Diagonalise <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">5</span> and <span class=\"m\">2</span> with eigenvectors <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, -2)</span> (from the eigen lesson).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; -2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">D = \\begin{bmatrix}5 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\det P = -3</span>, <span class=\"m\">P^{-1} = \\frac13\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A^n = \\frac13\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot5^n + 2^n &amp; 5^n - 2^n\\\\2\\cdot5^n - 2^{n+1} &amp; 5^n + 2^{n+1}\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Check <span class=\"m\">n = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac13\\begin{bmatrix}12 &amp; 3\\\\6 &amp; 9\\end{bmatrix} = A</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the steady state of <span class=\"m\">M = \\begin{bmatrix}0.8 &amp; 0.3\\\\0.2 &amp; 0.7\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Solve <span class=\"m\">(M - I)v = 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">-0.2v_1 + 0.3v_2 = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">v = (3, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Scale to sum 1: <span class=\"m\">(0.6, 0.4)</span>.</li>\n<li>The other eigenvalue is <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}M - 1 = 0.5</span>, which dies out, so every starting distribution approaches <span class=\"m\">(0.6, 0.4)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Fibonacci numbers satisfy <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}F_{n+1}\\\\F_n\\end{bmatrix} = A\\begin{bmatrix}F_n\\\\F_{n-1}\\end{bmatrix}</span> with <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Find the eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">A</span> and explain why <span class=\"m\">F_{n+1}/F_n</span> approaches the larger one.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Trace <span class=\"m\">1</span>, determinant <span class=\"m\">-1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 - \\lambda - 1 = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt5}{2}</span>, i.e. <span class=\"m\">\\varphi \\approx 1.618</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\psi \\approx -0.618</span>. Writing the start vector in the eigenvector basis, <span class=\"m\">A^n</span> multiplies the components by <span class=\"m\">\\varphi^n</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\psi^n</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">|\\psi| &lt; 1</span>, that part shrinks to zero, so the vector points more and more along the <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span> eigenvector <span class=\"m\">(\\varphi, 1)</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">F_{n+1}/F_n \\to \\varphi</span>. The exact formula is <span class=\"m\">F_n = (\\varphi^n - \\psi^n)/\\sqrt5</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Power iteration (repeatedly multiplying by <span class=\"m\">A</span>) finds the top eigenvector and is used in PageRank and spectral normalisation of GANs. Exploding and vanishing gradients in RNNs are diagonalisation in action: repeated multiplication by <span class=\"m\">W</span> scales each eigen-direction by <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^k</span>. Gaussian distributions are analysed by diagonalising the covariance <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma = Q\\Lambda Q^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Linear recurrences in algorithms, such as counting paths in a graph (<span class=\"m\">A^k</span> of the adjacency matrix counts walks of length <span class=\"m\">k</span>) or computing Fibonacci numbers in <span class=\"m\">O(\\log n)</span> by repeated squaring, rely on matrix powers.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Population models (Leslie matrices) predict that an age-structured population eventually grows at a rate equal to the largest eigenvalue, with an age distribution given by its eigenvector. Ecologists use this to decide which life stage to protect.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>, what is the bottom-right entry of <span class=\"m\">D^3</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"27\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3^3 = 27</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In <span class=\"m\">A = PDP^{-1}</span>, the columns of <span class=\"m\">P</span> are...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">eigenvectors of <span class=\"m\">A</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">rows of <span class=\"m\">A</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">A</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>And <span class=\"m\">D</span> holds the matching eigenvalues.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the trace of <span class=\"m\">A^2</span> for <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">A^2</span> are <span class=\"m\">9</span> and <span class=\"m\">1</span>; the trace is their sum. Directly, <span class=\"m\">A^2 = \\begin{bmatrix}5 &amp; 4\\\\4 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> diagonalisable?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It has only one eigenvector direction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A Markov chain has steady state <span class=\"m\">(5, 1)</span> before scaling. What fraction of time is spent in state 1?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5/6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Scale so the entries add to 1: <span class=\"m\">5/6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A matrix has eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">0.5</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.2</span>. What does <span class=\"m\">A^kx</span> approach as <span class=\"m\">k</span> grows?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the zero vector</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">an eigenvector of length 1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">infinity</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Both components shrink by factors less than 1 each step.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "positive-definite": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-quadratic-forms\" data-h=\"3\">Why quadratic forms?</h3>\n<p>A huge part of machine learning is minimising a loss. Near any minimum, a smooth loss looks like a bowl, and bowls in many dimensions are described by a symmetric matrix through the expression <span class=\"m\">x^TAx</span>, called a <strong>quadratic form</strong>. Whether the surface is a true bowl (with a single lowest point), a saddle (down in one direction, up in another), or a flat trough is decided by one property of <span class=\"m\">A</span>: whether it is <strong>positive definite</strong>. The same property tells you whether a covariance matrix is valid, whether Newton's method will head downhill, and whether a fast Cholesky factorisation can be used to solve a system.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine pouring a marble onto different surfaces. In a salad bowl it rolls to the bottom and stays there: there is a unique lowest point. On a horse saddle it rolls off sideways, even though the centre looks flat, because the surface curves up front-to-back but down side-to-side. In a long gutter (a trough) it settles somewhere along the bottom line, but not at a single point. These three shapes are exactly the three kinds of quadratic form: positive definite, indefinite and positive semidefinite.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-quadratic-form-is\" data-h=\"3\">What a quadratic form is</h3>\n<p>For a symmetric matrix <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\b &amp; c\\end{bmatrix}</span> and a vector <span class=\"m\">x = (x_1, x_2)</span>, the quadratic form is\n<span class=\"mb\">x^TAx = ax_1^2 + 2bx_1x_2 + cx_2^2</span>\nIt is a single number for each <span class=\"m\">x</span>. The off-diagonal entry appears twice, which is why we use the symmetric form. For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">x^TAx = 2x_1^2 + 2x_1x_2 + 3x_2^2</span>. At <span class=\"m\">x = (1, 1)</span> this is <span class=\"m\">2 + 2 + 3 = 7</span>; at <span class=\"m\">x = (1, -1)</span> it is <span class=\"m\">2 - 2 + 3 = 3</span>. Going the other way, any expression like <span class=\"m\">x^2 + 4xy + y^2</span> comes from a symmetric matrix: put the squared coefficients on the diagonal and split the cross term in half: <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"positive-definite-semidefinite-indefinite\" data-h=\"3\">Positive definite, semidefinite, indefinite</h3>\n<p>A symmetric matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> is <strong>positive definite</strong> if <span class=\"m\">x^TAx &gt; 0</span> for every nonzero <span class=\"m\">x</span>: the surface <span class=\"m\">z = x^TAx</span> is a bowl with its only lowest point at the origin. It is <strong>positive semidefinite</strong> if <span class=\"m\">x^TAx \\ge 0</span> for all <span class=\"m\">x</span>: a bowl or a trough. It is <strong>indefinite</strong> if <span class=\"m\">x^TAx</span> takes both signs: a saddle. For <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, at <span class=\"m\">(1, -1)</span> the form is <span class=\"m\">1 - 4 + 1 = -2</span> while at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> it is <span class=\"m\">6</span>, so it is indefinite. For <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, the form is <span class=\"m\">(x_1 + x_2)^2 \\ge 0</span>, which is zero along the whole line <span class=\"m\">x_2 = -x_1</span>: semidefinite, a trough.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g03-quadform\" data-args='{&quot;a&quot;: 2, &quot;b&quot;: 1, &quot;c&quot;: 3}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The level curves of 2x² + 2xy + 3y² are ellipses: a bowl seen from above.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"three-practical-tests\" data-h=\"3\">Three practical tests</h3>\n<p>Checking every <span class=\"m\">x</span> is impossible, so we use equivalent tests. <strong>Eigenvalue test:</strong> a symmetric matrix is positive definite exactly when all its eigenvalues are positive. The reason is the spectral theorem: in the orthonormal eigenvector basis, <span class=\"m\">x^TAx = \\lambda_1y_1^2 + \\lambda_2y_2^2 + \\dots</span>, which is positive for all nonzero <span class=\"m\">y</span> exactly when every <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_i &gt; 0</span>. <strong>Determinant test:</strong> for <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span>, <span class=\"m\">A</span> is positive definite exactly when <span class=\"m\">a &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">ac - b^2 &gt; 0</span>; for larger matrices, all the upper-left square determinants must be positive. For <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>: <span class=\"m\">2 &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">6 - 1 = 5 &gt; 0</span>, so it is a bowl. <strong>Pivot test:</strong> elimination without row swaps gives all positive pivots.</p>\n<h3 id=\"completing-the-square-shows-why\" data-h=\"3\">Completing the square shows why</h3>\n<p>Take <span class=\"m\">2x_1^2 + 2x_1x_2 + 3x_2^2</span>. Completing the square gives <span class=\"m\">2(x_1 + \\tfrac12x_2)^2 + \\tfrac52x_2^2</span>. Both coefficients, <span class=\"m\">2</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac52</span>, are positive, so the sum is positive unless both brackets are zero, which only happens at the origin. Those coefficients are exactly the pivots from elimination, and <span class=\"m\">2\\cdot\\tfrac52 = 5</span> is the determinant. The tests are all the same idea in different clothing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"where-positive-definite-matrices-come-from\" data-h=\"3\">Where positive definite matrices come from</h3>\n<p>For any matrix <span class=\"m\">B</span>, the matrix <span class=\"m\">B^TB</span> is positive semidefinite, because <span class=\"m\">x^TB^TBx = \\|Bx\\|^2 \\ge 0</span>. It is positive definite when <span class=\"m\">B</span> has independent columns. Covariance matrices are semidefinite because <span class=\"m\">v^T\\Sigma v</span> is the variance of the data projected on <span class=\"m\">v</span>, and variances can't be negative. Conversely, every positive definite matrix can be written as <span class=\"m\">A = LL^T</span> with <span class=\"m\">L</span> lower triangular with positive diagonal: the <strong>Cholesky factorisation</strong>. For <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">L = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, since <span class=\"m\">LL^T = \\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1 + 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Quadratic form</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a symmetric matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>, the function <span class=\"m\">x^TAx = \\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}x_ix_j</span>. For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> it is <span class=\"m\">2x^2 + 2xy + 3y^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Positive definite matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A symmetric matrix with <span class=\"m\">x^TAx &gt; 0</span> for every <span class=\"m\">x \\ne 0</span>; equivalently, all eigenvalues are positive. Positive semidefinite allows <span class=\"m\">\\ge 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cholesky factorisation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A = LL^T</span> with <span class=\"m\">L</span> lower triangular with positive diagonal entries; it exists exactly when <span class=\"m\">A</span> is symmetric positive definite.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Forgetting to halve the cross term when building <span class=\"m\">A</span> from an expression: <span class=\"m\">4xy</span> means <span class=\"m\">b = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Checking only that the diagonal entries are positive. <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> has a positive diagonal but is indefinite.</li>\n<li>Checking only <span class=\"m\">\\det A &gt; 0</span>. <span class=\"m\">-I</span> has determinant <span class=\"m\">1</span> but is negative definite.</li>\n<li>Applying the tests to non-symmetric matrices without symmetrising first.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A quadratic form <span class=\"m\">x^TAx</span> uses a symmetric matrix to describe a curved surface. Positive definite means a bowl (<span class=\"m\">x^TAx &gt; 0</span> for <span class=\"m\">x \\ne 0</span>), semidefinite allows a flat trough, and indefinite means a saddle. Test with eigenvalues (all positive), with <span class=\"m\">a &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\det A &gt; 0</span> in <span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span>, or with positive pivots. <span class=\"m\">B^TB</span> and covariance matrices are always semidefinite, and positive definite matrices factor as <span class=\"m\">LL^T</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g03-quadform\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Move a, b and c to change the matrix. The panel classifies the form; drag x to read off xᵀAx.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start at <span class=\"m\">a = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">c = 2</span>. The level curves are ellipses: a bowl, positive definite.\n2. Increase <span class=\"m\">b</span> past <span class=\"m\">2</span>. When <span class=\"m\">ac - b^2</span> becomes negative the curves turn into hyperbolas and the panel says indefinite (saddle).\n3. Set <span class=\"m\">a = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">c = 1</span>. The level curves become parallel lines: a trough, semidefinite.\n4. Drag <span class=\"m\">x</span> around in the saddle case and find points where <span class=\"m\">x^TAx</span> is negative.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the shape changes exactly when an eigenvalue crosses zero, which is when <span class=\"m\">ac - b^2</span> crosses zero, matching the determinant test in the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">x^TAx = ax_1^2 + 2bx_1x_2 + cx_2^2 \\ \\ \\text{for } A = \\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\b &amp; c\\end{bmatrix}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A \\succ 0 \\iff \\text{all } \\lambda_i &gt; 0 \\iff (2\\times2)\\ a &gt; 0,\\ ac - b^2 &gt; 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x^TB^TBx = \\|Bx\\|^2 \\ge 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = LL^T \\ (\\text{Cholesky})</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Eigenvalue test</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A symmetric matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> is positive definite if and only if all its eigenvalues are positive.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By the spectral theorem <span class=\"m\">A = Q\\Lambda Q^T</span>. Let <span class=\"m\">y = Q^Tx</span>, which is nonzero exactly when <span class=\"m\">x</span> is. Then <span class=\"m\">x^TAx = y^T\\Lambda y = \\sum\\lambda_iy_i^2</span>. This is positive for all nonzero <span class=\"m\">y</span> exactly when every <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_i &gt; 0</span> (choose <span class=\"m\">y = e_i</span> to see each <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_i</span> must be positive).</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Classify <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + 4xy + y^2</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Matrix: <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> (half of <span class=\"m\">4</span> off the diagonal).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">a = 1 &gt; 0</span> but <span class=\"m\">\\det A = 1 - 4 = -3 &lt; 0</span>.</li>\n<li>Indefinite: a saddle. Check: <span class=\"m\">f(1, -1) = 1 - 4 + 1 = -2 &lt; 0 &lt; f(1, 1) = 6</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the Cholesky factor of <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 &amp; 3\\\\3 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L = \\begin{bmatrix}l_{11} &amp; 0\\\\l_{21} &amp; l_{22}\\end{bmatrix}</span> with <span class=\"m\">l_{11}^2 = 9</span>, so <span class=\"m\">l_{11} = 3</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">l_{21}l_{11} = 3</span>, so <span class=\"m\">l_{21} = 1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">l_{21}^2 + l_{22}^2 = 5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">l_{22} = 2</span>. <span class=\"m\">L = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>; check <span class=\"m\">LL^T = \\begin{bmatrix}9 &amp; 3\\\\3 &amp; 1 + 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For which values of <span class=\"m\">k</span> is <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; k\\\\k &amp; 8\\end{bmatrix}</span> positive definite? For <span class=\"m\">k = 4</span>, find a nonzero <span class=\"m\">x</span> with <span class=\"m\">x^TAx = 0</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Need <span class=\"m\">2 &gt; 0</span> (true) and <span class=\"m\">16 - k^2 &gt; 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">-4 &lt; k &lt; 4</span>. For <span class=\"m\">k = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">x^TAx = 2x_1^2 + 8x_1x_2 + 8x_2^2 = 2(x_1 + 2x_2)^2</span>, which is zero for <span class=\"m\">x = (2, -1)</span>: check <span class=\"m\">2(2 - 2)^2 = 0</span>. So at <span class=\"m\">k = \\pm4</span> the matrix is only semidefinite.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Near a minimum, a loss behaves like <span class=\"m\">L(w^* + d) \\approx L(w^*) + \\frac12d^THd</span> with Hessian <span class=\"m\">H</span>. If <span class=\"m\">H</span> is positive definite, <span class=\"m\">w^*</span> is a strict local minimum; if indefinite, it is a saddle point, which is very common in deep networks. Kernel matrices in SVMs and Gaussian processes must be positive semidefinite, and Gaussian process libraries solve with Cholesky.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Cholesky solves symmetric positive definite systems about twice as fast as LU and is numerically stable without pivoting. To sample from a multivariate Gaussian with covariance <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma = LL^T</span>, draw standard normals <span class=\"m\">z</span> and return <span class=\"m\">\\mu + Lz</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Energy in physics is often a quadratic form: the potential energy of a system of springs is <span class=\"m\">\\frac12x^TKx</span> with stiffness matrix <span class=\"m\">K</span>. A stable structure needs <span class=\"m\">K</span> positive definite, so every small displacement costs energy.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">x = (1, 1)</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">x^TAx</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2 + 2\\cdot1 + 3 = 7</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span> positive definite?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3 &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\det = 6 - 1 = 5 &gt; 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The quadratic form <span class=\"m\">x^2 + 6xy + y^2</span> has which off-diagonal matrix entry?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Split the cross term: <span class=\"m\">6/2 = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A symmetric matrix has eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">4</span> and <span class=\"m\">-1</span>. Its quadratic form is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a bowl</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a saddle</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a trough</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Mixed signs mean indefinite.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For any matrix <span class=\"m\">B</span>, the matrix <span class=\"m\">B^TB</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">positive semidefinite</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">negative definite</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">always indefinite</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">x^TB^TBx = \\|Bx\\|^2 \\ge 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the largest whole number <span class=\"m\">k</span> for which <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; k\\\\k &amp; 10\\end{bmatrix}</span> is positive definite?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Need <span class=\"m\">10 - k^2 &gt; 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">k^2 &lt; 10</span>; <span class=\"m\">k = 3</span> works and <span class=\"m\">k = 4</span> does not.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "svd": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-svd\" data-h=\"3\">Why the SVD?</h3>\n<p>Eigenvalues are powerful, but they have limitations: they only exist for square matrices, they can be complex, and not every matrix is diagonalisable. The <strong>singular value decomposition</strong> has none of these problems. Every matrix, of any shape, factors as\n<span class=\"mb\">A = U\\Sigma V^T</span>\nwith <span class=\"m\">U</span> and <span class=\"m\">V</span> orthogonal and <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> diagonal with nonnegative entries. It is often called the most important factorisation in applied linear algebra. It gives the best low-rank approximation of a matrix (the basis of compression and recommender systems), the pseudoinverse, the condition number, the rank, and PCA itself.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine squeezing a round balloon between your hands at some angle. It turns into an oval. The SVD says that every linear transformation does essentially this: it takes a circle (or sphere) of inputs and turns it into an ellipse (or ellipsoid). There are special perpendicular input directions that end up along the axes of the ellipse, and the lengths of those axes are the <strong>singular values</strong>. Everything else about the matrix is just rotation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-geometry-rotate-stretch-rotate\" data-h=\"3\">The geometry: rotate, stretch, rotate</h3>\n<p>Read <span class=\"m\">A = U\\Sigma V^T</span> from right to left as three steps applied to a vector <span class=\"m\">x</span>. First <span class=\"m\">V^T</span>, an orthogonal matrix, rotates (or reflects) the input so that the special input directions line up with the axes. Then <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> stretches each axis by a singular value <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1 \\ge \\sigma_2 \\ge \\dots \\ge 0</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">U</span> rotates the result into its final position. The columns of <span class=\"m\">V</span> are the <strong>right singular vectors</strong> (special input directions), the columns of <span class=\"m\">U</span> are the <strong>left singular vectors</strong> (directions of the ellipse's axes), and\n<span class=\"mb\">Av_i = \\sigma_iu_i</span>\nEach special input direction <span class=\"m\">v_i</span> is sent to the axis <span class=\"m\">u_i</span>, stretched by <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_i</span>. Unlike eigenvectors, the output direction is allowed to be different from the input direction; what's special is that perpendicular inputs go to perpendicular outputs.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"svd\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Step through Vᵀ, Σ and U: a circle is rotated, stretched into an ellipse, then rotated again.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"how-to-compute-it\" data-h=\"3\">How to compute it</h3>\n<p>The trick is the symmetric matrix <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>. Using <span class=\"m\">A = U\\Sigma V^T</span>, <span class=\"m\">A^TA = V\\Sigma^TU^TU\\Sigma V^T = V\\Sigma^T\\Sigma V^T</span>. That is a diagonalisation of <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>! So the right singular vectors <span class=\"m\">v_i</span> are eigenvectors of <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>, and the singular values are the square roots of its eigenvalues, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_i = \\sqrt{\\lambda_i}</span>. Then each <span class=\"m\">u_i = Av_i/\\sigma_i</span>.</p>\n<p>Try <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\4 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">A^TA = \\begin{bmatrix}25 &amp; 20\\\\20 &amp; 25\\end{bmatrix}</span>, with trace <span class=\"m\">50</span> and determinant <span class=\"m\">625 - 400 = 225</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 - 50\\lambda + 225 = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 45</span> and <span class=\"m\">5</span>. The singular values are <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{45} = 3\\sqrt5 \\approx 6.71</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt5 \\approx 2.24</span>. The eigenvectors are <span class=\"m\">v_1 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">v_2 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, -1)</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">u_1 = Av_1/\\sigma_1 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(3, 9)/(3\\sqrt5) = \\frac1{\\sqrt{10}}(1, 3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">u_2 = Av_2/\\sigma_2 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(3, -1)/\\sqrt5 = \\frac1{\\sqrt{10}}(3, -1)</span>. These are perpendicular: <span class=\"m\">3 - 3 = 0</span>. As a check, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1\\sigma_2 = 3\\sqrt5\\cdot\\sqrt5 = 15 = |\\det A|</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rank-one-pieces-and-the-best-approximation\" data-h=\"3\">Rank-one pieces and the best approximation</h3>\n<p>Multiplying out <span class=\"m\">U\\Sigma V^T</span> writes <span class=\"m\">A</span> as a sum of simple rank-one matrices:\n<span class=\"mb\">A = \\sigma_1u_1v_1^T + \\sigma_2u_2v_2^T + \\dots + \\sigma_ru_rv_r^T</span>\nwhere <span class=\"m\">r</span> is the rank (the number of nonzero singular values). The pieces are ordered by importance. Keeping only the first <span class=\"m\">k</span> terms gives <span class=\"m\">A_k</span>, and the <strong>Eckart–Young theorem</strong> says <span class=\"m\">A_k</span> is the closest rank-<span class=\"m\">k</span> matrix to <span class=\"m\">A</span>. The error is controlled by the dropped singular values. For a <span class=\"m\">1000\\times1000</span> image, keeping 50 terms needs <span class=\"m\">50(1000 + 1000 + 1) = 100{,}050</span> numbers instead of <span class=\"m\">1{,}000{,}000</span>, a tenfold saving, and the picture usually still looks good.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-singular-values-tell-you\" data-h=\"3\">What singular values tell you</h3>\n<p>The largest singular value <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1</span> is the most <span class=\"m\">A</span> can stretch any unit vector: the <strong>spectral norm</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\|A\\|_2</span>. The sum of squares of all the singular values equals the sum of squares of all entries: <span class=\"m\">\\|A\\|_F^2 = \\sum\\sigma_i^2</span>. The ratio <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1/\\sigma_n</span> is the <strong>condition number</strong>: a large value means <span class=\"m\">Ax = b</span> is sensitive to small errors. For a symmetric positive definite matrix, the SVD and the eigendecomposition coincide.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Singular value decomposition</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A = U\\Sigma V^T</span> with <span class=\"m\">U</span>, <span class=\"m\">V</span> orthogonal and <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> diagonal with entries <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1 \\ge \\sigma_2 \\ge \\dots \\ge 0</span>. It exists for every matrix.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Singular values</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The numbers <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_i = \\sqrt{\\lambda_i(A^TA)}</span>: the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse that <span class=\"m\">A</span> makes from the unit circle.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Using eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">A</span> instead of square roots of eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that singular values are never negative, even when <span class=\"m\">\\det A &lt; 0</span>; the sign goes into <span class=\"m\">U</span> or <span class=\"m\">V</span>.</li>\n<li>Choosing <span class=\"m\">u_i</span> independently of <span class=\"m\">v_i</span> and getting the signs wrong. Compute <span class=\"m\">u_i = Av_i/\\sigma_i</span>.</li>\n<li>Thinking the SVD needs a square matrix. It works for every shape.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"svd-versus-eigendecomposition\" data-h=\"3\">SVD versus eigendecomposition</h3>\n<p>It is worth comparing the two factorisations side by side. The eigendecomposition <span class=\"m\">A = PDP^{-1}</span> uses one basis for both input and output, needs a square matrix, and may involve a <span class=\"m\">P</span> that is far from orthogonal or does not exist at all. The SVD <span class=\"m\">A = U\\Sigma V^T</span> uses two different bases, one for inputs and one for outputs, but both are orthonormal, and it always exists. For a symmetric positive definite matrix the two coincide: <span class=\"m\">U = V = Q</span> and the singular values are the eigenvalues. For a symmetric matrix with a negative eigenvalue, the singular value is its absolute value and the sign is absorbed into <span class=\"m\">U</span>. Because orthogonal matrices never amplify errors, SVD-based computations are among the most reliable in numerical computing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Every matrix factors as <span class=\"m\">A = U\\Sigma V^T</span>: rotate, stretch along the axes by the singular values, rotate again. The right singular vectors and squared singular values are the eigenvectors and eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span>, and <span class=\"m\">u_i = Av_i/\\sigma_i</span>. Writing <span class=\"m\">A</span> as a sum of rank-one pieces <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_iu_iv_i^T</span> and keeping the largest gives the best low-rank approximation. The rank is the number of nonzero singular values, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1</span> is the spectral norm, and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1/\\sigma_n</span> is the condition number.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"svd\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Step through Vᵀ, Σ and U. The unit circle becomes an ellipse with semi-axes σ₁ and σ₂.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Step through the three stages slowly. After <span class=\"m\">V^T</span> the circle is just turned; after <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> it is an axis-aligned ellipse; after <span class=\"m\">U</span> the ellipse is rotated into place.\n2. Change the matrix so it is nearly singular. <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_2</span> shrinks and the ellipse becomes a thin cigar.\n3. Make the matrix a pure rotation. Both singular values become 1 and the circle stays a circle.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the semi-axis lengths are always <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_2</span>, and their product is the area factor <span class=\"m\">|\\det A|</span>. A thin ellipse means a large condition number <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1/\\sigma_2</span>, as described in the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">A = U\\Sigma V^T = \\sum_{i=1}^r \\sigma_iu_iv_i^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A^TA = V\\Sigma^T\\Sigma V^T, \\qquad \\sigma_i = \\sqrt{\\lambda_i(A^TA)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Av_i = \\sigma_iu_i, \\qquad \\operatorname{rank}A = \\#\\{\\sigma_i &gt; 0\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|A\\|_2 = \\sigma_1, \\quad \\|A\\|_F^2 = \\sum\\sigma_i^2, \\quad \\kappa(A) = \\sigma_1/\\sigma_n</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Eckart–Young</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The best rank-<span class=\"m\">k</span> approximation of <span class=\"m\">A</span> in the Frobenius (or spectral) norm is <span class=\"m\">A_k = \\sum_{i=1}^k\\sigma_iu_iv_i^T</span>, with error <span class=\"m\">\\|A - A_k\\|_F^2 = \\sum_{i&gt;k}\\sigma_i^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Sketch of the error formula: <span class=\"m\">A - A_k = \\sum_{i&gt;k}\\sigma_iu_iv_i^T</span>, whose SVD has singular values <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_{k+1}, \\sigma_{k+2}, \\dots</span>, so its squared Frobenius norm is their sum of squares. That no other rank-<span class=\"m\">k</span> matrix does better is the content of the theorem; the full proof uses the fact that any rank-<span class=\"m\">k</span> matrix has a null space meeting the span of <span class=\"m\">v_1, \\dots, v_{k+1}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the SVD of <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A^TA = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span> has eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">4</span> and <span class=\"m\">0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_2 = 0</span>. Rank 1.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">v_1 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, 1)</span>; <span class=\"m\">u_1 = Av_1/2 = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A = 2u_1v_1^T = 2\\cdot\\frac12\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">A</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the singular values of <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; -3\\end{bmatrix}</span> and its condition number</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A^TA = \\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 9\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so the singular values are <span class=\"m\">3</span> and <span class=\"m\">2</span> (sorted).</li>\n<li>The minus sign goes into <span class=\"m\">U</span>: <span class=\"m\">u = (0, -1)</span> for <span class=\"m\">v = (0, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Condition number <span class=\"m\">3/2 = 1.5</span>; <span class=\"m\">\\|A\\|_F^2 = 4 + 9 = 13 = 3^2 + 2^2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\4 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>, find the singular values and the best rank-one approximation error <span class=\"m\">\\|A - A_1\\|_F</span>. Verify <span class=\"m\">\\|A\\|_F^2 = \\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>From the article, <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span> has eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">45</span> and <span class=\"m\">5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1 = 3\\sqrt5</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt5</span>. The rank-one error is <span class=\"m\">\\|A - A_1\\|_F = \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt5 \\approx 2.24</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">\\|A\\|_F^2 = 9 + 0 + 16 + 25 = 50 = 45 + 5</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Recommender systems approximate a users-by-items ratings matrix by a low-rank SVD, so each user and item gets a short vector of hidden factors. Latent semantic analysis applies SVD to word-document matrices. LoRA updates and model compression use low-rank factors, and the spectral norm <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1</span> is used to regularise GAN discriminators (spectral normalisation).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The pseudoinverse <span class=\"m\">A^+ = V\\Sigma^+U^T</span> (invert the nonzero singular values) gives least-squares solutions even when <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span> is singular. Numerical libraries estimate rank by counting singular values above a tolerance, and image and video codecs exploit low-rank structure.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A photo stored as a matrix of pixel brightnesses can be compressed by keeping its top singular values. Surveillance video is separated into a still background (low rank) and moving people (sparse) using the same ideas.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the larger singular value of <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; -5\\end{bmatrix}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Singular values are <span class=\"m\">|3|</span> and <span class=\"m\">|-5|</span>, sorted: <span class=\"m\">5</span> and <span class=\"m\">3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Singular values are the square roots of the eigenvalues of...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">A^TA</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A + A^T</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">A^TA = V\\Sigma^2V^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A matrix has singular values <span class=\"m\">3, 4</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">\\|A\\|_F^2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">9 + 16 = 25</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A matrix has singular values <span class=\"m\">10, 2, 0, 0</span>. What is its rank?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Count the nonzero singular values.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Singular values <span class=\"m\">10</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.1</span>: what is the condition number?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"100\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1/\\sigma_n = 10/0.1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Does every real matrix, of any shape, have an SVD?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Unlike diagonalisation, the SVD always exists.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "pca": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-pca\" data-h=\"3\">Why PCA?</h3>\n<p>Real datasets often have many features: hundreds of pixel values, thousands of gene measurements, dozens of survey answers. But features are usually correlated, so the data doesn't really fill all those dimensions. It lies close to a much smaller subspace. <strong>Principal component analysis (PCA)</strong> finds that subspace. It identifies the directions along which the data spreads out most, ranks them by how much spread they capture, and lets you keep only the top few. That gives you compression, noise reduction, faster models, and plots of high-dimensional data in two dimensions. PCA brings together almost everything in this course: centring (vectors), covariance (matrix products), projection, eigenvectors of symmetric matrices, and the SVD.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine measuring the height and arm span of everyone in a class. Plotted, the points form a long, thin, tilted cloud, because tall people tend to have long arms. If you had to describe each student with a single number instead of two, you wouldn't pick height alone or arm span alone. You'd pick their position along the long axis of the cloud, a kind of \"overall size\" score. The short axis, \"arm span relative to height\", varies much less and carries less information. PCA finds that long axis automatically, in any number of dimensions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"step-1-centre-the-data\" data-h=\"3\">Step 1: centre the data</h3>\n<p>Put the data in a matrix <span class=\"m\">X</span> with one row per example and one column per feature. First subtract the mean of each column, so the cloud is centred at the origin. PCA looks for directions through the centre of the data, so skipping this step gives wrong answers. Here is a tiny centred dataset of four points: <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(-2, -1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(-1, -2)</span>. Each column already averages to zero.</p>\n<h3 id=\"step-2-the-covariance-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">Step 2: the covariance matrix</h3>\n<p>The spread of the data along a unit direction <span class=\"m\">u</span> is measured by the variance of the projections <span class=\"m\">x_i\\cdot u</span>. Because the data is centred, the projections have mean zero, and their variance is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum(x_i\\cdot u)^2 = u^TCu</span>, where\n<span class=\"mb\">C = \\frac{1}{n-1}X^TX</span>\nis the <strong>covariance matrix</strong>. Its diagonal entries are the variances of the individual features and its off-diagonal entries are covariances, which measure how features move together. For our data, <span class=\"m\">X^TX = \\begin{bmatrix}10 &amp; 8\\\\8 &amp; 10\\end{bmatrix}</span> (for example, the top-left entry is <span class=\"m\">4 + 4 + 1 + 1</span> and the off-diagonal is <span class=\"m\">2 + 2 + 2 + 2</span>), and with <span class=\"m\">n - 1 = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">C = \\frac13\\begin{bmatrix}10 &amp; 8\\\\8 &amp; 10\\end{bmatrix}</span>. The positive off-diagonal says the two features tend to increase together.</p>\n<h3 id=\"step-3-eigenvectors-are-the-principal-components\" data-h=\"3\">Step 3: eigenvectors are the principal components</h3>\n<p>We want the unit vector <span class=\"m\">u</span> that maximises the variance <span class=\"m\">u^TCu</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">C</span> is symmetric, the spectral theorem says it has perpendicular unit eigenvectors, and in that basis <span class=\"m\">u^TCu = \\lambda_1y_1^2 + \\lambda_2y_2^2</span> with <span class=\"m\">y_1^2 + y_2^2 = 1</span>. This is largest when all the weight goes on the biggest eigenvalue. So <strong>the direction of maximum variance is the top eigenvector of <span class=\"m\">C</span></strong>, and the variance along it is the top eigenvalue. The next principal component is the best direction perpendicular to the first, which is the second eigenvector, and so on.</p>\n<p>For our data, <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}10 &amp; 8\\\\8 &amp; 10\\end{bmatrix}</span> has eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">18</span> and <span class=\"m\">2</span> with eigenvectors <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, -1)</span>. Dividing by 3, the variances are <span class=\"m\">6</span> along the first principal component (PC1) and <span class=\"m\">\\frac23</span> along PC2. The cloud is stretched along the diagonal <span class=\"m\">y = x</span>, as you'd expect from the points.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"pca\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>PC1 points along the longest direction of the cloud; PC2 is perpendicular to it.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"step-4-explained-variance-and-projection\" data-h=\"3\">Step 4: explained variance and projection</h3>\n<p>The total variance is the trace of <span class=\"m\">C</span>, which equals the sum of the eigenvalues: <span class=\"m\">6 + \\frac23 = \\frac{20}{3}</span>. The fraction captured by PC1 is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{6}{20/3} = \\frac{18}{20} = 0.9</span>. Keeping only PC1 keeps 90% of the spread while halving the number of numbers. To compress, project each centred point onto the chosen components: the PC1 score of <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> is <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)\\cdot\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, 1) = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt2} \\approx 2.12</span>. To reconstruct an approximation, multiply the score back by the component: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{3}{\\sqrt2}\\cdot\\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, 1) = (1.5, 1.5)</span>, close to the original <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>. In matrix form, with the top <span class=\"m\">k</span> components in the columns of <span class=\"m\">W</span>, scores are <span class=\"m\">Z = XW</span> and the reconstruction is <span class=\"m\">ZW^T</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-svd-connection\" data-h=\"3\">The SVD connection</h3>\n<p>In practice PCA is computed with the SVD of the centred data, <span class=\"m\">X = U\\Sigma V^T</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">X^TX = V\\Sigma^2V^T</span>, so the principal components are the right singular vectors (columns of <span class=\"m\">V</span>) and the variances are <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_i^2/(n-1)</span>. This avoids forming <span class=\"m\">X^TX</span>, which is more accurate numerically. The scores are <span class=\"m\">XV = U\\Sigma</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>PCA is the best possible projection in two equivalent senses: the top-<span class=\"m\">k</span> components capture the most variance of any <span class=\"m\">k</span>-dimensional subspace, and they minimise the total squared reconstruction error. These are the same because of Pythagoras: each point's squared length splits into the captured part plus the error, so maximising one minimises the other.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Principal component</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A unit eigenvector of the data's covariance matrix. The first principal component is the direction of greatest variance; each later one is the direction of greatest variance perpendicular to the earlier ones.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Covariance matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For centred data <span class=\"m\">X</span> (<span class=\"m\">n</span> rows), <span class=\"m\">C = \\frac{1}{n-1}X^TX</span>. Diagonal entries are feature variances; off-diagonal entries are covariances.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Explained variance ratio</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\lambda_i/\\sum_j\\lambda_j</span>: the fraction of the total variance captured by component <span class=\"m\">i</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Forgetting to centre the data first.</li>\n<li>Running PCA on features with wildly different units (metres and millimetres) without standardising; the largest-unit feature dominates.</li>\n<li>Reading principal components as original features. Each is a mix of features.</li>\n<li>Assuming PCA finds the most useful directions for prediction. It finds the most spread, which may not be what the labels depend on.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>PCA centres the data, forms the covariance matrix <span class=\"m\">C = \\frac{1}{n-1}X^TX</span>, and takes its eigenvectors as principal components, ordered by eigenvalue (variance). Keeping the top <span class=\"m\">k</span> components and projecting gives the best <span class=\"m\">k</span>-dimensional summary, with explained variance ratio <span class=\"m\">\\sum_{i\\le k}\\lambda_i/\\sum\\lambda_i</span>. Computationally, the components are the right singular vectors of the centred data matrix.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"pca\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Change the spread and tilt. PC1 (orange) always points along the longest direction. Project to 1-D to compress.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Make the cloud long and thin. PC1 lines up with its long axis and its share of the variance is close to 100%.\n2. Rotate the tilt. PC1 rotates with the cloud: it depends on the data, not on the axes.\n3. Make the cloud round. The two variances become similar and PC1's direction becomes unstable: no direction is special.\n4. Project to 1-D. Each point drops perpendicularly onto PC1.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> projecting onto PC1 throws away only the short-axis spread, which is the fraction <span class=\"m\">1 - \\lambda_1/(\\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2)</span> from the article. Round clouds can't be compressed well.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">C = \\frac{1}{n-1}X^TX \\ \\ (X \\text{ centred}), \\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(Xu) = u^TCu</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Cw_i = \\lambda_iw_i, \\qquad \\text{explained ratio} = \\frac{\\lambda_i}{\\sum_j\\lambda_j}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Z = XW_k, \\qquad \\hat X = ZW_k^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">X = U\\Sigma V^T \\Rightarrow \\lambda_i = \\frac{\\sigma_i^2}{n-1}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">The top eigenvector maximises variance</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For symmetric <span class=\"m\">C</span> with eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_1 \\ge \\lambda_2 \\ge \\dots</span>, the maximum of <span class=\"m\">u^TCu</span> over unit vectors <span class=\"m\">u</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_1</span>, attained at the top eigenvector.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Write <span class=\"m\">u = \\sum y_iq_i</span> in the orthonormal eigenbasis, with <span class=\"m\">\\sum y_i^2 = 1</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">u^TCu = \\sum\\lambda_iy_i^2 \\le \\lambda_1\\sum y_i^2 = \\lambda_1</span>, with equality when <span class=\"m\">u = q_1</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Run PCA on the points <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(2, 2)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(4, 4)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Mean <span class=\"m\">(2, 2)</span>; centred points <span class=\"m\">(-2, -2)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(2, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">X^TX = \\begin{bmatrix}8 &amp; 8\\\\8 &amp; 8\\end{bmatrix}</span>, eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">16</span> and <span class=\"m\">0</span>.</li>\n<li>PC1 <span class=\"m\">= \\frac1{\\sqrt2}(1, 1)</span> explains <span class=\"m\">16/16 = 100\\%</span>: the points lie exactly on a line.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A 4-feature dataset has covariance eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">5, 3, 1, 1</span>. How many components keep at least 80% of the variance?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Total <span class=\"m\">= 10</span>.</li>\n<li>One component: <span class=\"m\">5/10 = 50\\%</span>. Two: <span class=\"m\">8/10 = 80\\%</span>.</li>\n<li>Two components are enough.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Centred 2-D data has covariance <span class=\"m\">C = \\begin{bmatrix}5 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Find the principal components, the explained variance ratio of PC1, and the PC1 score of the centred point <span class=\"m\">(1, 3)</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Trace <span class=\"m\">7</span>, determinant <span class=\"m\">10 - 4 = 6</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 - 7\\lambda + 6 = (\\lambda - 6)(\\lambda - 1)</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 6</span>: <span class=\"m\">C - 6I = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; -4\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so PC1 <span class=\"m\">= \\frac1{\\sqrt5}(2, 1)</span>. PC2 <span class=\"m\">= \\frac1{\\sqrt5}(-1, 2)</span> (perpendicular, eigenvalue 1). Ratio <span class=\"m\">6/7 \\approx 0.857</span>. Score of <span class=\"m\">(1, 3)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{2 + 3}{\\sqrt5} = \\sqrt5 \\approx 2.24</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>PCA is used to reduce input dimension before training, to whiten data, and to visualise embeddings and hidden activations in 2-D. Eigenfaces represent faces as combinations of principal components of face images. Autoencoders with linear layers and squared loss learn the same subspace as PCA.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For very large datasets, randomised SVD and incremental PCA compute the top components without storing all of <span class=\"m\">X^TX</span>. Search engines and databases use PCA-like reductions to speed up nearest-neighbour search.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Psychologists summarise dozens of personality-survey answers by a few principal factors; the \"Big Five\" traits emerged from this kind of analysis. Finance uses PCA on interest rates, where three components (level, slope, curvature) explain most daily movements.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Covariance eigenvalues are <span class=\"m\">9</span> and <span class=\"m\">1</span>. What fraction of variance does PC1 explain?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"9/10|0.9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">9/(9 + 1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Before PCA, the data should be...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">centred (mean subtracted from each feature)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">sorted</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">squared</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>PCA directions pass through the mean of the data.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Principal components are eigenvectors of...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the covariance matrix</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the data matrix <span class=\"m\">X</span> itself</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the identity</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>They maximise <span class=\"m\">u^TCu</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">6, 3, 1</span>. How many components are needed to explain at least 90%?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">6/10 = 60\\%</span>; <span class=\"m\">(6 + 3)/10 = 90\\%</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Are two different principal components perpendicular?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>They are eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With SVD of centred <span class=\"m\">X</span> (<span class=\"m\">n = 11</span> rows) and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1 = 10</span>, what is the variance along PC1?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1^2/(n - 1) = 100/10</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "tensors": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-tensors\" data-h=\"3\">Why tensors?</h3>\n<p>Scalars, vectors and matrices are arrays with zero, one and two axes. Machine learning constantly needs more. A colour image has height, width and three colour channels: three axes. A batch of 32 images adds a fourth. A batch of sentences in a transformer has batch, position and embedding axes. These multi-axis arrays are called <strong>tensors</strong> in ML libraries (PyTorch, TensorFlow, JAX), and fluency with their shapes is one of the most practical skills in the field. Most bugs in model code are shape bugs, and most speed-ups come from replacing loops with whole-tensor operations.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Think of a filing system. A single number is one sticky note. A list of numbers is a row of sticky notes (a vector). A spreadsheet is a grid (a matrix). A binder of spreadsheets, one per month, is three-dimensional: month, row, column. A shelf of binders, one per year, is four-dimensional. Nothing new happens mathematically; you just keep adding labelled axes. A tensor's <strong>shape</strong> is the list of how long each axis is, like \"4 years, 12 months, 30 rows, 5 columns\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"shape-rank-and-size\" data-h=\"3\">Shape, rank and size</h3>\n<p>In ML usage, a tensor's <strong>rank</strong> (also called <code>ndim</code>) is the number of axes, and its shape lists their lengths. A scalar has shape <span class=\"m\">()</span>, a vector of length 5 has shape <span class=\"m\">(5)</span>, a <span class=\"m\">3\\times4</span> matrix has shape <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>, and a batch of 32 RGB images of size <span class=\"m\">224\\times224</span> has shape <span class=\"m\">(32, 3, 224, 224)</span>, rank 4. The total number of entries is the product of the shape: <span class=\"m\">32\\cdot3\\cdot224\\cdot224 = 4{,}816{,}896</span>. Be careful: \"rank\" here means the number of axes, which is different from the rank of a matrix in the earlier lesson. An entry is picked out by one index per axis, like <code>x[b, c, i, j]</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Tensor</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A multi-dimensional array of numbers. Its shape lists the length of each axis, and its rank (ndim) is the number of axes.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"reshaping-and-transposing\" data-h=\"3\">Reshaping and transposing</h3>\n<p>A <strong>reshape</strong> reorganises the same numbers into a new shape with the same total size. A tensor of shape <span class=\"m\">(2, 3, 4)</span> holds 24 numbers and can be reshaped to <span class=\"m\">(6, 4)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(24)</span> or <span class=\"m\">(4, 6)</span>, but not <span class=\"m\">(5, 5)</span>. Flattening an image of shape <span class=\"m\">(28, 28)</span> into a vector of length 784 is how classic networks fed images into a dense layer. A <strong>transpose</strong> or <strong>permute</strong> reorders axes: converting images from <span class=\"m\">(B, H, W, C)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(B, C, H, W)</span> moves the channel axis.</p>\n<h3 id=\"elementwise-operations-and-broadcasting\" data-h=\"3\">Elementwise operations and broadcasting</h3>\n<p>Adding or multiplying two tensors of the same shape works entry by entry. <strong>Broadcasting</strong> extends this to different shapes: compare shapes from the right; two axis lengths are compatible if they are equal or one of them is 1, and the length-1 axis is stretched (virtually copied) to match. So a <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span> tensor plus a <span class=\"m\">(1, 4)</span> tensor gives <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>, and a <span class=\"m\">(32, 10)</span> batch of scores plus a bias of shape <span class=\"m\">(10)</span> adds the same bias to every row. For example, <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}10 &amp; 20 &amp; 30\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}11 &amp; 21 &amp; 31\\\\12 &amp; 22 &amp; 32\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Shapes <span class=\"m\">(3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(4)</span> are not compatible and raise an error.</p>\n<h3 id=\"contractions-matrix-multiplication-generalised\" data-h=\"3\">Contractions: matrix multiplication generalised</h3>\n<p>The most important tensor operation is <strong>contraction</strong>: multiply along a shared axis and sum it away. Matrix multiplication contracts the last axis of <span class=\"m\">A</span> with the first of <span class=\"m\">B</span>: <span class=\"m\">(n, m)</span> times <span class=\"m\">(m, p)</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(n, p)</span>. <strong>Batched</strong> matrix multiplication treats leading axes as batch axes: <span class=\"m\">(B, n, m)</span> times <span class=\"m\">(B, m, p)</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(B, n, p)</span>, one matrix product per batch element. Einstein summation notation writes these compactly: <code>einsum('bij,bjk-&gt;bik', A, B)</code> means \"for each <span class=\"m\">b</span>, sum over <span class=\"m\">j</span>\". In a transformer, queries of shape <span class=\"m\">(B, T, d)</span> times transposed keys <span class=\"m\">(B, d, T)</span> give attention scores of shape <span class=\"m\">(B, T, T)</span>: one score for every pair of positions in every sequence.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;rotate&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Every grid point is moved by the same matrix: a whole batch of vectors transformed by one operation.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"vectorised-thinking\" data-h=\"3\">Vectorised thinking</h3>\n<p>Suppose you need predictions for 10,000 examples with 100 features. A Python loop computes 10,000 separate dot products, one at a time. The vectorised version stacks the examples in a <span class=\"m\">(10000, 100)</span> matrix <span class=\"m\">X</span> and computes <code>X @ w</code> in one call. The answer is identical, but the library runs it in optimised compiled code that uses the CPU's vector units or the GPU's thousands of cores, often 100 times faster or more. Writing code this way means thinking about whole tensors and their shapes, rather than individual numbers. A good habit is to write the shape of every tensor as a comment next to each line.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>Tensors are just organised collections of numbers, and tensor operations are the same linear algebra you already know applied many times in parallel. Broadcasting is shorthand for \"repeat this operation across an axis\". Contraction is the dot product, summed along a chosen axis. Because the operations are uniform, hardware can run them in parallel.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Confusing tensor rank (number of axes) with matrix rank (number of independent columns).</li>\n<li>Reshaping when you meant to transpose. Reshape keeps the memory order; it doesn't swap axes.</li>\n<li>Silent broadcasting bugs: <span class=\"m\">(32, 1)</span> minus <span class=\"m\">(32)</span> gives a <span class=\"m\">(32, 32)</span> tensor, not <span class=\"m\">(32, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Multiplying with <code>*</code> (elementwise) when you meant <code>@</code> (matrix product), or the reverse.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A tensor is an array with any number of axes; its shape lists the axis lengths and its size is their product. Reshape changes the shape without changing the numbers; transpose reorders axes. Elementwise operations broadcast by stretching length-1 axes. Contractions, including batched matrix multiplication and einsum, multiply along a shared axis and sum it away. Vectorised code applies these to whole tensors at once, which is how ML runs fast.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;shear&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The grid is a batch of points, a tensor of shape (N, 2). One matrix transforms all of them at once.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose <strong>shear</strong> and press <strong>Animate</strong>. Every grid point moves at once: that is one matrix product applied to a whole batch of points.\n2. Imagine the grid points stacked as the rows of a matrix <span class=\"m\">X</span> of shape <span class=\"m\">(N, 2)</span>. The new points are <span class=\"m\">XA^T</span>, also <span class=\"m\">(N, 2)</span>.\n3. Change the matrix by dragging <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span>. The whole batch updates together, never one point at a time.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the batch axis <span class=\"m\">N</span> just comes along for the ride while the 2-length axis is contracted with the matrix, exactly the batched-operation idea from the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{size} = \\prod_i \\text{shape}_i</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(n, m) \\times (m, p) \\to (n, p), \\qquad (B, n, m) \\times (B, m, p) \\to (B, n, p)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Broadcast: } (3, 1) + (1, 4) \\to (3, 4)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\langle A, B\\rangle = \\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ij} = \\operatorname{tr}(A^TB)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Elementwise inner product is a trace</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For matrices <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> of the same shape, <span class=\"m\">\\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ij} = \\operatorname{tr}(A^TB)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The diagonal entry <span class=\"m\">(j, j)</span> of <span class=\"m\">A^TB</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i(A^T)_{ji}B_{ij} = \\sum_iA_{ij}B_{ij}</span>. Summing over <span class=\"m\">j</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ij}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A batch of 64 sentences, each 128 tokens, with embeddings of size 512, passes through a linear layer with weight <span class=\"m\">W</span> of shape <span class=\"m\">(512, 256)</span>. Find the output shape and the number of weights</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Input shape <span class=\"m\">(64, 128, 512)</span>. The last axis is contracted with the first axis of <span class=\"m\">W</span>.</li>\n<li>Output shape <span class=\"m\">(64, 128, 256)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">W</span> has <span class=\"m\">512\\cdot256 = 131{,}072</span> weights, shared across all 8,192 tokens.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">What shape results from adding tensors of shapes <span class=\"m\">(8, 1, 5)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span>?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Align from the right: <span class=\"m\">(8, 1, 5)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(\\ , 3, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Last axis: 5 vs 1, gives 5. Middle: 1 vs 3, gives 3. First: 8 (missing axis treated as 1).</li>\n<li>Result <span class=\"m\">(8, 3, 5)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A tensor has shape <span class=\"m\">(4, 3, 10)</span>. (a) How many entries does it hold? (b) Can it be reshaped to <span class=\"m\">(12, 10)</span>? To <span class=\"m\">(6, 20)</span>? To <span class=\"m\">(7, 17)</span>? (c) After <code>x.sum(axis=1)</code>, what is the shape?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">4\\cdot3\\cdot10 = 120</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">(12, 10)</span>: yes, <span class=\"m\">120</span> entries. <span class=\"m\">(6, 20)</span>: yes, <span class=\"m\">120</span>. <span class=\"m\">(7, 17)</span>: no, <span class=\"m\">119 \\ne 120</span>. (c) Summing over axis 1 removes the length-3 axis, giving shape <span class=\"m\">(4, 10)</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every layer of a deep network is a tensor operation: convolutions contract image patches with filters, attention contracts queries with keys, and dense layers contract features with weights. Gradients have the same shape as the parameters they belong to, so shape reasoning is also how you check backpropagation code.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Tensors are stored as flat memory plus a shape and strides (how far to jump in memory per step along each axis). Transposing usually just changes the strides without copying data, while reshaping a non-contiguous tensor may force a copy. GPUs are built to run the same operation on many entries at once (SIMD), which is why vectorised code is so much faster.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box code\"><div class=\"box-label\">In code</div><div class=\"box-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">import numpy as np\nx = np.zeros((32, 3, 224, 224)); x.size     # 4816896\na = np.arange(3).reshape(3, 1); b = np.arange(4)\n(a + b).shape                                # (3, 4) by broadcasting\nA = np.ones((8, 10, 20)); B = np.ones((8, 20, 7))\n(A @ B).shape                                # (8, 10, 7)\n</code></pre></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A colour video is a rank-4 tensor (time, height, width, colour). A weather model stores temperature on a grid of latitude, longitude, altitude and time. A shop's sales database by store, product and day is a rank-3 tensor.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many entries does a tensor of shape <span class=\"m\">(2, 3, 4)</span> have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"24\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2\\cdot3\\cdot4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A batch of images has shape <span class=\"m\">(B, C, H, W)</span>. What is its rank (number of axes)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>One axis each for batch, channel, height and width.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Broadcasting shapes <span class=\"m\">(5, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, 3)</span> gives...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">(5, 3)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(5, 1)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">an error</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Length-1 axes stretch to match.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the last dimension of <span class=\"m\">(8, 10, 20) @ (8, 20, 7)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Batched matmul: <span class=\"m\">(8, 10, 7)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A <span class=\"m\">(6, 4)</span> tensor is reshaped to <span class=\"m\">(3, k)</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">k</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"8\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The size <span class=\"m\">24</span> must be preserved: <span class=\"m\">24/3 = 8</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">\\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ij}</span> for <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\3 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">B = I</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Only the diagonal survives: <span class=\"m\">1 + 4 = \\operatorname{tr}(A)</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>"}, "formulas": [{"l": "vectors", "t": "What is a vector?", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">v = (v_1, \\dots, v_n) \\in \\mathbb{R}^n</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|v\\| = \\sqrt{v_1^2 + v_2^2 + \\dots + v_n^2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat v = \\frac{v}{\\|v\\|}\\quad (v \\ne 0)</span></p>"}, {"l": "vector-ops", "t": "Vector addition and scaling", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">u + v = (u_1 + v_1, \\dots, u_n + v_n)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">cv = (cv_1, \\dots, cv_n), \\qquad \\|cv\\| = |c|\\,\\|v\\|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{dist}(u, v) = \\|u - v\\|</span></p>"}, {"l": "span", "t": "Linear combinations, span and independence", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{span}(v_1, \\dots, v_k) = \\{c_1v_1 + \\dots + c_kv_k : c_i \\in \\mathbb{R}\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{independent} \\iff \\left(c_1v_1 + \\dots + c_kv_k = 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{all } c_i = 0\\right)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\dim \\mathbb{R}^n = n</span></p>"}, {"l": "vector-spaces", "t": "Vector spaces and subspaces", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">S \\text{ is a subspace} \\iff 0 \\in S,\\ u + v \\in S,\\ cv \\in S \\ \\ (\\forall u, v \\in S,\\ c \\in \\mathbb{R})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">N(A) = \\{x : Ax = 0\\} \\text{ is always a subspace}</span></p>"}, {"l": "dot-product", "t": "The dot product", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">u \\cdot v = \\sum_i u_iv_i = \\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|\\cos\\theta</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">v \\cdot v = \\|v\\|^2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\cos\\theta = \\frac{u \\cdot v}{\\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{proj}_u v = \\frac{u\\cdot v}{u\\cdot u}\\,u</span></p>"}, {"l": "cross-product", "t": "The cross product", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">u \\times v = (u_2v_3 - u_3v_2,\\ u_3v_1 - u_1v_3,\\ u_1v_2 - u_2v_1)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|u \\times v\\| = \\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|\\sin\\theta = \\text{area of parallelogram}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">v \\times u = -(u \\times v), \\qquad u \\times u = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{volume} = |u \\cdot (v \\times w)|</span></p>"}, {"l": "norms", "t": "Norms and distances", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_1 = \\sum_i |x_i| \\qquad \\|x\\|_2 = \\sqrt{\\textstyle\\sum_i x_i^2} \\qquad \\|x\\|_\\infty = \\max_i |x_i|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_p = \\left(\\textstyle\\sum_i |x_i|^p\\right)^{1/p}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|x\\|_\\infty \\le \\|x\\|_2 \\le \\|x\\|_1 \\le n\\,\\|x\\|_\\infty</span></p>"}, {"l": "matrices", "t": "Matrices and matrix-vector multiplication", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">(Ax)_i = \\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij}x_j = (\\text{row } i)\\cdot x</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Ax = x_1 a_1 + x_2 a_2 + \\dots + x_n a_n \\quad (a_j = \\text{column } j)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(m\\times n)(n\\times1) = (m\\times 1)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A(x + y) = Ax + Ay, \\qquad A(cx) = cAx</span></p>"}, {"l": "transformations", "t": "Matrices as linear transformations", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">T(u + v) = T(u) + T(v), \\qquad T(cv) = cT(v)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = \\big[\\,T(\\hat\\imath)\\ \\ T(\\hat\\jmath)\\,\\big]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">R_\\theta = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos\\theta &amp; -\\sin\\theta\\\\ \\sin\\theta &amp; \\cos\\theta\\end{bmatrix}</span></p>"}, {"l": "matmul", "t": "Matrix multiplication", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">(AB)_{ij} = \\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">AB = [\\,Ab_1 \\ \\cdots\\ Ab_p\\,]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(m\\times n)(n\\times p) = m\\times p</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(AB)C = A(BC), \\quad A(B + C) = AB + AC, \\quad AI = IA = A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(AB)^T = B^TA^T, \\qquad u\\cdot v = u^Tv</span></p>"}, {"l": "special-matrices", "t": "Special matrices", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">D^k = \\operatorname{diag}(d_1^k, \\dots, d_n^k), \\qquad D^{-1} = \\operatorname{diag}(1/d_1, \\dots, 1/d_n)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A \\text{ symmetric} \\iff A = A^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Q^TQ = I \\iff Q^{-1} = Q^T, \\qquad \\|Qx\\| = \\|x\\|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{tr}(A) = \\sum_i A_{ii}, \\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(AB) = \\operatorname{tr}(BA)</span></p>"}, {"l": "elimination", "t": "Solving linear systems with elimination", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Row ops: } R_i \\leftrightarrow R_j, \\quad R_i \\to cR_i\\ (c \\ne 0), \\quad R_i \\to R_i + cR_j</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = LU \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad Ly = b,\\ \\ Ux = y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Cost of elimination on } n\\times n: \\ \\approx \\tfrac23 n^3 \\text{ operations}</span></p>"}, {"l": "inverse", "t": "The inverse matrix", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">AA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d &amp; -b\\\\-c &amp; a\\end{bmatrix}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Ax = b \\Rightarrow x = A^{-1}b</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}, \\quad (A^T)^{-1} = (A^{-1})^T</span></p>"}, {"l": "determinants", "t": "Determinants", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\det\\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix} = ad - bc</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(AB) = \\det A\\det B, \\quad \\det(A^{-1}) = \\frac{1}{\\det A}, \\quad \\det(A^T) = \\det A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(cA) = c^n\\det A \\ (n\\times n), \\qquad \\det(\\text{triangular}) = \\prod_i A_{ii}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A \\text{ invertible} \\iff \\det A \\ne 0</span></p>"}, {"l": "rank", "t": "The four fundamental subspaces and rank", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{rank}(A) = \\dim C(A) = \\dim C(A^T) = \\#\\text{pivots}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{rank}(A) + \\dim N(A) = n</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\dim N(A^T) = m - r</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">N(A) \\perp C(A^T), \\qquad N(A^T) \\perp C(A)</span></p>"}, {"l": "change-of-basis", "t": "Change of basis and similar matrices", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">v = B[v]_\\mathcal{B}, \\qquad [v]_\\mathcal{B} = B^{-1}v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Orthonormal basis: } [v]_\\mathcal{B} = Q^Tv, \\ \\ c_i = q_i\\cdot v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A_\\mathcal{B} = B^{-1}AB</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(B^{-1}AB) = \\det A, \\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(B^{-1}AB) = \\operatorname{tr}A</span></p>"}, {"l": "projections", "t": "Orthogonal projections", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">p = \\frac{u\\cdot v}{u\\cdot u}u, \\qquad e = v - p \\perp u</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P = \\frac{uu^T}{u^Tu}, \\qquad P^2 = P, \\qquad P^T = P</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A^TA\\hat x = A^Tv, \\qquad P = A(A^TA)^{-1}A^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P = QQ^T \\ \\ (\\text{orthonormal columns})</span></p>"}, {"l": "gram-schmidt", "t": "Orthonormal bases and Gram–Schmidt", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">q_1 = \\frac{a_1}{\\|a_1\\|}, \\qquad v_k = a_k - \\sum_{i&lt;k}(q_i\\cdot a_k)q_i, \\qquad q_k = \\frac{v_k}{\\|v_k\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">v = \\sum_i (q_i\\cdot v)\\,q_i \\ \\ (\\text{orthonormal basis})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = QR, \\qquad Q^TQ = I, \\qquad R_{ij} = q_i\\cdot a_j</span></p>"}, {"l": "least-squares", "t": "Least squares and linear regression", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\hat w = \\arg\\min_w \\|y - Xw\\|^2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">X^TX\\hat w = X^Ty, \\qquad \\hat w = (X^TX)^{-1}X^Ty</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Line } y = c + mx: \\ \\begin{bmatrix}n &amp; \\sum x_i\\\\\\sum x_i &amp; \\sum x_i^2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c\\\\m\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sum y_i\\\\\\sum x_iy_i\\end{bmatrix}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Ridge: } (X^TX + \\lambda I)\\hat w = X^Ty</span></p>"}, {"l": "eigen", "t": "Eigenvectors and eigenvalues", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">Av = \\lambda v, \\quad v \\ne 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\det(A - \\lambda I) = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">2\\times2: \\ \\lambda^2 - (\\operatorname{tr}A)\\lambda + \\det A = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum\\lambda_i = \\operatorname{tr}A, \\qquad \\prod\\lambda_i = \\det A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A^kv = \\lambda^kv, \\qquad A^{-1}v = \\lambda^{-1}v</span></p>"}, {"l": "diagonalization", "t": "Diagonalization and matrix powers", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">A = PDP^{-1}, \\qquad D = P^{-1}AP</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A^k = PD^kP^{-1}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Symmetric: } A = Q\\Lambda Q^T, \\ Q^TQ = I</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Markov steady state: } Mv = v</span></p>"}, {"l": "positive-definite", "t": "Quadratic forms and positive definite matrices", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">x^TAx = ax_1^2 + 2bx_1x_2 + cx_2^2 \\ \\ \\text{for } A = \\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\b &amp; c\\end{bmatrix}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A \\succ 0 \\iff \\text{all } \\lambda_i &gt; 0 \\iff (2\\times2)\\ a &gt; 0,\\ ac - b^2 &gt; 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x^TB^TBx = \\|Bx\\|^2 \\ge 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A = LL^T \\ (\\text{Cholesky})</span></p>"}, {"l": "svd", "t": "Singular value decomposition", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">A = U\\Sigma V^T = \\sum_{i=1}^r \\sigma_iu_iv_i^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A^TA = V\\Sigma^T\\Sigma V^T, \\qquad \\sigma_i = \\sqrt{\\lambda_i(A^TA)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Av_i = \\sigma_iu_i, \\qquad \\operatorname{rank}A = \\#\\{\\sigma_i &gt; 0\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\|A\\|_2 = \\sigma_1, \\quad \\|A\\|_F^2 = \\sum\\sigma_i^2, \\quad \\kappa(A) = \\sigma_1/\\sigma_n</span></p>"}, {"l": "pca", "t": "Principal component analysis", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">C = \\frac{1}{n-1}X^TX \\ \\ (X \\text{ centred}), \\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(Xu) = u^TCu</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Cw_i = \\lambda_iw_i, \\qquad \\text{explained ratio} = \\frac{\\lambda_i}{\\sum_j\\lambda_j}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">Z = XW_k, \\qquad \\hat X = ZW_k^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">X = U\\Sigma V^T \\Rightarrow \\lambda_i = \\frac{\\sigma_i^2}{n-1}</span></p>"}, {"l": "tensors", "t": "Tensors and vectorized thinking", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{size} = \\prod_i \\text{shape}_i</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(n, m) \\times (m, p) \\to (n, p), \\qquad (B, n, m) \\times (B, m, p) \\to (B, n, p)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Broadcast: } (3, 1) + (1, 4) \\to (3, 4)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\langle A, B\\rangle = \\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ij} = \\operatorname{tr}(A^TB)</span></p>"}]});
